    "homepage_text": "Xingyu Zhu\nXingyu Zhu 朱星宇\nPhD Student\nPrinceton University\nAbout Me\nI am a third year PhD Candidate in Computer Science at Princeton University working on theoretical machine learning and language modeling. I am fortunate to be advised by Professor\nSanjeev Arora\n. I did my undergrad at Duke, where I was fortunate to be advised by Professor\nRong Ge\n.\nDownload CV\nInterests\nMachine Learning Theory\nLanguage Modeling\nTheoretical Computer Science\nEducation\nPh.D. in Computer Science\nPrinceton University\nB.S. in Math & Computer Science\nDuke University\nResearch Interests\nMy interest spans across both theoretical and empirical ML. Recently I am especially interested in understanding the power of large language models through a semi-theoretical lens. I have also worked on optimization dynamics of deep neural nets. Besides, I am also broadly interested in theoretical computer science and algorithmic fairness.\nRecent News\nContextual Drag accepted as an Oral Presentation (4 out of 100 accepted) in the Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI) Workshop in ICLR 2026\nMar 6, 2026\nCome and chat with us @ Rio!\nRead more\nMar 6, 2026\nContext-Enhanced Learning accepted by ICML 2025 as spotlight!\nMay 1, 2025\nCome and chat with us @ Vancouver!\nRead more\nMay 1, 2025\nNew Paper - On the Power of Context-Enhanced Learning in LLMs\nMar 3, 2025\nCheck out our new paper on context-enhanced learning! When training LLMs, putting helpful information purely in-context can exponentially improve sample efficiency while leading to little verbatim memorization of the in-context materials!\nRead more\nMar 3, 2025\nFeatured Publications\nLatent Reasoning\nT2MLR: Transformer with Temporal Middle-Layer Recurrence\nMar 2, 2026\nComputer Science - Computation and Language\nContextual Drag - How Errors in the Context Affect LLM Reasoning\nFeb 4, 2026\nComputer Science - Machine Learning\nOn the Power of Context-Enhanced Learning in LLMs\nMar 1, 2025\nComputer Science - Machine Learning\nUnderstanding Edge-of-Stability Training Dynamics with a Minimalist Example\nFeb 1, 2023\nRecent Publications\nZiyang Cai*\n,\nXingyu Zhu*\n,\nYihe Dong\n,\nYinghui He\n,\nSanjeev Arora\n(2026).\nT2MLR: Transformer with Temporal Middle-Layer Recurrence\n.\nLIT Workshop @ ICLR 2026.\nCite\nOpenReview\nYun Cheng\n,\nXingyu Zhu\n,\nHaoyu Zhao\n,\nSanjeev Arora\n(2026).\nContextual Drag - How Errors in the Context Affect LLM Reasoning\n.\narXiv\n.\nCite\nDOI\nURL\nXingyu Zhu*\n,\nAbhishek Panigrahi*\n,\nSanjeev Arora\n(2025).\nOn the Power of Context-Enhanced Learning in LLMs\n.\nICML 2025\n(Spotlight)\n.\nCite\nDOI\nURL\nXingyu Zhu*\n,\nZixuan Wang*\n,\nXiang Wang\n,\nMo Zhou\n,\nRong Ge\n(2023).\nUnderstanding Edge-of-Stability Training Dynamics with a Minimalist Example\n.\nICLR 2023.\nCite\nURL\nZeyu Shen\n,\nZhiyi Wang\n,\nXingyu Zhu\n,\nBrandon Fain\n,\nKamesh Munagala\n(2023).\nFairness in the Assignment Problem with Uncertain Priorities\n.\nAAMAS 2023.\nCite\nURL\nSee all publications\nTalks\nOn the Power of Context-Enhanced Learning in LLMs\nPLI Weekly Lunch Seminar\nMar 27, 2025\nUnderstanding Edge-of-Stability\nInvited Talk in ELLIS Reading Group on Mathematics of Deep Learning\nMay 24, 2023"
  },
