    "homepage_text": "Yong  Li, Ph.D. | BCM\nSkip to main content\nHealthcare\nGeneral Inquiries\nCall today to schedule an appointment or fill out an online request form. If requested before 2 p.m. you will receive a response today.\nCall\n713-798-1000\nMonday - Friday 8 a.m. - 5 p.m.\nONLINE\nRequest Now\nRequest non-urgent appointments\nImage\nAs Houston's premier academic medical practice, Baylor Medicine delivers compassionate, innovative, evidence-based care.\nFind a Doctor\nEducation\nAmerica's fourth largest city is a great place to live, work and play. Find out why.\nGet to Know Houston\nResearch\nAmerica's fourth largest city is a great place to live, work and play. Find out why.\nGet to Know Houston\nCommunity\nAmerica's fourth largest city is a great place to live, work and play. Find out why.\nGet to Know Houston\nAbout\nAmerica's fourth largest city is a great place to live, work and play. Find out why.\nGet to Know Houston\nIntranet\nCareers\nGive\nSearch\nToggle navigation\nMenu\nSearch\nGive\nCareers\nIntranet\nBaylor College of Medicine\nBaylor College of Medicine\nPeople Search\nYong  Li, Ph.D.\nYong Li\nYong  Li, Ph.D.\nPicture\nYong  Li, Ph.D.\nProfessor\nPhone\n713-798-7506\nPositions\nProfessor\nMedicine - Epidemiology & Population Science\nBaylor College of Medicine\nMember\nDan L Duncan Comprehensive Cancer Center\nBaylor College of Medicine\nHouston, Texas (TX)\nAddresses\nN720 (Lab)\nAlkek Building\nOne Baylor Plaza\nHouston, TX 77030\nUnited States\nPhone: (713) 798-7506\nEducation\nBS from Lanzhou University\nLanzhou\nPhD from Chinese Academy of Sciences\nShanghai\nPostdoctoral Training at Yale University\nNew Haven\nProfessional Interests\nNon-Hodgkin's Lymphoma & Multiple Myeloma\nEnvironmental Carcinogenesis\nCancer Epidemiology\nArtificial intelligence\nCancer Prevention and Interception\nProfessional Statement\nMy research aims to elucidate the role of genetic and environmental etiological factors in cancer and to define the precise molecular and cellular events and genetic polymorphisms intersecting with toxicants and infectious pathogens responsible for the epidemiological association, cancer initiation and progression, and clinical outcomes. The translational objective of my research is to develop novel small molecules, monoclonal antibodies, and vaccines for cancer prevention and intervention.\nSelected Publications\nYu X, Li W, Deng Q, Liu H, Wang X, Hu H, Cao Y, Xu-Monette ZY, Li L, Zhang M, Lu Z, Young KH, Li Y\n\"\nMYD88 L265P elicits mutation-specific ubiquitination to drive NF-κB activation and lymphomagenesis\n.\"\nBlood.\n2021;137(12):1615-1627.\nPubmed PMID:\n33025009\nWang L, Deng Q, Hu H, Liu M, Gong Z, Zhang S, Xu-Monette ZY, Lu Z, Young KH, Ma X, Li Y\n\"\nGlyphosate induces benign monoclonal gammopathy and promotes multiple myeloma progression in mice\n.\"\nJ Hematol Oncol.\n2019;12(1):70.\nPubmed PMID:\n31277689\nLi Y, Gordon MW, Xu-Monette ZY, Visco C, Tzankov A, Zou D, Qiu L, Montes-Moreno S, Dybkaer K, Orazi A, Zu Y, Bhagat G, Richards KL, Hsi ED, Choi WW, van Krieken JH, Huang Q, Ai W, Ponzoni M, Ferreri AJ, Winter JN, Go RS, Piris MA, et al\n\"\nSingle nucleotide variation in the TP53 3' untranslated region in diffuse large B-cell lymphoma treated with rituximab-CHOP: a report from the International DLBCL Rituximab-CHOP Consortium Program\n.\"\nBlood.\n2013;121(22):4529-40.\nPubmed PMID:\n23515929\nChai D, Wang J, Lim JM, Xie X, Yu X, Zhao D, Maza PAMA, Wang Y, Cyril-Remirez D, Young KH, Li Y.\n\"\nLipid nanoparticles deliver DNA-encoded biologics and induce potent protective immunity\n.\"\nMol Cancer.\n2025;24(1):12.\nPubmed PMID:\n39806486\nChai D, Wang J, Fan C, Lim JM, Wang X, Neeli P, Yu X, Young KH, Li Y.\n\"\nRemodeling of anti-tumor immunity with antibodies targeting a p53 mutant.\n.\"\nJ Hematol Oncol.\n2024;17(1):45.\nPubmed PMID:\n38886748\ni W, Wang R, Wang J, Chai D, Xie X, Young KHH, Cao Y, Li Y, Yu X.\n\"\nLasalocid A selectively induces the degradation of MYD88 in lymphomas harboring the MYD88 L265P mutation\n.\"\nBlood.\n2024;22:blood.2024026781.\nPubmed PMID:\n39576960\nYu X, Li W, Liu H, Wang X, Coarfa C, Cheng C, Yu X, Zeng Z, Cao Y, Young KH, Li Y.\n\"\nPD-L1 translocation to the plasma membrane enables tumor immune evasion through MIB2 ubiquitination.\n.\"\nJ Clin Invest.\n2023;41(1):210.\nPubmed PMID:\n36719382\nHide 3 publications\nShow 3 more selected publications\nMemberships\nAmerican Association for Cancer Research\nAmerican Society of Hematology\nAmerican Association for the Advancement of Science\nSociety of Toxicology\nLog In\nto edit your profile\nBack to top"
  },
