  "deep_homepage::Jun Yu::https://sites.google.com/view/yu-jun/%E9%A6%96%E9%A1%B5": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/view/yu-jun/%E9%A6%96%E9%A1%B5",
    "all_emails": [
      "yujun@hit.edu.cn"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Jun Yu's Homepage\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nJun Yu's Homepage\nJun Yu\nProfessor,\nDean\nSchool of Intelligent Science and Engineering\nHarbin Institute of Technology\n, Shenzhen, China\nContact Info: yujun@hit.edu.cn\nResearch Interests:\nMM\n: Multimedia Retrieval, Question Answering, Multimedia Applications (Medical), etc.\nCV\n: Embodied Intelligence, Image Segmentation, Image Generation, etc.\nDM\n: Recommender System, etc.\nBiography:\nDr. Jun Yu is currently the Dean of School of Intelligent Science and Engineering, Harbin Institute of Technology (Shenzhen). His current research interests include multimedia, language and vision and social media. He has authored over 100 journal and conference papers in these areas and the Google Scholar citations for those papers is more than 21000. He served as editor of the IEEE TCSVT, IEEE TMM, Pattern Recognition and the guest editors of several international journals. He also served as technical program committee members and reviewers of  over 20 prestigious international conferences, and a reviewer of over 20 prestigious international journals. He has received the best paper awards as the first author from journals of  IEEE TMM, TIP, and TCYB. Dr. Yu joined CCF, CSIG, IEEE and ACM.\nRecent Publications:\nGoogle scholar page for Impact.\nDBLP for updated publication list.\n1. Yiqian Zhang, Buyu Liu, Jun Bao, Qiang Huang, Min Zhang, Jun Yu*, Learnability Matters: Active Learning for Video Captioning, Neural Information Processing Systems (NIPS), 2025.\n2. Buyu Liu, Kai Wang, Yansong Liu, Jun Bao, Tingting Han, Jun Yu*, MVPbev: Multi-view Perspective Image Generation from BEV with Test-time Controllability and Generalizability, ACM Multimedia, 2024.\n3. Ke Zhang, Yan Yang, Jun Yu*, Jianping Fan, Hanliang Jiang, Qingming Huang, and Weidong Han, Attribute Prototype-Guided Iterative Scene Graph for Explainable Radiology Report Generation, IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging, 2024.\n4. Yan Yang, Jun Yu*, Zhenqi Fu, Ke Zhang, Ting Yu, Xianyun Wang, Hanliang Jiang, Junhui Lv, Qingming Huang, and Weidong Han, Token-Mixer: Bind Image and Text in One Embedding Space for Medical Image Reporting, IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging, 2024.\n5. Jun Bao, Buyu Liu, Kui Ren, Jun Yu*, GLOW: Global Layout Aware Attacks on Object Detection, CVPR, 2024.\n6. Zhou Yu, Lixiang Zheng, Zhou Zhao, Fei Wu, Jianping Fan, Kui Ren, Jun Yu*, ANetQA: A Large-scale Benchmark for Fine-grained Compositional Reasoning over Untrimmed Videos, CVPR, 2023.\nProfesional Activities:\nEditor:\nIEEE Transactions on Circuits System and Video Technology (TCSVT), from 2021~2023\nIEEE Transactions on Multimedia (TMM), from 2025~\nInformation Sciences, Elsevier from 2016 ~\nPattern Recognition,  Elsevier from 2019 ~\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Mingming Gong::https://mingming-gong.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://mingming-gong.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "mingming.gong@unimelb.edu.au"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Mingming Gong\nMingming Gong\nHome\nNews\nGroup\nResearch\nPublications\nTeaching\nTalks\nService\nAwards & Honors\nHome\nMingming Gong\nAssociate Professor in Data Science\nSchool of Mathematics and Statistics\nMelbourne Centre for Data Science\nThe University of Melbourne\nAssociate Professor in Machine Learning (Affiliated)\nDepartment of Machine Learning\nMohamed bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence\n, United Arab Emirates\nOffice: 112 Old Geology Building South, Parkville, VIC, Australia\nE-mail: mingming.gong[at]unimelb.edu.au\n[Google Scholar]\n[UoM Homepage]\n[MBZUAI Homepage]\nI am an associate professor in Data Science at the School of Mathematics and Statistics and\nMelbourne Centre for Data Science\n, the University of Melbourne (UoM), and an affiliated associate professor of Machine Learning at\nMohamed Bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence (MBZUAI)\n. I lead the\nMachine Learning and Reasoning (MLR)\ngroup. Before joining UoM, I was a postdoctoral research fellow at University of Pittsburgh and Carnegie Mellon University, working with\nProf Kayhan Batmanghelich\nand\nProf Kun Zhang\n. \n        I obtained my PhD from\nUniversity of Technology Sydney\n, supervised by\nProf Dacheng Tao\nand co-supervised by\nProf Kun Zhang\n,  Master degree from\nHuazhong University of Science and Technology\n, and bachelor's degree from\nNanjing University\n.\n        From 03/2013 - 10/2013, I was a research intern at\nMax-Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems (\nProf Bernhard Sch&oumllkopf\n's lab).\nI am always looking for highly-motivated students with strong mathematical background and/or proficient coding skills. If you are interested in working with me, please send me an email about your interests and background (attaching your CV, transcripts, and any previous research papers). Thanks!\nTo potential PhD/MPhil students. I accept PhD/MPhil students at both UoM and MBZUAI. Feel free to contact me if you plan to do research on machine learning for your PhD's thesis. Full scholarships—including government, university, and industry-funded opportunities—are available for exceptional PhD applicants. Please note that self-funded PhD students will not be considered.\nTo UoM Master by Coursework students. Please contact me if you plan to do research on machine learning for your Master's thesis. I supervise students from data science, statistics, and computer science. I prefer to mentor students who are genuinely committed to pursuing a research career.\nTo undergraduate students. Due to my current heavy supervisory commitments, I am unable to take on undergraduate students at this time.\nTo visiting scholar/PhD students. Please drop me an email if your research area overlaps with mine.\nResearch Interests\nI am dedicated to providing theoretical foundations and computational innovations in machine learning and reasoning. My current research focuses on two levels of generative models and their applications.\nThe first level involves data-driven generative models that learn from observational data to efficiently generate new samples from the same distribution. These models capture surface-level patterns for tasks such as\nrealistic data generation or augmentation\n.\nThe second level explores causal and structural generative models, which go beyond replicating data to uncover the underlying causal or physical mechanisms that govern the data distribution. These models serve as world models that enable\nreasoning, intervention, and simulation\nby allowing controlled manipulation of variables and observation of their effects. This capability is essential for building intelligent agents that can understand, predict, and interact with complex environments.\nOn the application side, my current interests include\nImage generation\n3D reconstruction/generation\nMultimodal LLMs\nLLM reasoning\nEmbodied AI\nRecent Updates\n12/2025, I was promoted to Associate Professor at UoM. Grafeful to everyone who has supported me!\n07/2025, we have two industry-funded PhD positions available at UoM, focusing on efficient/3D generative models. If you have a strong background in a related area and are interested in this opportunity, please feel free to contact me. (positions filled)\n05/2025, we are hiring three postdocs at MBZUAI for a collaborative project with Etihad. More information can be found\nhere\n. (positions filled)\n03/2025, we are organising the\nAAAI’25 Workshop on Artificial Intelligence with Causal Techniques\n.\n03/2025, we are organising the\nMBZUAI-Berkeley Workshop on Emerging Directions of Machine Learning\n.\n12/2024, we are organising a workshop on\nCausal Representation Learning\nat ICDM 2024.\n12/2024, we are presenting a tutorial on\nCausality and Large Models\nat ACML 2024 and ICDM 2024.\n09/2024, congrats to Yuanyuan and Dongting for their papers accepted to NeurIPS 2024. Additionally, two collaborative papers got accepted, congrats to the team!\n07/2024, congrats to Jinghe for his paper accepted to ECCV 2024.\n06/2024, we are organising the\nMBZUAI Workshop on Machine Learning for Large Models\n.\n05/2024, one paper on treatment effect inference was accepted to KDD 2024.\n05/2024, congrats to Wenjie and Shunxin for their papers accepted to ICML 2024.\n05/2024, congrats to Archer for his paper accepted to IEEE-TNNLS.\n02/2024, congrats to Maggie for her paper accepted to CVPR 2024.\n01/2024, congrats to Erdun and Aoqi for their papers accepted to ICLR 2024. Additionally, two collaborative papers got accepted, congrats to the team!\n01/2024, congrats to Wenqin for her first paper accepted to CLeaR 2024.\n01/2024, I am honored to announce my role as the Program Co-Chair for the Australasian Conference on Artificial Intelligence 2024. I extend a warm invitation for you to attend the conference, scheduled to take place in Melbourne this November.\n11/2023, we are honored to receive an ARC discovery project to work on federated causal inference.\n09/2023, congratulations to Yuanyuan on her first NeurIPS paper! Additionally, four collaborative papers got accepted, congrats to the team!\n07/2023, congrats to Dongting and Ziye for their papers accepted to ICCV 2023.\n10/2022, congrats to Erdun for his paper on federated DAG learning accepted to TMLR.\n08/2022, I will serve as Area Chair for ICML 2023 and UAI 2023 and senior PC for IJCAI 2023 and ECAI 2023.\n12/2022, I am hornored to receive the Australasian Artificial Intelligence Emerging Research Contribution Award.\n10/2022, congrats to Haopeng and Maggie for their papers accepted to WACV 2023.\n09/2022, congrats to Aoqi and Erdun for their papers accepted to NeurIPS 2022. Our collaborative work with the PGM group at University of Adelaide on efficient differentiable DAG learning also got accepted.\n08/2022, I was promoted to Senior Lecturer (grateful to my students and collaborators).\n08/2022, I will serve as an Area Chair for ICLR 2023.\n08/2022, I was selected to join the editorial board of\nMachine Learning Journal\n.\n07/2022, I was awarded the CCF-Tencent Rhino-Bird Young Faculty Open Research Fund. Thank you Tencent and CCF.\n07/2022, congrats to Dongting for his first paper accepted to ECCV 2022. Our collaborative work with Alibaba on Adaptive Sparse Radiance Grid also got accepted.\n07/2022, Prof\nNengkun Yu\ngave us a wonderful talk on quantum computing and learning.\n06/2022, congrats to Haopeng for his paper accepted to IEEE T-PAMI.\n04/2022, I was selected as one of Global Top Young Chinese Scholars in AI by Baidu Scholar 2022.\n03/2022, I will serve as an Area Chair for NeurIPS 2022.\n03/2022, our collaborative work with Ebay on recommender systems was accepted to\nSIGIR 2022.\n03/2022, five papers (2 on image-to-image translation, 2 on self-supervised learning, and 1 collaborative work with Baidu on few-shot font style transfer) were accepted to\nCVPR 2022.\n01/2022, our paper on statistical/causal fair learning under label noise were accepted to\nCLeaR 2022.\n01/2022, four papers (1 on causal robustness, 1 on causal RL, 2 on weakly-supervised learning)  were accepted to\nICLR 2022.\n12/2021, I will serve as an Area Chair for ICML 2022.\n11/2021, Our Python package for causal discovery\ncausal-learn\nwas released.\n11/2021, I will serve as an Area Chair for UAI 2022.\n10/2021, our papers on causal treatment of domain adaptation and label noise learning were accepted to\nNeurIPS 2021.\n07/2021, I will be the Area Chair and Publicity Chair at the\n1st conference on Causal Learning and Reasoning (CLeaR).\nSee more in\nNews\n."
  },
  "deep_homepage::Tongliang Liu::https://tongliang-liu.github.io/": {
