    "homepage_text": "Ye Zhu Research Group\nSkip to content\nYe Zhu Research Group\nHome\nWELCOME TO YE’S LABORATORY\nOrganic chemistry | Asymmetric catalysis | Molecular interactions\nResearch\nChemical synthesis of biologically relevant molecules remains the rate-limiting step in drug discovery and development despite the tremendous progress that has been made in academia and industry. Our research focuses on inventing and developing enabling chemical tools to address significant synthetic challenges facing biomedical sciences and pharmaceutical industry, thus accelerating creation of potential new medicines. To achieve this goal, our effort is directed towards:\nInnovating chemo-, regio-, and stereo-selective catalytic reactions that are compatible with polar groups and heterocycles;\nDesigning and developing novel strategies for late-stage functionalization of synthetic scaffolds and natural products;\nDiscovering molecular recognitions between biomolecules and artificial receptors in\nthree-dimensional chemical space\n.\nStereoselectivity\nJ. Am. Chem. Soc.\n2022,\n144\n, 123–129 [\nopen access\n];\nChem Catalysis\n2023,\n3\n, 100523 [\nopen access\n];\nJ. Am. Chem. Soc.\n2023,\n145\n, 16796–16811[\nopen access\n].\nCooperativity\nChem. Commun.\n2022,\n58\n, 4719–4722 [\nopen access\n];\nTetrahedron Lett.\n2023,\n119,\n154408. [\nopen access\n];\nNature\n2023, 622, 754–760 [\nlink\n].\nMolecular Interactions\nChem\n2022, 8, 2843–2855 [\nopen archive\n];\nChem\n2024,\n10\n, 3323–3341 [\nopen access\n];\nAngew. Chem. Int. Ed.\n2025, e202417827[\nlink\n].\nJoin Us\nWe now have 2 open positions for Research Fellows.\nPlease contact Ye\n.\nWe welcome undergraduate and M.Sc./Ph.D. students.\nApply to NUS Chemistry\n.\nGet In Touch\nchmzhu@nus.edu.sg\n(65) 6601-7501\nDrop By\nNUS S9 Wet Science Building, Level 14\n4 Science Drive 2, Singapore 117544\nThe views and opinions expressed herein are those of the author(s) and do not represent the views and opinions of the National University of Singapore or any of its subsidiaries or affiliates\n.\nYe Zhu Research Group\n(NUS blog).\nYe Zhu Research Group\nSign up\nLog in\nCopy shortlink\nReport this content\nManage subscriptions\nLoading Comments...\nWrite a Comment...\nEmail (Required)\nName (Required)\nWebsite\n\n---\n\nAbout Ye – Ye Zhu Research Group\nSkip to content\nYe Zhu Research Group\nAbout Ye\nYe received his B.Sc. degree from Peking University in 2006, and Ph.D. from The University of Chicago in 2012. His thesis work, with Professor Viresh Rawal, focused on squaramide-catalyzed phospha-Michael addition and palladium-catalyzed indole functionalization reactions. He then moved to the Massachusetts Institute of Technology to pursue postdoctoral studies with Professor Stephen Buchwald where he worked in the area of ligand-controlled asymmetric arylation of aliphatic\na\n-amino anion equivalents.\nIn 2014, inspired to transform patients’ lives through science, Ye joined the Bristol-Myers Squibb, working on process development, route scouting, and the design and implementation of safe, efficient and robust chemical processes for the synthesis of drug candidates. He was a member of the team that developed P(V) reagents for oligonucleotide synthesis, which was awarded Horizon Prize—Robert Robinson Award in Synthetic Organic Chemistry by the Royal Society of Chemistry.\nIn 2018, passionate about developing efficient and practical methods for application in the pharmaceutical industries (and others), Ye started as an assistant professor in the National University of Singapore.\nOriginally from China, Ye studied chemistry in Beijing and Chicago, Illinois, worked in New Brunswick, New Jersey, and is now\nteaching\nin Singapore.\nexperience\nSenior Research Investigator, 2018, Bristol-Myers Squibb\nPostdoctoral Fellow, 2014, MIT\nEDUCATION\nPh.D., 2012, The University of Chicago\nB.Sc., 2006, Peking University\nAWARDS\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Awards from Taiwan and Hong Kong (2024)\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Awards from Japan and South Korea (2023)\nHorizon Prize (team member) – Robert Robinson Award in Synthetic Organic Chemistry (2022)\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Award from Mainland China (2022)\nCurriculum Vitae\n[\npdf\n]\nGet in touch\nZhu, Ye (朱烨)\nNational University of Singapore\nDepartment of Chemistry\nOffice: S9-14-01C\nEmail: chmzhu@nus.edu.sg\nPhone: (65) 6601-7501\nFAX: (65) 6779-1691\nLinkedIn\nYe Zhu Research Group\nSign up\nLog in\nCopy shortlink\nReport this content\nManage subscriptions\nLoading Comments...\nWrite a Comment...\nEmail (Required)\nName (Required)\nWebsite\n\n---\n\nAbout Ye – Ye Zhu Research Group\nSkip to content\nYe Zhu Research Group\nAbout Ye\nYe received his B.Sc. degree from Peking University in 2006, and Ph.D. from The University of Chicago in 2012. His thesis work, with Professor Viresh Rawal, focused on squaramide-catalyzed phospha-Michael addition and palladium-catalyzed indole functionalization reactions. He then moved to the Massachusetts Institute of Technology to pursue postdoctoral studies with Professor Stephen Buchwald where he worked in the area of ligand-controlled asymmetric arylation of aliphatic\na\n-amino anion equivalents.\nIn 2014, inspired to transform patients’ lives through science, Ye joined the Bristol-Myers Squibb, working on process development, route scouting, and the design and implementation of safe, efficient and robust chemical processes for the synthesis of drug candidates. He was a member of the team that developed P(V) reagents for oligonucleotide synthesis, which was awarded Horizon Prize—Robert Robinson Award in Synthetic Organic Chemistry by the Royal Society of Chemistry.\nIn 2018, passionate about developing efficient and practical methods for application in the pharmaceutical industries (and others), Ye started as an assistant professor in the National University of Singapore.\nOriginally from China, Ye studied chemistry in Beijing and Chicago, Illinois, worked in New Brunswick, New Jersey, and is now\nteaching\nin Singapore.\nexperience\nSenior Research Investigator, 2018, Bristol-Myers Squibb\nPostdoctoral Fellow, 2014, MIT\nEDUCATION\nPh.D., 2012, The University of Chicago\nB.Sc., 2006, Peking University\nAWARDS\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Awards from Taiwan and Hong Kong (2024)\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Awards from Japan and South Korea (2023)\nHorizon Prize (team member) – Robert Robinson Award in Synthetic Organic Chemistry (2022)\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Award from Mainland China (2022)\nCurriculum Vitae\n[\npdf\n]\nGet in touch\nZhu, Ye (朱烨)\nNational University of Singapore\nDepartment of Chemistry\nOffice: S9-14-01C\nEmail: chmzhu@nus.edu.sg\nPhone: (65) 6601-7501\nFAX: (65) 6779-1691\nLinkedIn\nYe Zhu Research Group\nSign up\nLog in\nCopy shortlink\nReport this content\nManage subscriptions\nLoading Comments...\nWrite a Comment...\nEmail (Required)\nName (Required)\nWebsite\n\n---\n\nAbout Ye – Ye Zhu Research Group\nSkip to content\nYe Zhu Research Group\nAbout Ye\nYe received his B.Sc. degree from Peking University in 2006, and Ph.D. from The University of Chicago in 2012. His thesis work, with Professor Viresh Rawal, focused on squaramide-catalyzed phospha-Michael addition and palladium-catalyzed indole functionalization reactions. He then moved to the Massachusetts Institute of Technology to pursue postdoctoral studies with Professor Stephen Buchwald where he worked in the area of ligand-controlled asymmetric arylation of aliphatic\na\n-amino anion equivalents.\nIn 2014, inspired to transform patients’ lives through science, Ye joined the Bristol-Myers Squibb, working on process development, route scouting, and the design and implementation of safe, efficient and robust chemical processes for the synthesis of drug candidates. He was a member of the team that developed P(V) reagents for oligonucleotide synthesis, which was awarded Horizon Prize—Robert Robinson Award in Synthetic Organic Chemistry by the Royal Society of Chemistry.\nIn 2018, passionate about developing efficient and practical methods for application in the pharmaceutical industries (and others), Ye started as an assistant professor in the National University of Singapore.\nOriginally from China, Ye studied chemistry in Beijing and Chicago, Illinois, worked in New Brunswick, New Jersey, and is now\nteaching\nin Singapore.\nexperience\nSenior Research Investigator, 2018, Bristol-Myers Squibb\nPostdoctoral Fellow, 2014, MIT\nEDUCATION\nPh.D., 2012, The University of Chicago\nB.Sc., 2006, Peking University\nAWARDS\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Awards from Taiwan and Hong Kong (2024)\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Awards from Japan and South Korea (2023)\nHorizon Prize (team member) – Robert Robinson Award in Synthetic Organic Chemistry (2022)\nAsian Core Program Lectureship Award from Mainland China (2022)\nCurriculum Vitae\n[\npdf\n]\nGet in touch\nZhu, Ye (朱烨)\nNational University of Singapore\nDepartment of Chemistry\nOffice: S9-14-01C\nEmail: chmzhu@nus.edu.sg\nPhone: (65) 6601-7501\nFAX: (65) 6779-1691\nLinkedIn\nYe Zhu Research Group\nSign up\nLog in\nCopy shortlink\nReport this content\nManage subscriptions\nLoading Comments...\nWrite a Comment...\nEmail (Required)\nName (Required)\nWebsite"
