    "homepage_text": "Hui Li's Homepage\nEditorial\nby HTML5 UP\nTwitter\nFacebook\nSnapchat\nInstagram\nMedium\nHui Li (李辉)\nTheoretical and Computational Astrophysicist at Tsinghua University\nI am an associate professor at the\nDepartment of Astronomy, Tsinghua University\n. Currently, I'm leading the\nTANGO\n(\nT\nheoretical\nA\nnd\nN\numerical\nG\nalaxy f\nO\nrmation)\ngroup. I was Hubble fellow at Columbia University, collaborating with Prof. Greg Bryan. Before joining Columbia, I was a postdoc at the MIT Kavli Institute for Astrophysics and Space Research working with Prof. Mark Vogelsberger. My current research interests include galaxy formation, evolution of giant molecular clouds and formation of most massive star clusters, origin of globular clusters in different types of galaxies, stellar dynamics of globular clusters, cosmic ray acceleration and propagation around supernova remnants and pulsars, astrophysical origins of γ-rays in the Galaxy, etc. See my\nresearch\nin details.\nI received my Ph.D. degree in the\nAstronomy Department\n, University of Michigan in Dec. 2017, supervised by\nProf. Oleg Y. Gnedin\n. Before coming to the US, I got my Master and Bachelor's degree from Nanjing University, which provides the most prestigious astronomical program in China.\nCurriculum Vitae\nResearch Milestones\n77 Refereed Publications\n25 first/corresponding author journal papers, see\nPublication\nfor details.\nOver 2100 citations with h-index=26\nfirst-author paper citation>1300\n8 Successful Proposals for High-Performance Computing\nin total about 100 million CPU hours from various supercomputer centers, equivelent to ~ 1.5 million dollar\nMore than 100 Conference Presentations and Seminars\nmore than 40 Invited Talks, 20 Invited Colloquiums/Seminars\nGet in touch\nIf you are interested in any of my projects and would like to collaborate, do not hesitate to contact me!\nhliastro@tsinghua.edu.cn\nlihui870222@gmail.com\nNews\nSep. 2020: I move to Columbia University as a Hubble fellow.\nApr. 2020: I receive the IOP Outstanding Reviewer Awards 2019.\nMar 2020: Our paper \"NuSTAR and Chandra Observation of the Galactic Center Non-thermal X-ray Filament G0.13-0.11: A Pulsar Wind Nebula Driven Magnetic Filament\" led by Dr. Shuo Zhang has been accepted for publication at ApJ.\nAll summer travels, including Ringberga and KITP workshop, are canceled due to coronavirus spread across the globe.\nMar. 2020: I give a Astro seminar at UC Riverside.\nJan. 2020: My paper \"The effects of subgrid models on the properties of giant molecular clouds in galaxy formation simulations\" is submitted in MNRAS.\nDec. 1st, 2019: My Hubble fellowship starts at MIT.\nMore"
  },
