      "airoffice@air.tsinghua.edu.cn"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/wei-ying-ma-16a0171/",
    "homepage_text": "ï»¿\nWei-Ying Ma-AIR\nChinese\nAbout Us\nAbout AIR\nAbout Dean\nFounding Dean\nCommittee\nNews Center\nNews\nDialogue with Masters\nAcademic Activities\nMedia Reports\nOur Team\nResearch Team\nIndustry Partnership Team\nAdministrative Team\nPost-Doctorals\nDoctor of Engineering\nDoctorals\nResearch\nAI+IoT\nAI+Transportation\nAI+Healthcare\nBig Data Intelligence ï¼BDIï¼Lab\nDISCOVER Laboratory\nPartnerships\nCareer\nFaculty\nPost-Doctorals\nInterns\nOpen Menu\nAbout Us\nGo Back\nAbout AIR\nAbout Dean\nFounding Dean\nCommittee\nNews Center\nGo Back\nNews\nDialogue with Masters\nAcademic Activities\nMedia Reports\nOur Team\nGo Back\nResearch Team\nIndustry Partnership Team\nAdministrative Team\nPost-Doctorals\nDoctor of Engineering\nDoctorals\nResearch\nGo Back\nAI+IoT\nAI+Transportation\nAI+Healthcare\nBig Data Intelligence ï¼BDIï¼Lab\nDISCOVER Laboratory\nPartnerships\nCareer\nGo Back\nFaculty\nPost-Doctorals\nInterns\nOur Team\nResearch Team\nIndustry Partnership Team\nAdministrative Team\nPost-Doctorals\nDoctor of Engineering\nDoctorals\nlocation:\nHome\n>\nOur Team\n>\nResearch Team\n>\nProfessor\n>\r\n                Content\nWei-Ying Ma\nChief Scientist/Huiyan Chair Professor\nWei-Ying Ma is a Huiyan Chair Professor at Tsinghua University and the Chief Scientist at Institute for AI Industry Research (AIR), which he co-founded with Dr. Ya-Qin Zhang in 2020. Currently, he is leading a team of faculties to work on AI for Science, with a particular focus on AI-powered drug discovery and generative AI for biology and chemistry. In addition, he has co-founded Helixon, a startup aimed at developing next-generation AI for antibody design and protein therapeutics discovery. From 2021-2023, he served as the Director of Health Computing Research Center with Beijing Academy of Artificial Intelligence (BAAI).\nWei-Ying Ma is a Fellow of the ACM and a Fellow of the IEEE. He has published more than 300 papers and has been granted with 169 patents. In the past, he has served as a program co-chair of the International Conference on World Wide Web (WWW) 2008 and the general co-chair of ACM Special Interest Group on Information Retrieval (SIGIR) 2011. He has also served on many editorial boards, including ACM Transactions on Information Systems (TOIS) from 2005-2012, the ACM/Springer Multimedia Systems Journal from 2004-2012, and the Journal of Multimedia Tools and Applications from 1998-2004. Additionally, Wei-Ying Ma was a member of International World Wide Web Conferences Steering Committee from 2010-2016, responsible for the publicity and organization of the conference in different countries and locations.\nPrior to joining Tsinghua University, Wei-Ying Ma was a Vice President and the Head of AI Lab at ByteDance. He founded ByteDance AI Lab in 2017 and was responsible for research and technology development in areas including machine learning, computer vision, natural language processing, speech and audio processing, recommender system and search. He led ByteDance AI Lab to develop various AI-powered content creation and content dissemination technologies and systems which have been integrated into ByteDance's products including Douyin, TikTok, Jinri Toutiao, CapCut and Lark. Wei-Ying Ma and his teams made an important contribution to the phenomenal success of Douyin and TikTok, which have revolutionized how people create and share content from their mobile phones through AI technology. As of 2023, TikTok has reached over 1.1 billion monthly active users globally and Douyin has over 700 million daily active users in China. These products have reshaped the landscape of the Internet industry.\nDuring his time at ByteDance, Wei-Ying Ma recruited over 300 researchers and built labs in Beijing, Shanghai, Shenzhen, Mountain View, Seattle, London, and Singapore. His notable works include industry-leading machine learning models capable of running on mobile devices to support on-device content analysis, enabling many automatic video and audio/music editing features. He also developed large-scale video analysis systems that run distributed machine learning models in datacenters, capable of processing an extremely large volume of short-form videos created by TikTok and Douyin users. Additionally, Wei-Ying Ma created music generation and recommendation based on video content, speech recognition and text-to-speech synthesis techniques, and text and image generation techniques that facilitate content creation and advertisement design for ByteDance products. For his work at ByteDance, Wei-Ying Ma received the Wu Wenjun Artificial Intelligence Science and Technology Award, a prestigious award in China.\nBefore joining ByteDance, Wei-Ying Ma worked at Microsoft Research Asia (MSRA) from 2001 to 2017. During his time at MSRA, he was an Assistant Managing Director from 2009-2017 and managed five research groups including Web Search and Data Mining, Natural Language Computing, Machine Learning, Social Computing, and Urban Computing. From 2005-2009, he led his teams to consistently contribute 5-10% of full papers on web-search related topics at the ACM Special Interest Group on Information Retrieval (SIGIR) conference, the International World Wide Web (WWW) conference, and the ACM Multimedia conference. These research contributions made his team one of the global powerhouses and trendsetters in these fields.\nAt Microsoft, Wei-Ying Ma led his team to develop many technologies that were successfully transferred to Microsoftâs Bing, Ads Center, Cognitive Services, Exchange, SharePoint, Azure, as well as Microsoftâs conversational agents including Xiaoice (in China) and Cortana. He also contributed to the establishment of Microsoftâs Search Technology Center (STC) in China. In 2009, he was awarded the Management Excellence Trailblazer Award, a recognition given to individuals who have gone above and beyond their duty to advance Management Excellence at Microsoft. Furthermore, he and his team developed the first technique to analyze web pages using visual cues and use the information to model the web and extract structured data from web pages. He also led the initial development of Microsoft Academic Search, an object-level search engine that provides automatic summaries of entities and allows users to navigate and explore their relationships. These techniques have been widely adopted by commercial search engines and academic web services in recent years.\nAmong his other important works at Microsoft, Wei-Ying Ma developed the Microsoft Graph Engine, a powerful graph processing engine designed to serve a large knowledge graph for web search and natural language understanding. He also played a key role in creating the Microsoft Concept Graph, which contains a big graph of concepts and facts/instances extracted from billions of web pages and years' worth of Bing search logs. Additionally, Wei-Ying Ma led the development of the Distributed Machine Learning Toolkit, making machine learning tasks involving big data highly efficient and scalable. He received many Ship-It Awards at Microsoft and was granted over 160 patents that contributed to core intellectual properties of Microsoft's Bing and many other products.\nFrom 1997-2001, Wei-Ying Ma worked in HP Labs at Hewlett-Packard Company on multimedia analysis and adaptive content delivery systems to transcode videos at edge servers according to the capability of the user's device.\nWei-Ying Ma received a Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering from the University of California at Santa Barbara in 1997. He was supervised by Prof. B. S. Manjunath, and his dissertation is titled \"Netra: A Toolbox for Navigating Large Image Databases.\" He performed works in the Alexandria Digital Library Project funded by National Science Foundation from 1994-1997 and developed one of the first content-based image retrieval systems on the Web (called Netra) that has been viewed as one of the most influential image retrieval systems. He also developed widely used Gabor texture features for image retrieval, and one of the first practical image segmentation solutions for processing a large number and variety of natural scene images, which enables image retrieval systems to provide region-based search capability.\nWei-Ying Ma received a B.S. in Electrical Engineering at National Tsing Hua University in Taiwan in 1990 and an M.S. in Electrical and Computer Engineering from the University of California at Santa Barbara in 1994.\nHe was awarded Distinguished Alumni from the school of EECS at National Tsing Hua University in 2010. Wei-Ying Ma received the honor to deliver a Distinguished Lecture titled \"the Next Frontier for Web Search and Knowledge Mining: My Journey since Alexandria Digital Library Project at UCSB\" at UCSB for ECE Dept. 50th Anniversary in 2013.\nYou can visit him on\nLinkedIn\nor find his publications at\nGoogle Scholar\n.\nRecent Papers:\nTan H, Gao B, Li J, Jia Y, Zhu W, Xie W, Liu Y, Huang Y, Wang J, Mo Y, Zhang YQ, Wei-Ying Ma, Lan Y. Drugging the Undruggable: Benchmarking and Modeling Fragment-Based Screening, ICLR 2026.\nYu H, Chen T, Feng J, Chen J, Dai W, Yu Q, Zhang YQ, Wei-Ying Ma, Liu J, Wang M, Zhou H. MemAgent: Reshaping Long-Context LLM with Multi-Conv RL-based Memory Agent, ICLR 2026.\nZhang Z, Feng Y, Song Y, Qiu K, Zhou H, Wei-Ying Ma. DCFold: Efficient Protein Structure Generation with Single Forward Pass, ICLR 2026.\nWang B, Ouyang Y, Li Y, Pan M, Tang Y, Cui H, Wang Y, Zhang J, Wang X, Wei-Ying Ma, Zhou H. MoMa: A Simple Modular Learning Framework for Material Property Prediction, ICLR 2026.\nFeng S, Ni Y, Lu Y, Ma Z,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Lan Y.\nUniGEM:\nA Unified Approach to Generation and Property Prediction for Molecules\n,\nICLR 2025.\nWu H, Song Y, Gong J, Cao Z, Ouyang Y, Zhang J, Zhou H,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Liu J.\nA Periodic Bayesian Flow for Material Generation\n, ICLR 2025.\nGong J, Pei Y, Long S, Song Y, Zhang Z, Huang W, Cao Z, Zhang S, Zhou H,\nWei-Ying Ma\n.\nSteering Protein Family Design through Profile Bayesian Flow\n,\nICLR 2025.\nHuang Y, Gao B, Jia Y, Ma H,\nWei-Ying Ma,\nZhang Y, Lan Y.\nRedefining the Task of Bioactivity Prediction\n, ICLR 2025.\nGao B, Tan H, Huang Y, Ren M, Huang X,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Zhang Y, Lan Y.\nReframing Structure-Based Drug Design Model Evaluation via Metrics Correlated to Practical Needs\n, ICLR 2025.\nY. Song, Z. Zhang, Y. Pei, J. Gong, M. Wang, H. Zhou, J. Liu,\nWei-Ying Ma\n,\nShortListing Model: A Streamlined Simplex Diffusion for Biological Sequence Generation\n,\nICLR 2025 Workshop on Machine Learning for Genomics Explorations.\nHu J, Hu, S, Song Y, Huang Y, Wang M, Zhou H, Liu Z,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Sun M.\nACDiT: Interpolating Autoregressive Conditional Modeling and Diffusion Transformer\n,\nICLR 2025 Workshop on World Models.\nXiangzhe Kong, R. Jiao, H. Lin, R. Guo, W. Huang,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Z. Wang, Y. Liu, Jianzhu Ma,\nPeptide Design through Binding Interface Mimicry\n, bioRxiv 2025.\nWang Y, Song Y, Xu M, Wang R, Zhou H,\nWei-Ying Ma\n.\nRetroDiff: Retrosynthesis as Multi-stage Distribution Interpolation\n, AISTATS 2025.\nWang B, Ouyang Y, Li Y, Wang Y, Cui H, Zhang J, Wang X,\nWei-Ying Ma,\nH. Zhou.\nMoMa: A Modular Deep Learning Framework for Material Property Predictio\nn\n, AI4MAT 2025.\nQiu K, Song Y, Fan Z, Liu P, Zhang Z, Zheng M, Zhao H,\nWei-Ying Ma\n. Piloting Structure-Based Drug Design via Modality-Specific Optimal Schedule, ICLR 2025.\nSong Y, Shi J, Gong J, Xu M,\nStefano Ermon, Zhou H,\nWei-Ying Ma\n. Smooth Interpolation for Improved Discrete Graph Generative Models, ICML 2025.\nQiu K, Song Y, Yu J, Ma H, Cao Z, Zhang Z, Wu Y, Zheng M, Zhou H,\nWei-Ying Ma\n. Empower Structure-based Molecule Optimization with Gradient Guidance, ICML 2025.\nNi,Y., Feng,S.,  Hong,X., Sun,Y.,\nWei-Ying M\na\n, Ma,Z., Ye,Q., & Lan,Y., Pre-training with Fractional Denoising to Enhance Molecular Property Prediction, Nature Machine Intelligence, 2024.\nYang, J., Zheng, K., Long, S., Dai, X., Zhang, M., Nie, Z.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Zhou, H. Mol-AE: Auto-Encoder Based Molecular Representation Learning With 3D Cloze Test Objective, ICML 2024.\nZheng, K., Long, S., Lu T., Yang, J.,  Dai, X., Zhang, M., Nie, Z.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Zhou, H. Multi-Scale Protein Language Model for Unified Molecular Modeling, ICML 2024.\nGao, B., Ren, M., Ni, Y., Huang, Y., Qiang, B., Ma, Z.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, & Lan, Y. Rethinking Specificity in SBDD: Leveraging Delta Score and Energy-Guided Diffusion, ICML 2024.\nQu, Y., Qiu, K., Song, Y., Gong, J., Han, J., Zheng, M., Zhou H.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n. MolCRAFT: Structure-Based Drug Design in Continuous Parameter Space, ICML 2024.\nFeng,S., Ni,Y., Li,M., Huang,Y., Ma,Z.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, &Lan,Y., UniCorn: A Unified Contrastive Learning Approach for Multi-view Molecular Representation Learning, ICML 2024.\nSong, Y., Gong, J., Qu, Y., Zheng, M., Zhou, H., Liu, J., &\nWei-Ying Ma\n.\nUnified generative modeling of 3D molecules with Bayesian flow networks\n, ICLR 2024 (oral presentation, top 1.2%).\nGao, B., Jia, Y., Mo, Y., Ni, Y.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Ma, Z., & Lan, Y.\nSelf-supervised pocket pretraining via protein fragment-surroundings alignment\n, ICLR 2024.\nFeng, S., Li, M., Jia, Y.,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Lan, Y.\nProtein-ligand binding representation learning from fine-grained interactions\n, ICLR 2024.\nNi, Y., Feng, S.,\nWei-Ying M\na, Ma, Z., Lan, Y.\nSliced denoising: a physics-informed molecular pre-training method\n, ICLR 2024.\nBowen Gao, Bo Qiang, Haichuan Tan, Yinjun Jia, Minsi Ren, Minsi Lu, Jingjing Liu,\nWei-Ying Ma\n,  Yanyan Lan. DrugCLIP: Contrastive Protein-Molecule Representation Learning for Virtual Screening, NeurIPS 2023.\nYuxuan Song, Jingjing Gong, Minkai Xu, Ziyao Cao, Yanyan Lan, Stefano Ermon, Hao Zhou,\nWei-Ying Ma\n. Equivariant Flow Matching with Hybrid Probability Transport for 3D Molecule Generation, NeurIPS 2023.\nGuo Pu, Yifang Men, Yiming Mao, Yuning Jiang,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Zhouhui Lian. Controllable Image Synthesis With Attribute-Decomposed GAN. IEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell. 45(2): 1514-1532, 2023.\nShikun Feng, Yuyan Ni, Yanyan Lan, Zhi-Ming Ma,\nWei-Ying Ma\n. Fractional Denoising for 3D Molecular Pre-training. International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2023), pp. 9938-9961.\nBo Qiang, Yuxuan Song, Minkai Xu, Jingjing Gong, Bowen Gao, Hao Zhou,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Yanyan Lan. Coarse-to-Fine: a Hierarchical Diffusion Model for Molecule Generation in 3D. International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2023): 28277-28299.\nYuancheng Sun, Yimeng Chen, Weizhi Ma, Wenhao Huang, Kang Liu, Zhiming Ma,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Yanyan Lan. PEMP: Leveraging Physics Properties to Enhance Molecular Property Prediction. CIKM 2022: 3505-3513.\nJiaqi Guan, Wesley Wei Qian, Qiang Liu,\nWei-Ying Ma\n, Jianzhu Ma, Jian Peng. Energy-Inspired Molecular Conformation Optimization. International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) 2022.\nSelected Publications\nJournal Papers\n(Ranked by Citations)\n1.\nB. S. Manjunath and\nWei-Ying Ma\n.\nTexture features for browsing and retrieval of image data\n. IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence, Vol. 18, No. 8, pp. 837-842. Aug 1996.\n2.\nWei-Ying Ma\nand B. S. Manjunath.\nNetra: ...[truncated]"
