    "homepage_text": "Mingyu Ding - 丁明宇\nMingyu Ding - 丁明宇\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Computer Science at UNC-Chapel Hill\nmd at cs dot unc dot edu\n[Google Scholar]\nHi there! I am a tenure-track assistant professor at the\nDepartment of Computer Science, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill (UNC)\n. I was a postdoc at BAIR@UC Berkeley and was a visiting PhD at CSAIL@MIT. I received my doctorate from the University of Hong Kong and finished my undergraduate degrees at Renmin University of China.\nI’m leading the\nI\nntelligent\nD\nexterous\nE\nmbodied\nA\nI\nL\nab at UNC (\nIDEAL@UNC\n)\n. My research interests lie at the intersection of robotics, embodied AI, and computer vision.\nI am actively seeking self-motivated students for PhD and intern positions. If you’re interested, please fill out this\nform\nand\nemail me\nwith your CV, thanks! Meanwhile, we highly recommend you to submit your applications through the\nUNC Graduate Admissions portal\nby December 15 and list me as faculty interest.\nNews\n[06/2025]\n4 papers accepted at IROS 2025; 2 papers accepted by TPAMI.\n[05/2025]\nOur paper won the\nICRA 2025 Best Paper Award in Automation\n.\n[04/2025]\nHonored to receive the\nAmazon Research Award\n.\n[04/2025]\nInvited to give guest lectures at Duke and TAMU.\n[04/2025]\nOur paper\nPhysics-Aware Robotic Palletization with Online Masking Inference\nwas nominated as a Finalist for both Best Paper Award in Automation and Best Conference Paper Award at ICRA 2025.\n[03/2025]\nServing as Area Chairs for ICCV,  NeurIPS, CVPR, etc.\n[03/2025]\n3 papers accepted at CVPR 2025 with 1 Highlight.\n[01/2025]\n3 papers accepted at ICRA 2025; 2 papers accepted at ICLR 2025 with 1 Oral.\nResearch Highlights\nMy long-term research goal is to build intelligent robots capable of interacting with the physical world as naturally and dexterously as humans.\nRobot Learning\n: Dexterous/Loco-Manipulation, Sim2Real, RL, Physical Simulation\nFoundational Models\n: Embodied Agent, 3D vision, Multimodal LLMs, Video/World Models\nICRA25: Manipulation via Scene Flow\nIROS25: PhyGrasp\nICRA24: RT-X\nIROS25: ReBot\nNeurIPS23: EmbodiedGPT\nCVPR25: DexHandDiff\nCoRL24: Sparse Diffusion Policy\nCVPR23: EC for Embodied Control\nPreprint: Long-Horizon Tasks with LLM\nPreprint: REMAC\nCoRL22: Embodied Concept Learner\nRSS24: Human-oriented Manipulation\nNeurIPS23 Dataset: Physion++\nNeurIPS22: ComPhy Benchmark\nSelected Awards\n2025 Amazon Research Award\n2025 ICRA Best Paper Award in Automation (10/4,250)\n2024 ICRA Best Paper Award (2/3,937)\n2023 ME Rising Stars, UC Berkeley (30 worldwide/year)\n2022 WAIC Rising Stars (15 worldwide/year)\n2021 Baidu Fellowship (10 worldwide/year)\n2020 Microsoft Fellowship Nomination Award (15 in Asia/year)\n2018 Best Student Paper Runner-up Award in 25th ICONIP (2/575)\nTeachings\nSpring 2026\nCOMP 790-192 Embodied and Agentic AI, UNC\nFall 2025\nCOMP 790-186 Robot Learning, UNC\nSpring 2025\nGuest Lecture: ME555 Robotic Manipulation, Duke\nSpring 2025\nGuest Lecture: CSCE753 Computer Vision and Robot Perception, TAMU\nActivities\nArea Chair for CVPR, NeurIPS, RSS, ICCV, ICLR, WACV, VALSE\nSession Chair for ICRA 25; Associate Editor for IROS\nEditorial Board/Guest Editor for Robotics, Mechatronics and Intelligent Machines; Robotics - Embodied Intelligence; Pattern Recognition - Spatial Embodied Intelligence\nConference Reviewer for ICML, ICLR, NeurIPS, CVPR, RSS, CoRL, ICRA, IROS, ICCV, ECCV, AAAI, ACMMM, ACC, etc\nJournal Reviewer for TPAMI, TMLR, IJCV, RA-L, TIP, TCSVT, TMM, TITS, ACM CSUR, etc\n\n---\n\nCV -- Mingyu Ding\nMingyu Ding\nmyding@berkeley.edu\ndingmyu.github.io\nBAIR, UC Berkeley\nResearch interests:\nEmbodied AI, Physical Reasoning, Multimodal Planning and Robot Learning\nExperience\nPostdoc\n,\nUC Berkeley\nDec. 2022 - Present\nAdvisor: Prof. Masayoshi Tomizuka\nVisiting PhD Student\n,\nMIT\n(Cambridge, MA)\nFeb. 2022 - Aug. 2022\nAdvisor: Prof. Joshua B. Tenenbaum\nResearch Intern\n,\nMicrosoft · Cognitive Services Research\n(Redmond, WA)\nAug. - Nov. 2021\nMentored by Dr. Lu Yuan, Dr. Jingdong Wang, and Dr. Bin Xiao. Conducted research on scalable foundation models. Proposed the\nfirst vision transformer architecture (DaViT) with 1 TFLOPS achieving 90+% Top-1 accuracy on ImageNet.\nSWE Intern in Machine Learning\n,\nGoogle Brain · Tensorflow Team\n(Beijing, China)\nJun. - Sep. 2019\nHosted by Dr. Tian Lin. Created a trainable hotword detection model and deployed it on the client-side using TensorFlow Lite. Explored\ndomain adaptation methods for on-device models with few-shot data.\nEducation\nPh.D. in Computer Science\n,\nUniversity of Hong Kong\nOct. 2022\nAdvisor: Prof. Ping Luo\nM.S. in Computer Science\n,\nRenmin Univerity of China\nJun. 2019\nAdvisor: Prof. Zhiwu Lu\nB.S. in Computer Science and Mathematics\n,\nRenmin Univerity of China\nJun. 2017\nPublications\nEmbodied Concept Learner: Self-supervised Learning of Concepts and Mapping through Instruction Following\nMingyu Ding\n,\n    \n\n    \n    \n    \n    \n\n    \n        Yan Xu,\n    \n\n    \n    \n    \n    \n\n    \n        Zhenfang Chen,\n    \n\n    \n    \n    \n    \n\n    \n        David Daniel Cox,\n    \n\n    \n    \n    \n    \n\n    \n        Ping Luo,\n    \n\n    \n    \n    \n    \n\n    \n        Joshua B. Tenenbaum,\n    \n\n    \n    \n    \n    \n\n    \n        and Chuang Gan\nConference on Robot Learning (CoRL), 2022\npaper\nEmbodied Concept Learner: Self-supervised Learning of Concepts and Mapping through Instruction Following\nMingyu Ding\n, Yan Xu, Zhenfang Chen, David Daniel Cox, Ping Luo, Joshua B. Tenenbaum, Chuang Gan\nCoRL 2022\npaper\nDaViT: Dual Attention Vision Transformers\nMingyu Ding\n, Bin Xiao, Noel Codella, Ping Luo, Jingdong Wang, Lu Yuan\nECCV 2022\n[paper]\n[code]\nDynamic Visual Reasoning by Learning Differentiable Physics Models from Video and Language\nMingyu Ding\n, Zhenfang Chen, Tao Du, Ping Luo, Joshua B. Tenenbaum, Chuang Gan\nNeurIPS 2021\n[paper]\n[code]\n[project]\nMiscellaneous\nSelected Honors\n2022 WAIC Yunfan Award · Rising Star\n(15 Chinese AI researchers worldwide)\n2021 Baidu Fellowship\n(10 PhD candidates worldwide)\n2020 Microsoft Fellowship Nomination Award\n(15 nominations in Asia)\nBest Student Paper Runner-up Award in 25th International Conference on Neural Information Processing\nOutstanding Graduate of Beijing\nAcademic Services\nConference Reviewer for ICML, ICLR, NeurIPS, CVPR, ICCV, ECCV, AAAI, IROS, ACMMM, etc\nJournal Reviewer for TPAMI, IJCV, TIP, TCSVT, TMM, TOMM, TITS, TIV, RA-L, ACM CSUR, etc"
