  "deep_homepage::Yu Yang::https://sites.google.com/view/yu-yang": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/view/yu-yang",
    "all_emails": [
      "yu.yang@ise.ufl.edu"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/Zhengzhong-You",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Yu Yang's home page\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nYu Yang's home page\nYu Yang\nA\nssistant\nP\nrofessor\n,\nDepartment of Industrial and Systems Engineering\n(ISE),\nU\nF\nA\nssistant\nD\nirector\n,\nCenter for Applied Optimization\n(CAO), UF\nOffice\n:\n401C Weil Hall\nAddress\n:\nP.O. Box 116595\n, Gainesville, FL 32611\nEmail\n:\nyu.yang@ise.ufl.edu\nPhone\n: (352) 294-7727\nFax\n: 352-392-3537\nCurriculum Vitae\n:\nPDF\nGoogle Scholar\nWelcome to My Personal Webpage!\nI currently serve as an assistant professor in ISE and an assistant director of the CAO at UF. I obtained my\nPh.D. in Operations Research, with a minor in Mathematics, from\nISyE at\nGeorgia Institute of Technology in 2020.\nDuring my stay at Georgia Tech, I had the privilege to work with Prof.\nMartin Savelsbergh\n(advisor), Prof.\nNatasia Boland\n(co-advisor), Prof.\nAlan Erera\n, and Prof.\nGuanghui (George) Lan\n. Before that, I earned my\nB.S. in Applied Mathematics in 2016 from the\nSchool of Mathematical Sciences at\nPeking University\nand conducted undergraduate research under the guidance of Prof.\nZaiwen Wen\nand Prof.\nBin Dong\n.\nMy research focuses on solving critical decision-making challenges in\nlogistics,\nhealthcare,\nsustainable energy, and public safety\n. By integrating combinatorial optimization, machine learning (ML), and artificial intelligence (AI), I develop innovative solutions that provide actionable insights for organizations facing complex, real-world problems, such as drone-aided delivery, radiotherapy in cancer treatment, and the design of large-scale inland wind farms. My work on novel modeling and ML-enhanced strategies for branching, variable reduction, and decomposition has led to breakthroughs in solving these problems optimally, as exemplified by\nRouteOpt\n, our open-source software achieving leading performance in optimizing vehicle routes for last-mile delivery\n.\nI am also interested in developing zeroth- and first-order methods with provable convergence rates to tackle large-scale, high-dimensional problems arising in\nmodern AI\n/ML\napplications and business\nanalytics\n. Collectively, these efforts promote strategic planning and operational efficiency across engineering and business domains.\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Yanqiao Zhu::https://sxkdz.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://sxkdz.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "yzhu@cs.ucla.edu"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/SXKDZ",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/sxkdz",
    "homepage_text": "Yanqiao ZHU’s Homepage\nYanqiao ZHU\nCS PhD Candidate at UCLA / Large Language Models / AI for Scientific Discovery / Quantitative Trading\nFollow\nLos Angeles, CA\nEmail\nGitHub\nDBLP\nGoogle Scholar\nSemantic Scholar\nLinkedIn\nORCID\nYanqiao ZHU’s Homepage\nWelcome to my Personal Homepage!\nI am a fourth-year Ph.D. candidate in Computer Science at the\nUniversity of California, Los Angeles\n, where I work with Professors\nWei Wang\nand\nYizhou Sun\n. Before joining UCLA, I obtained my master’s degree in Computer Science from the\nInstitute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences\n.\nMy research centers on developing AI systems that understand and reason with complex, structured information. Currently, I focus on large language models for scientific applications, with particular emphasis on search, planning, and optimization capabilities that enable autonomous discovery. My broader research interests include graph and geometric deep learning and data-efficient learning. Moreover, I am also interested in quantitative trading with a focus on structured data understanding and mining in financial markets.\nRecent Updates\n[9/18/2025]\nSPiCE\naccepted to NeurIPS 2025. See you in 🌮 San Diego!\n[7/1/2025]\nLearning on Graphs conference 2025 will be held in-person at Arizona State University, 🌵 Phoenix, USA. See the calls for\npapers\nand\nreviewers\n[6/28/2025] Selected for\n2025 Amazon Fellowship\n[3/31/2025] Started internship at\nPoint72\n[2/14/2025] Selected for\nD. E. Shaw Research Doctoral & Postdoctoral Fellowship\n[1/22/2025]\nMolLEO\naccepted to ICLR 2025. Congratulations to all collaborators!\n[11/12/2024] Passed oral qualifying exam and advanced to candidacy\n[9/20/2024]\nBMRetriever\naccepted to EMNLP 2024. Congratulations to Ran and Yue!\n[6/24/2024] Started summer internship at\nMeta\n[5/1/2024]\nSciBench\naccepted to ICML 2024. Thanks to all collaborators!\n\n---\n\nCurriculum Vitae - Homepage\nYanqiao ZHU\nCS PhD Candidate at UCLA / Large Language Models / AI for Scientific Discovery / Quantitative Trading\nFollow\nLos Angeles, CA\nEmail\nGitHub\nDBLP\nGoogle Scholar\nSemantic Scholar\nLinkedIn\nORCID\nCurriculum Vitae\nClick\nhere\nto download the PDF version.\nEducation\nPh.D. Candidate\n, University of California, Los Angeles, 2022–2027 (expected)\nDissertation:\nAccelerating Chemistry Discovery with Artificial Intelligence: From Representation Learning to Autonomous Agents\nCommittee:\nWei Wang\n(Co-Chair),\nYizhou Sun\n(Co-Chair),\nAbigail G. Doyle\n,\nAditya Grover\n, and\nConnor W. Coley\nMaster of Engineering\n, Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, 2019–2022\nAdvisors:\nTieniu Tan\nand\nShu Wu\nThesis:\nSelf-Supervised Representation Learning on Graph-Structured Data\nCommittee:\nXilin Chen\n(Chair),\nZhouchen Lin\n,\nJi-Rong Wen\n,\nJiwen Lu\n,\nPeng Cui\n, and\nShiming Xiang\nExperience\nResearch Analyst Intern\n, Point72, March 2025–present\nResearch Scientist Intern\n, Meta, June 2024–January 2025\nResearch Assistant\n, NSF Center for Computer Assisted Synthesis (C-CAS), September 2022–present\nResearch Assistant\n, Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences, September 2019–June 2022\nAcademic Services\nLogistic Chairs:\nLoG\n2025, 2024, 2023, 2022\nConference Program Committee Members:\nNeurIPS 2025, 2023, 2022, 2021, 2020\nICML 2025, 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021\nICLR 2024, 2023, 2022, 2021\nKDD 2024, 2023, 2022\nWWW 2024, 2021\nAAAI 2022, 2021\nJournal Reviewers:\nIEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell.\nIEEE Trans. Med. Imaging\nIEEE Trans. Neural Netw. Learn. Syst.\nIEEE Trans. Knowl. Data Eng.\nIEEE Trans. Emerg. Topics Comput.\nIEEE Trans. Signal Inf. Process. Netw.\nIEEE Comput. Intell. Mag.\nACM Trans. Inf. Syst.\nSelected Honors and Awards\nAmazon Fellowship (2025)\nD. E. Shaw Research Doctoral and Postdoctoral Fellowship (2025)\nNeurIPS Scholar Award (2023)\nGraduate Dean’s Scholar Award, University of California, Los Angeles (2022)\nNational Scholarship, The Ministry of Education of China (2021, 2017)\nNeurIPS Outstanding Reviewer Award (2021)\nSIGMM Student Travel Grant Award (2021)\nSIGIR Student Travel Award (2021)\nICML Participation Grant (2021)\nOutstanding Student, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences (2021, 2020)\nAAAI Student Scholarship (2019)\nTeaching Experience\nTeaching Assistant for CS145: Introduction to Data Mining (Winter 2025, Spring 2024)\nTeaching Assistant for CS245: Big Data Analytics (Fall 2024, Fall 2023, Summer 2023)\nTeaching Assistant for CS247: Advanced Data Mining (Winter 2024)"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Yuanqi Du::https://yuanqidu.github.io/": {
