  "deep_homepage::Shenyang Huang::https://shenyanghuang.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://shenyanghuang.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "andy.huang@cs.ox.ac.uk",
      "shenyang.huang@mail.mcgill.ca"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/shenyangHuang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shenyang-huang",
    "homepage_text": "Shenyang Huang\nShenyang Huang\nPreferred name: Andy\nPostdoctoral Researcher at\nDepartment of Computer Science\n, University of Oxford\nPh.D.\nSchool of Computer Science\n, McGill University\nand\nMila\n- Quebec Artificial Intelligence Institute\nE-mail: shenyang.huang@mail.mcgill.ca\nE-mail: andy.huang@cs.ox.ac.uk\nGoogle Scholar:\nlink\nGithub:\nhttps://github.com/shenyangHuang\nCV:\nShenyang Huang\nLinkedin:\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/in/shenyang-huang\nX:\nshenyangHuang\nBluesky:\nshenyanghuangtg.bsky.social\nBio\nI am a Postdoctoral Researcher at the University of Oxford working with Prof.\nMichael Bronstein\n. Previously, I obtained my Ph.D. at\nMcGill University\nand affiliated with\nMila\n, \n      supervised by Prof.\nReihaneh Rabbany\nand Prof.\nGuillaume Rabusseau\n.\n      I also obtained an Honours in Computer Science from McGill University in 2019. I have a broad interest in temporal graph neural networks, graph transformers, graph neural networks and spectral methods. \n      My research focuses on machine learning models for complex and evolving networks in the real world, referred to as Temporal Graph Learning (TGL). I also actively engage in building the TGL community by organizing the\nTGL reading group\nand two editions of the\nTGL workshop\n@ NeurIPS 2022 / 2023. Through my research, I aim to answer the following questions:\nHow to design more scalable, expressive and powerful temporal graph methods?\nHow to properly evaluate temporal graph methods based on real world considerations?\nHow to deploy TGL methods for applications such as disease modeling, anomaly detection and forecasting?\nNews!\n[2025/11]\nI will be traveling to NeurIPS 2025 and then to LOG 2025, presenting papers and tutorial, see you there.\n[2025/10]\nI am excited to share that TGM: a novel deep learning library for temporal graph has entered beta. The project has been accepted at ICML CodeML workshop. TGM is designed to accelerate training on dynamic graphs while enabling rapid prototyping of new methods. Try out TGM from our\ngithub\nand learn more on\nmy Linkedin post\n[2025/07]\nI will be traveling to KDD 2025, delivering\nRelational Deep Learning Tutorial\n(Monday August 4th 8am - 11am) and organizing the\nTemporal Graph Learning Workshop\n(Monday August 4th 1pm-5pm). Feel free to reach out if you want to connect there.\n[2025/07]\nExcited to annouce that our work\nT-GRAB: A Synthetic Diagnostic Benchmark for Learning on Temporal Graphs\nis selected for\noral presentation\nat ML for Graph in the Era of GenAI workshop at KDD 2025. Thanks to my amazing collaborators: Alireza Dizaji, Benedict Aaron Tjandra, Mehrab Hamidi and Guillaume Rabusseau.\n[2025/07]\nExcited to annouce that our tutorial: Relational Deep Learning: Challenges, Foundations and Next-Generation Architectures will be presented at KDD 2025. Thanks to my amazing co-organizers at Stanford: Vijay Prakash Dwivedi, Charilaos I. Kanatsoulis and Jure Leskovec.\n          See you at KDD 2025 and here is the\ntutorial website\nand\npre-print of our survey paper\n.\n[2025/02]\nDelievered three lectures on temporal graph learning at\nCOMP 511: Network Science\n. In this lectures, I cover\nAn introduction to temporal graph\n,\nTemporal Graph Neural Networks\nas well as\nFrontiers of Temporal Graph Research\n. You can find the slides on the resource section of this website.\n[2025/01]\nI have successfully defended my thesis, my PhD journey will not be possible without my amazing supervisors prof. Reihaneh Rabbany and prof. Guillaume Rabusseau. I would also like to thank my family, friends and collaborators for their support and advice.\nPublications\n2025\nShirzadkhani, R.*, Ngo, T. G. B.*, Shamsi, K.*\nHuang, S.\n, Poursafaei, F., Azad, P., Rabbany, R., Coskunuzer, B., Rabusseau, G., and Akcora, G. C.\nMiNT: Multi-Network Training for Transfer Learning on Temporal Graphs\n(NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track)\nDwivedi, V. P., Kanatsoulis, C.,\nHuang, S.\n, Leskovec, J.\nRelational Deep Learning: Challenges, Foundations and Next-Generation Architectures\n(KDD 2025)\nYuan, Y., Zhang, Z., He, X., Nitta, A., Hu, W., Shah, M., Stojanovič, B.,\nHuang, S.\n, Lenssen, J., Leskovec, J., Fey, M.\nContextGNN: Beyond Two-Tower Recommendation Systems\n(ICLR 2025)\nDizaji, A., Tjandra, B. A., Hamidi, M.,\nHuang, S.\n, Rabusseau, G.\nT-GRAB: A Synthetic Diagnostic Benchmark for Learning on Temporal Graphs\n(KDD 2026 Datasets and Benchmarks Track, Oral at MLoG-GenAI Workshop @ KDD 2025)\nHuang, S.*\n, Chmura, J.*, Parviz, A., Poursafaei, F., Leskovec, J., Bronstein, M., Rabusseau, G., Fey, M., Rabbany, R.\nTGM: a Modular and Efficient Library for Machine Learning on Temporal Graphs\n(ICML 2025 CodeML Workshop)\nKondrup, E., Sabry, S., Abdallah, H., Yang, Z., Zhou, J., Pelrine, K., Godbout, J.F., Bronstein, M., Rabbany, R.,\nHuang, S.*\nCrediBench: Building Web-Scale Network Datasets for Information Integrity\n(NeurIPS 2025 New Perspectives in Advancing Graph Machine Learning Workshop)\nHuang, S.*\n, Ding, Z., Cao, Z., Kondrup, E., Yang, Z., Huang, X., Sui, Y.,  Yuan, Z., Zhu, Y., Hu, X., He, Y., Poursafaei, F., Bronstein, M., Vlachos, A.,\nSelf-Exploring Language Models for Explainable Link Forecasting on Temporal Graphs via Reinforcement Learning\n(NeurIPS 2025 New Perspectives in Advancing Graph Machine Learning Workshop)\nHuang, S.\n, Parviz, A., Kondrup, E., Yang, Z., Ding, Z., Bronstein, M., Rabbany, R., Rabusseau, G.\nAre Large Language Models Good Temporal Graph Learners?\n(NeurIPS 2025 New Perspectives in Advancing Graph Machine Learning Workshop)\n2024\nHuang, S.*\n, Gastinger, J.* Galkin, M., Loghmani, E., Parviz, A., Poursafaei, F., Danovitch, J., Rossi, E., Koutis, I., \n          Stuckenschmidt, H., Rabbany, R., and Rabusseau, G.\nTGB 2.0: A Benchmark for Learning on Temporal Knowledge Graphs and Heterogeneous Graphs\n(NeurIPS 2024 Datasets and Benchmark Track)\nHuang, S.*\n, Poursafaei, F.*, Rabbany, R., Rabusseau, G. and Rossi, E.\nUTG: Towards a Unified View of Snapshot and Event Based Models for Temporal Graphs\n(LOG 2024)\nPetrović., K.,\nHuang, S.\n, Poursafaei, F., and Veličković, P.\nTemporal Graph Rewiring with Expander Graphs\n(ICML 2024 Workshop on Geometry-grounded Representation Learning and Generative Modeling)\nKläser, K., Banaszewski, B., Maddrell-Mander, S., McLean, C., Müller, L., Parviz, A.,\nHuang, S.\nand Fitzgibbon, A.\nMiniMol: A Parameter-Efficient Foundation Model for Molecular Learning\n(Spotlight Poster at ICML 2024 Workshop on Efficient and Accessible Foundation Models for Biological Discovery)\nShirzadkhani, R.,\nHuang, S.\n, Leung, A. and Rabbany, R.\nStatic graph approximations of dynamic contact networks for epidemic forecasting\n(Scientific Reports)\nShamsi, K., Poursafaei, F.,\nHuang, S.,\nNgo, B., Coskunuzer, B. and Akcora, C.\nGraphpulse: Topological representations for temporal graph property prediction\n(ICLR 2024)\nBeaini, D.,\nHuang, S.,\nCunha, J.A., Li, Z., Moisescu-Pareja, G., Dymov, O., Maddrell-Mander, S., McLean, C., Wenkel, F., Müller, L., Mohamud, J., Parviz, A., Craig, M., Koziarski, M., Lu, J., Zhu, Z., Gabellini, C., Klaser, K., Dean, J., Wognum, C., Sypetkowski, M., Rabusseau, G., Rabbany, R., Tang, J., Morris, C., Koutis, I., Ravanelli, M., Wolf, G., Tossou, P., Mary, H., Bois, T., Fitzgibbon, A., Banaszewski, B., Martin, C., and Masters, D.\nTowards Foundational Models For Molecular Learning on Large-scale Multi-task Datasets\n(ICLR 2024)\nShirzadkhani, R.,\nHuang, S.,\n, Kooshafar, E., Rabbany, R., and Poursafaei, F.\nTemporal Graph Analysis with TGX\n(WSDM 2024 Demo Track)\n2023\nHuang, S.*,\n, Poursafaei, F.*, Danovitch, J.,  Fey, M., Hu, W., Rossi, E., Leskovec, J., Bronstein, M., Rabusseau G. and Rabbany R.\nTemporal Graph Benchmark for Machine Learning on Temporal Graphs\n(NeurIPS 2023 Datasets and Benchmarks Track)\nHuang, S.,\n, Danovitch, J., Rabusseau G., Rabbany R.\nFast and Attributed Change Detection on Dynamic Graphs with Density of States\n(PAKDD 2023)\nHuang, S.,\nCoulombe, S., Hitti, Y., Rabbany, R., Rabusseau, G.\nLaplacian Change Point Detection for Single and Multi-view Dynamic Graphs\n(ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data, TKDD)\nMasters, D., Dean, J. Klaser, K., Li, Z., Mander, S., Sanders, A., Helal, H., Beker, D., Fitzgibbon, A.,\nHuang, S.,\nRampášek, L., Beaini, D.\nGPS++: Reviving the Art of Message Passing for Molecular Property Prediction\n(TMLR)\nPupneja, Y., Zou, J., Lévy, S.\nHuang, S.,\nUnderstanding Opinions Towards Climate Change on Social Media\n(NeurIPS 2023 Workshop: Tackling Climate Change with Machine Learning)\nJiang, L., Zhang, C., Poursafaei, F.,\nHuang, S.,\nTowards Temporal Edge Regression: A Case Study on Agriculture Trade Between Nations\n(preprint)\n2022\nHuang, S.*,\nPoursafaei, F.*, Pelrine, K., Rabbany, R.\nTowards Better Evaluation for Dynamic Link Prediction\n(NeurIPS 2022 Datasets and Benchmarks Track)\n2021\nHuang, S.\n, Wang, K.,  Rabusseau, G., & Makhzani, A.\nFew Shot Image Generation via Implicit Autoencoding of Support Sets\n5th Workshop on Meta-Learning at NeurIPS 2021\nHuang, S.\n, Rabusseau, G. & Rabbany, R.\nScalable Change Point Detection for Dynamic Graphs\n6th Outlier Detection and Description Workshop at KDD 2021\nHuang, S.\n, François-Lavet, V., & Rabusseau, G.\nUnderstanding Capacity Saturation in Incremental Learning\n. Canadian Conference on Artificial Intelligence 2021\nDing, X.,\nHuang, S.,\nLeung, A., Rabbany, R.\nIncorporating dynamic flight network in SEIR to model mobility between populations\n. Applied Network Science, Special issue on Epidemics Dynamics & Control on Networks\n2020\nHuang, S.\n, Hitti, Y., Rabusseau, G. & Rabbany, R.\nLaplacian Change Point Detection for Dynamic Graphs\n. (KDD 2020)\nLeung, A., Ding, X.,\nHuang, S.,\nRabbany, R.\nContact Graph Epidemic Modelling of COVID-19 for Transmission and Intervention Strategies\n.\nAlletto, S.,\nHuang, S.\n, François-Lavet, V., Nakata, Y., & Rabusseau, G.\nRandomNet: Towards Fully Automatic Neural Architecture Design for Multimodal Learning.\nAAAI 2020 Meta-Eval Workshop\n2019\nHuang, S.\n, François-Lavet, V., & Rabusseau, G.\nNeural Architecture Search for Class-incremental Learning\n(previous version of \"Understanding Capacity Saturation in Incremental Learning\")\n2018\nHuang, S.\n, François-Lavet, V., Rabusseau, G. & Pineau, J.\nExploring Continual Learning Using Incremental Architecture Search\nNeuIPS Continual Learning Workshop 2018.\nBlogs\nTemporal Graph Learning in 2024\nTemporal Graph Benchmark\nTemporal Graph Learning in 2023\nTowards Better Link Prediction in Dynamic Graphs\nFlight-SEIR, Flight Data for Epidemiological Modelling\nLaplacian Change Point Detection for Dynamic Graphs\nLecture Slides\nTutorial Speaker, KDD 2025,\nRelational Deep Learning: Challenges, Foundations and Next-Generation Architectures\nLectures on Temporal Graph Learning, COMP 511 Network Science McGill University Winter 2025\nIntroduction to Temporal Graph Learning\nTemporal Graph Neural Networks\nFrontiers of Temporal Graph Learning\nGuest Lecture, COMP 599 Network Science McGill University Fall 2022 / 2021\nFall 2022,\nAnomaly Detection for Dynamic Graphs (updated slides)\nFall 2021,\nAnomaly Detection for Dynamic Graphs\nTeaching\nKDD 2025, Tutorial Speaker,\nRelational Deep Learning: Challenges, Foundations and Next-Generation Architectures\nWinter 2025, Course Lecturer for Advanced Topic on Temporal Graph Learning,\n3 Lectures on TGL\n,\nCOMP 511 Network Science\n, McGill University\nWinter 2025, Mentor,\nGeometric Deep Learning L65\n, University of Cambridge\nWinter 2024, Mentor,\nGeometric Deep Learning L65\n, University of Cambridge\nWinter 2023, Mentor,\nRepresentation Learning on Graphs and Networks L45\n, University of Cambridge\nFall 2022, Guest Lecturer,\nAnomaly Detection for Dynamic Graphs (updated slides)\n,\nCOMP 599 Network Science\n, McGill University\nFall 2021, Guest Lecturer,\nAnomaly Det