    "homepage_text": "Home | Shirley Zhang Lab\ntop of page\nShirley Zhang Lab\nChronobiology research group\nOur research focus is on understanding the temporal gate separating the body from the brain\nResearch\nWelcome to Zhang Lab! We study the blood-brain barrier (BBB), a structure that both protects delicate neurons from environmental toxins yet also allows transport of nutrients. A molecular clock within this gateway to the brain makes it highly dynamic over the course of the day:night cycle. Our work seeks to understand how the clock influences the BBB, what molecules are gated by time, and how this process might be changed by disease.\nRead More\nAnnouncements\nFeb 2025 G3 publication \"Arsenic impairs\nDrosophila\nneural stem cell mitotic progression and sleep behavior in a tauopathy model\"\nYay! Arsenic for the win. Wait what?\nFlies are #1!\nJan 2023 eLife publication \"Ecdysone acts through cortex glia to regulate sleep in Drosophila\"\nYay! I've been mentioned.\nMar 2024 Circulation Research review published \"Circadian rhythms of the blood-brain barrier and drug delivery\"\nI'm in a book!\nMay 2024 Book chapter published in Vitamins and Hormones \"A circadian clock regulates the blood-brain barrier across phylogeny\"\nWhat? Why are there no mice in this paper?\nOct 2024 eLife \"Evidence for a role of human blood-born factors in mediating age-associated changes in molecular circadian rhythms\"\nReady. Set. Go!\nSept 2021 Zhang lab starts at Emory University\nJan 2025 PNAS publication \"Carnitine Transporter at the Blood-Brain Barrier Modulates Sleep via Glial Lipid Metabolism in\nDrosophila\n\"\nI win! Flies are the best.\nWho needs vertebrae to have fun? or sleep?\nWhat? Why are there no mice in this paper?\nOct 2024 eLife \"Evidence for a role of human blood-born factors in mediating age-associated changes in molecular circadian rhythms\"\nMy big debut. Look ma,  I'm a star!\nDec 2025 JCBFM. \"Pharmacological modulation of circadian rhythms in brain microvasculature\"\nbottom of page\n\n---\n\nContact | Shirley Zhang Lab\ntop of page\nShirley Zhang Lab\nContact Us\nWe are looking for dedicated scientists at all levels to join our team. Undergraduates, graduates, research technicians, and postdoctoral researchers are welcome.\nEmory University School of Medicine: Cell Biology Department\n​\nWhitehead Biomedical Research Building\n615 Michael St. Atlanta, GA 30322\nLab room 535\n|\nOffice room 543\n​\n​\n​\nEmory lab website\nYou can also contact us by filling this contact form:\nFirst Name\nLast Name\nEmail\nPhone\nSubject\nMessage\nSubmit\nThanks for submitting!\nbottom of page\n\n---\n\nContact | Shirley Zhang Lab\ntop of page\nShirley Zhang Lab\nContact Us\nWe are looking for dedicated scientists at all levels to join our team. Undergraduates, graduates, research technicians, and postdoctoral researchers are welcome.\nEmory University School of Medicine: Cell Biology Department\n​\nWhitehead Biomedical Research Building\n615 Michael St. Atlanta, GA 30322\nLab room 535\n|\nOffice room 543\n​\n​\n​\nEmory lab website\nYou can also contact us by filling this contact form:\nFirst Name\nLast Name\nEmail\nPhone\nSubject\nMessage\nSubmit\nThanks for submitting!\nbottom of page"
  },
