    "homepage_text": "Junghyun Lee\nSearch\nJunghyun Lee\nPhD Student\nKAIST AI\nWelcome!\nI am a final-year PhD student at the\nKim Jaechul Graduate School of AI\n, KAIST, where I am very fortunate to be advised by\nSe-Young Yun\n(\nOSI Lab\n) and\nChulhee “Charlie” Yun\n(OptiML Lab).\nI had the pleasure of spending the summer of 2025 as a Research Scientist Intern at Adobe Research, San Jose, working with\nBranislav “Brano” Kveton\n,\nAnup Rao\n,\nSubhojyoti Mukherjee\n,\nRyan A. Rossi\n,\nSunav Choudhary\n, and\nAlexa Siu\n.\nMy research focuses primarily on\ninteractive machine learning\n(bandits, RL, active learning), “theoretical aspects” of LLMs (alignment, reasoning), optimization theory, and statistical analyses of large networks with an emphasis on\ncommunity detection\n.\nMore broadly, my interests encompass all aspects of\nmathematical and theoretical AI\n, as well as related problems in mathematics and statistics.\nI enjoy organizing seminars and workshops to foster Korea’s theoretical AI community; see ‘Organizer’.\nOutside of research, I am an avid violinist and have performed as a first violinist with various amateur orchestras, including the KAIST Orchestra (KAO), PASSIONATE Orchestra, MDOP Orchestra, KAO-S, PROJECT Starlight, and PROJECT IDEA.\nFeel free to reach out if you’d like to collaborate on any of my research topics or just to connect!\n(\nThird-person bio here\n.)\n(Last updated: 2026.02.12.)\nInterests\nBandits, Online Learning\nStatistical RL\nTopics related to LLMs\nStatistics, Learning Theory\nCommunity Detection, Networks\nDL/Optim Theory\nAlgorithmic Fairness\nDistributed Algorithms\nEducation\nPhD in Artificial Intelligence (GSAI), 2023-\nKorea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology\nMSc in Artificial Intelligence (GSAI), 2021-2023\nKorea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology\nBSc in Mathematical Sciences and Computer Science (double major, cum laude), 2017-2021\nKorea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology\nFeatured Publications\nGL-LowPopArt: A Nearly Instance-Wise Minimax-Optimal Estimator for Generalized Low-Rank Trace Regression\nWe present GL-LowPopArt, a novel Catoni-style estimator for generalized low-rank trace regression. Building on LowPopArt (Jang et al., …\nJunghyun Lee\n,\nKyoungseok Jang\n,\nKwang-Sung Jun\n,\nMilan Vojnović\n,\nSe-Young Yun\nPDF\nCite\nNear-Optimal Clustering in Mixture of Markov Chains\nWe study the problem of clustering T trajectories of length H, each generated by one of K unknown ergodic Markov chains over a finite …\nJunghyun Lee\n,\nYassir Jedra\n,\nAlexandre Proutière\n,\nSe-Young Yun\nPDF\nCite\nRegularized Online RLHF with Generalized Bilinear Preferences\nWe consider the problem of contextual online RLHF with general preferences, where the goal is to identify the Nash Equilibrium. We …\nJunghyun Lee\n,\nMinju Hong\n,\nKwang-Sung Jun\n,\nChulhee Yun\n,\nSe-Young Yun\nPDF\nCite\nA Jointly Efficient and Optimal Algorithm for Heteroskedastic Generalized Linear Bandits with Adversarial Corruptions\nWe consider the problem of heteroskedastic generalized linear bandits (GLBs) with adversarial corruptions, which subsumes various …\nSanghwa Kim\n,\nJunghyun Lee\n,\nSe-Young Yun\nPDF\nCite\nLearning to Reason in LLMs by Expectation Maximization\nLarge language models (LLMs) solve reasoning problems by first generating a rationale and then answering. We formalize reasoning as a …\nJunghyun Lee\n,\nBranislav Kveton\n,\nAnup Rao\n,\nSubhojyoti Mukherjee\n,\nRyan A. Rossi\n,\nSunav Choudhary\n,\nAlexa Siu\nPDF\nCite\nSee all publications\nCollaborators\nAlexa Siu\nAlexandre Proutière\nAnup Rao\nBohan Wang\nBranislav Kveton\nChang D. Yoo\nChulhee Yun\nGwangsu Kim\nHanseul Cho\nHuishuai Zhang\nJinwoo Shin\nKunwoo Na\nKwang-Sung Jun\nKyoungseok Jang\nLaura Schmid\nMilan Vojnović\nMinju Hong\nPrin Phunyaphibarn\nRyan A. Rossi\nSanghwa Kim\nSe-Young Yun\nSeong Jin Cho\nShin Yoo\nSubhojyoti Mukherjee\nSunav Choudhary\nSungbin Lim\nWoosung Koh\nYassir Jedra\nKeywords\nActive Learning\nBandits\nClustering\nDeep Learning Theory\nDiffusion Model\nFairness\nInformation Theory\nLearning in Games\nLLMs\nMarkov Chain\nMARL\nNetworks\nOnline Learning\nOptimization\nProbabilistic Machine Learning\nProbability Theory\nReasoning\nRL\nRLHF\nSBSE\nStatistics\nContact\njh_lee00(AT)kaist.ac.kr\n85 Hoegi-ro, Dondaemun-gu, Seoul, 02455\nCite\n×\nCopy\nDownload\n\n---\n\nJunghyun Lee is a fourth-year PhD student at the Kim Jaechul Graduate School of AI, KAIST, jointly advised by Professors Se-Young Yun (OSI Lab) and Chulhee \"Charlie\" Yun (OptiML Lab). His research primarily focuses on mathematical and theoretical AI, with a strong emphasis on interactive machine learning (bandits, RL, active learning), theoretical aspects of LLMs (alignment, reasoning), optimization theory, and statistical analysis of large networks (community detection).\nHe spent the summer of 2025 as a research scientist intern at Adobe Research, San Jose, working with Branislav \"Brano\" Kveton and others.\nHis work has been presented at leading international AI conferences, including AISTATS, NeurIPS, ICLR, and AAAI, where he has also contributed as a reviewer. He received the Best Reviewer Award at AISTATS 2025, Best Student Paper Award at OPODIS 2023, and Best Paper Awards at multiple Korean AI conferences, including KSC '23, JKAIA '21, and CKAIA '22 and '24. Junghyun earned his MSc from the same school and his BSc in Mathematical Sciences and Computer Science (double major, cum laude), also from KAIST.\n\n---\n\nJunghyun Lee is a fourth-year PhD student at the Kim Jaechul Graduate School of AI, KAIST, jointly advised by Professors Se-Young Yun (OSI Lab) and Chulhee \"Charlie\" Yun (OptiML Lab). His research primarily focuses on mathematical and theoretical AI, with a strong emphasis on interactive machine learning (bandits, RL, active learning), theoretical aspects of LLMs (alignment, reasoning), optimization theory, and statistical analysis of large networks (community detection).\nHe spent the summer of 2025 as a research scientist intern at Adobe Research, San Jose, working with Branislav \"Brano\" Kveton and others.\nHis work has been presented at leading international AI conferences, including AISTATS, NeurIPS, ICLR, and AAAI, where he has also contributed as a reviewer. He received the Best Reviewer Award at AISTATS 2025, Best Student Paper Award at OPODIS 2023, and Best Paper Awards at multiple Korean AI conferences, including KSC '23, JKAIA '21, and CKAIA '22 and '24. Junghyun earned his MSc from the same school and his BSc in Mathematical Sciences and Computer Science (double major, cum laude), also from KAIST."
  },
