    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/szl2",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Fanghui Liu\nFanghui Liu\n☰\n×\nAbout\nResearch\nPublications\nTalks\nAwards\nTeaching\nCourses\nTutorials\nSupervision\nStudents\nOpen Positions\nService\nAcademic Service\nNeurIPS’24 FITML Workshop\nWelcome to Fanghui Liu's Homepage!\nI received my Bachelor’s degree in Automation from Harbin Institute of Technology in 2014 and my PhD degree from Shanghai Jiao Tong University (SJTU) in 2019. I subsequently held postdoctoral research positions at KU Leuven and EPFL from 2019 to 2023. After that, I have held faculty positions (or affiliated) at the University of Warwick, the Centre for Discrete Mathematics and its Applications, DIMAP;\nFAM Division\n; the Technical University of Munich (Global Visiting Professor), and Shanghai Jiao Tong University. I received AAAI’24 New Faculty Award, Rising Stars in AI Symposium (KAUST 2023), and several Excellent Doctoral Dissertation Awards at 2019.\nEmail:\nx\n@\ny\nwith\nx\n=\nfanghui.liu\n,\ny\n=\nsjtu.edu.cn\nor\nwarwick.ac.uk\nor\nepfl.ch\nThe website was updated at Dec. 22nd, 2025.\nResearch Interests\nI'm generally interested in\nfoundations of modern machine learning\nfrom the lens of\nlearning efficiency\n, both theoretically and empirically. My research is always contributing to how to\nhandle nonlinearity at a theoretical level\nand how to precisely and efficiently\napproximate nonlinearity at a practical level\nunder theoretical guidelines, which is a longstanding question over science, technology and engineering.\nMy research (the past, ongoing and future) focuses on the following directions:\nmachine learning theory: what is the largest function space that can be learned by neural networks, both statistically and computationally efficiently (computational-statistical gaps)?\npost-training in LLMs:  expand the frontiers of empirical and theoretical knowledge on when and where to fine-tune\ninference, and how much we can fine-tune\ninference, precisely, efficiently, and robustly\nMy research is supported by grants from Royal Society (UK), Alan Turing Institute (UK), and DAAD (Germany).\nWe're organising\nIJCV Special Issue on Post-Training in Large Language Models for Computer Vision\n!\nVisitors\nPowered by\njemdoc + new design\n."
  },
  "deep_homepage::Wenxuan Zhang::https://isakzhang.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://isakzhang.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "wxzhang@sutd.edu.sg"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/DAMO-NLP-SG",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/wenxuan-zhang-608b88153/",
    "homepage_text": "Wenxuan Zhang's Homepage\nWenxuan Zhang\n(张雯轩)\nwxzhang [at] sutd.edu.sg\nSUTD Assistant Professor\nInformation Systems Technology and Design Pillar\nSingapore University of Technology and Design\nFaculty Page\n|\nGoogle Scholar\n|\nLinkedIn\n|\nX (Twitter)\n|\n小红书\nAbout\nPublications\nGroup\nOpenings\nAbout me\n[\nShort bio\n]\nI am currently a tenure-track Assistant Professor at the Information Systems Technology and Design (ISTD) Pillar, Singapore University of Technology and Design (SUTD), under the prestigious \"\nSUTD Assistant Professorship (SAP)\n\" early career award. Before this, I was a research scientist at\nAlibaba Group Singapore\n. I received my Ph.D. from\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong\n, where I was advised by\nProf. Wai Lam\n.\nMy research interests lie broadly in natural language processing and machine learning, though most of my research are centered around large language models these days. \n  I am passinate to advance NLP models that are\ninclusive\n, supporting diverse languages and cultures (e.g., multilingual large language models), while also\ntrustworthy\nthrough techniques that improve understanding, safety and robustness of language models.\nCurrent Research Projects (more on\nresearch page\n):\n[\nMultilingual/Multicultural\n] Building (and understanding) multilingual & multicultural & multimodal LLMs (\nSeaLLMs\n,\nBabel\n,\nSeaLLMs-Audio\n,\nAbstract-Thought\n)\n[\nAccessiblity\n] Making LLMs more accessible and efficient (e.g.,\nCustom Pruning\n,\nPEAR\n)\n[\nSafety\n] Ensuring safety of LLMs from development to deployment (\nMultiJail\n,\nSafety Neuron\n)\n[\nEvaluation\n] Fair & dynamic evaluation of foundation models (\nM3Exam\n,\nAuto-Arena\n,\nDR-Arena\n)\n[\nInterpretability\n] Understaning what is happening inside LLMs (\nKnowledge\n& Reasoning)\n📍\nWe have multiple fully-funded positions (Postdoc / PhD (2026 Fall) / Visiting) available — see\n[details]\n. Feel free to email me if you're interested. Students with relevant research or publication experience will be preferred.\nNews\n(\nI have to admit this section doesn't get updated as often as my\nsocial media\naccounts do\n)\n[Jan 2026] Happy to share that we have 4 papers accepted to ICLR 2026 (out of 4 submissions!)\n[Jan 2026] Invited to serve as Registration Chair for AACL 2026\n[Jan 2026] Invited to serve as Area Chair for COLM 2026\n[Dec 2026] Excited to share that our project about \"Culturally-Aware Proactive Conversational AI\" has been awarded\nSGD 1M\nin funding under the AISG Research-Governance Grant!\n[Nov 2025] Invited to serve as an area chair at ICML 2026\n[Nov 2025] Happy to share that I will give a talk at AAAI 2026 New Faculty Highlight Programme!\n[Oct 2025] Glad to give a talk at China-Singapore InnovateConnect 2025 on \"Culturally Intelligent AI\"\n[Sep 2025] Glad to be listed as a Stanford Top 2% Scientist this year!\n[Aug 2025] Invited to serve as an area chair for ICLR 2026\n[May 2025] Glad to share that we have 8 (out of 8 submissions!) papers accepted to\nACL 2025\n, huge congrats to all students, and see you in Vienna 🇦🇹! (Coffee chats are welcome!)\n[Apr 2025] I was interviewed at AIMX Podcast to share my thoughts on various topics of multilingual LLMs, also got the chance to share SeaLLMs-Audio and Babel project,\ncheck it out on Spotify\n!\n[Mar 2025] I will serve as an area chair for COLM 2025 and NeurIPS 2025\n[Mar 2025] We release\nSeaLLMs-Audio\n- the multimodal (audio) extension of SeaLLMs and the first large audio-language model designed to support multiple Southeast Asian languages!\n[Mar 2025] We release\nBabel\n- open-source multilingual LLMs that serve over 90% of global speakers. Two sizes of models (9B and 83B) have been released, both base and chat versions are open-sourced!\n[Feb 2025] I officially joined SUTD!\n[Jan 2025] Thrilled to receive the prestigious \"\nSUTD Assistant Professorship (SAP)\n\" award\n[Oct 2024] Glad to be invited to give a keynote talk at SSNLP 2024.\n[Aug 2024] I will serve as an Area Chair for ICLR 2025\n[July 2024]\nSeaLLMs 3\nis here - the latest iteration of SeaLLMs with SOTA performance of diverse tasks while specifically enhanced to be more trustworthy. Check out the\nmodel\nand\npaper\n!\n[June 2024] SeaLLMs was awarded the\n\"Best Innovate for Impact Award\"\nby The International Telecommunication Union (ITU) of United Nations.\n[May 2024] M3Exam was officialy used to\nevaluate GPT-4o's multilingual and multimodal capability\n.\n[Apr 2024] I am honored to speak at SCS and IMDA about our SeaLLMs project and the evaluation / safety measures we've implemented during the development.\n[Nov 2023] We release\nSeaLLMs\n- the first LLM dedicated to languages in Southeast Asia region.\nSelected Honors & Awards\nAAAI New Faculty Highlight, 2026\nWorld's Top 2% Scientists (by Stanford and Elsevier), 2025\nSUTD Assistant Professorship\n(SAP) award, 2025\nAlibaba Star (阿里星), Alibaba Group, 2022\nProfessional Service\nOrganizing Committee:\nGeneral Chair,\nThe 2026 Singapore Symposium on Natural Language Processing (SSNLP 2026)\nArea Chair: ACL 2026, ICML 2026, COLM 2026, ICLR 2026, EMNLP 2025, NeurIPS 2025, ACL 2025, COLM 2025, ICLR 2025, NAACL 2025, EMNLP 2024\nJournal Reviewer: Computational Linguistics (CL), ACM Transactions on Information Systems (TOIS), IEEE Transactions on Knowledge and Data Engineering (TKDE), ACM Transactions on the Web (TWEB), Neurocomputing, Transactions on Audio, Speech and Language Processing (TASLP)\nTeaching\n51.511 Multimodal Generative AI: 2026 Spring\n50.040 Natural Language Processing\n: 2025 Fall (check the course website for a brand new NLP course!)\n50.043 Database Systems: 2025 Summer\nInvited Talks\nJan 2026: invited panel discussion \"LLM Localization: Challenges and Opportunities\" at\nIAAI 2026\nNov 2025: invited panel discussion at\nSEA-LION Summit @ AIMX 2025\nOct 2025: invited talk at InnovateConnect 2025 on \"Culturally Intelligent AI\"\nNov 2024: invited keynote talk at\nSSNLP 2024\nOct 2024: invited talk and panel discussion at\nSingapore Week of Innovation Technology (SWITCH) 2024\nJuly 2024: invited keynote talk at\nSingapore ACM SIGKDD Symposium 2024\n.\nApr 2024: invited talk on the SeaLLMs project at\nSingapore Computer Society (SCS)\nApr 2024: invited talk at\nInfocomm Media Development Authority (IMDA)\nof Singapore\nSep 2023: invited talk on M3Exam at multilingual & multimodal LLMs session of\nMLNLP 2023\nAug 2023: half-day tutorial \"\nSentiment Analysis in the Era of LLMs\n\" at IJCAI 2023. [\nslides\n]\nMar 2023: invited talk \"A Survey on Aspect-Based Sentiment Analysis\" at\nSingapore Symposium\n      on Sentiment Analysis (S3A)\n.\nLast updated: Feb 2026\n\n---\n\nWenxuan Zhang's Homepage\nWenxuan Zhang\n(张雯轩)\nwxzhang [at] sutd.edu.sg\nSUTD Assistant Professor\nInformation Systems Technology and Design (ISTD) pillar\nSingapore University of Technology and Design\nFaculty Page\n|\nGoogle Scholar\n|\nLinkedIn\n|\nX (Twitter)\n|\nZhihu\nAbout\nPublications\nGetting Invovled\nShort Bio\nDr Wenxuan Zhang is currently a tenure-track SUTD Assistant Professor (SAP) at the Information Systems Technology and Design (ISTD) pillar, Singapore University of Technology and Design (SUTD). He received his PhD degree from the Chinese University of Hong Kong, and then joined Alibaba Singapore as a research scientist with the prestigious Ali Star award. His primary research areas are natural language processing (NLP) and large language models (LLMs). His research aims to advance NLP models that are inclusive, supporting diverse languages and cultures through multilingual language models; while also trustworthy by improving the understanding, safety, and robustness of the models. He (co-)led multiple influential open-source research projects, including SeaLLMs (LLMs specialized for Southeast Asian languages, with 300k+ model downloads), Babel (general multilingual large language models serving over 90% global speakers), M3Exam, and AutoArena. He has published over 40 papers in top-tier AI conferences and journals, where multiple papers have been recognized as among the top 2% or top 3% most cited at their respective conferences. He also regularly serves as an area chair and on program committees for multiple leading conferences and journals.\nLast updated: Feb 2025\n\n---\n\nWenxuan Zhang's Homepage\nWenxuan Zhang\n(张雯轩)\nwxzhang [at] sutd.edu.sg\nSUTD Assistant Professor\nInformation Systems Technology and Design (ISTD) pillar\nSingapore University of Technology and Design\nFaculty Page\n|\nGoogle Scholar\n|\nLinkedIn\n|\nX (Twitter)\n|\nZhihu\nAbout\nPublications\nGetting Invovled\nShort Bio\nDr Wenxuan Zhang is currently a tenure-track SUTD Assistant Professor (SAP) at the Information Systems Technology and Design (ISTD) pillar, Singapore University of Technology and Design (SUTD). He received his PhD degree from the Chinese University of Hong Kong, and then joined Alibaba Singapore as a research scientist with the prestigious Ali Star award. His primary research areas are natural language processing (NLP) and large language models (LLMs). His research aims to advance NLP models that are inclusive, supporting diverse languages and cultures through multilingual language models; while also trustworthy by improving the understanding, safety, and robustness of the models. He (co-)led multiple influential open-source research projects, including SeaLLMs (LLMs specialized for Southeast Asian languages, with 300k+ model downloads), Babel (general multilingual large language models serving over 90% global speakers), M3Exam, and AutoArena. He has published over 40 papers in top-tier AI conferences and journals, where multiple papers have been recognized as among the top 2% or top 3% most cited at their respective conferences. He also regularly serves as an area chair and on program committees for multiple leading conferences and journals.\nLast updated: Feb 2025"
  },
