    "homepage_text": "Ganghua Wang | Nice to meat you.\nHomepage\nGanghua Wang\nFaraco Postdoctoral Fellow\nData Science Institute\nUniversity of Chicago\n5460 S University Avenue, Room 202\nChicago IL, 60615\nHi, I am Ganghua Wang, a Faraco Postdoctoral Fellow at the Data Science Institute, University of Chicago, fortunately mentored by Prof. Haifeng Xu and Prof. Bo Li. I received my Ph.D. in Statistics from University of Minnesota in 2024, where I was fortunate to be advised by Prof. Jie Ding and Prof. Yuhong Yang. My current research focuses on trustworthy AI, exploring both its theoretical foundations and the development of practical algorithms. Feel free to visit my website to learn more about my research.\nI will join the Department of Mathematics at the University of Arizona as a tenure-track Assistant Professor starting in August 2026. I am always looking for self-motivated graduate students to join my group. If you are interested in working with me, please feel free to contact me and include your CV.\nNews\nOur paper “Model Privacy: A Unified Framework for Understanding Model Stealing Attacks and Defenses.” was accepted by the Journal of the Royal Statistical Society, Series B!\nI am honored to be\nselected as the DSI Faraco Postdoctoral Fellow\nfor the 2025–2026 academic year, and I am grateful for this recognition and support.\n\n---\n\nAbout | Ganghua Wang\nHomepage\nThis is a personal website of Ganghua Wang, created using\nJekyll\n.\nGallery\n2025\nHello World, Luca! Welcomed our first kid.\n2024\nAfter my defense. From left to right are Prof.\nJie Ding\n, Yingyu Shen, Ganghua Wang, and Prof.\nYuhong Yang\n.\n2023\nMe attending JSM 2023, Toronto, Canada.\nYingyu Shen (Left 1); big, medium, and small good sons (in the backpack); really big peanut penguin (Right 1). It is my fortune to have you as my wife and have those three adorable \"kids\".\n2021\nLake Superior. From left to right are Xinran Wang, Prof.\nJie Ding\n, Prof.\nYuhong Yang\nand Ganghua Wang.\n2020\nWith four parameters I can fit an elephant, and with five I can make him wiggle his trunk.\n-- John von Neumann\n$\\leftarrow$ How to fit an elephant like this?\n2019\nA comic from\nPhD comics\n.\nA picture of Ford Hall.\nThanks letter I received as TA.\n2018\nA toy example of style transfer:\nFast Patch-based Style Transfer of Arbitrary Style\n.\nStyle picture.\nOriginal photo of Boya Tower,\nPeking University.\nFinal output.\nFor the sake of completeness, I attach a pic of Weiming Lake below as well.\nWeiming Lake, Peking University.\n2017\nA drunk man will find his way home, but a drunk bird may get lost forever.\n-- Shizuo Kakutani\nThe first time I heard this story is from my instructor of\nApplied Stochastic Processes\n,\nProf. Dayue Chen\n.\nAnother example I learned in this course is that “Life is a martingale.” Though he meant that life is ergodic and don’t feel upset when you meet troubles, I hope that life can be a sub-martingale.\n\n---\n\nAbout | Ganghua Wang\nHomepage\nThis is a personal website of Ganghua Wang, created using\nJekyll\n.\nGallery\n2025\nHello World, Luca! Welcomed our first kid.\n2024\nAfter my defense. From left to right are Prof.\nJie Ding\n, Yingyu Shen, Ganghua Wang, and Prof.\nYuhong Yang\n.\n2023\nMe attending JSM 2023, Toronto, Canada.\nYingyu Shen (Left 1); big, medium, and small good sons (in the backpack); really big peanut penguin (Right 1). It is my fortune to have you as my wife and have those three adorable \"kids\".\n2021\nLake Superior. From left to right are Xinran Wang, Prof.\nJie Ding\n, Prof.\nYuhong Yang\nand Ganghua Wang.\n2020\nWith four parameters I can fit an elephant, and with five I can make him wiggle his trunk.\n-- John von Neumann\n$\\leftarrow$ How to fit an elephant like this?\n2019\nA comic from\nPhD comics\n.\nA picture of Ford Hall.\nThanks letter I received as TA.\n2018\nA toy example of style transfer:\nFast Patch-based Style Transfer of Arbitrary Style\n.\nStyle picture.\nOriginal photo of Boya Tower,\nPeking University.\nFinal output.\nFor the sake of completeness, I attach a pic of Weiming Lake below as well.\nWeiming Lake, Peking University.\n2017\nA drunk man will find his way home, but a drunk bird may get lost forever.\n-- Shizuo Kakutani\nThe first time I heard this story is from my instructor of\nApplied Stochastic Processes\n,\nProf. Dayue Chen\n.\nAnother example I learned in this course is that “Life is a martingale.” Though he meant that life is ergodic and don’t feel upset when you meet troubles, I hope that life can be a sub-martingale."
  },
  "deep_homepage::Xuan Bi::https://sites.google.com/site/xuanbigts/home": {
