    "homepage_text": "Ke Wang - Homepage - Ke Wang - Homepage\nKe Wang\nSoftware Engineer at Meta, working on open source database query engine Presto and Velox\nFollow\nSunnyvale, CA\nMeta\nEmail\nGoogle Scholar\nGitHub\nLinkedIn\nKe Wang - Homepage\nAbout Me\nKe Wang is currently a software engineer at Meta, working on open source database query engine\nPresto\nand\nVelox\n.\nBefore that, he spent two years at Ant Group, working on next-generation payment systems. He also worked as a full-time researcher in Shanghai Qi Zhi Institute, with a focus on database index on persistent memory.\nKe is actively working on data-intensive applications research, including but not limited to database, high performance computing system and storage system, and machine learning applications. He worked closely with\nProf. Tong Yang\n,\nProf. Xin Jin\nand\nProf. Mingyu Gao\n.\nLast updated: April 2025\n\n---\n\nRedirecting…\nRedirecting…\nClick here if you are not redirected.\n\n---\n\nRedirecting…\nRedirecting…\nClick here if you are not redirected.\n\n---\n\nRedirecting…\nRedirecting…\nClick here if you are not redirected.\n\n---\n\nCV - Ke Wang - Homepage\nKe Wang\nSoftware Engineer at Meta, working on open source database query engine Presto and Velox\nFollow\nSunnyvale, CA\nMeta\nEmail\nGoogle Scholar\nGitHub\nLinkedIn\nCV\nEducation\nYale University\n, M.S. in Computer Science, 2021-2022\nPeking University\n, B.S. in Computer Science with minor in Economics, 2016-2020\nWork experience\nMeta\n, Software Engineer (Feb 2025 - Present)\nWorking on open source database query engine Presto and Velox\nAlipayUS\n, Software Engineer (Feb 2023 - Feb 2025)\nWorking on next-generation payment systems\nShanghai Qi Zhi Institute\n, Researcher (Aug 2020 - Aug 2021)\nHigh performance system group, working on database index on persistent memory\nBytedance\n, Software Engineer Intern (June 2020 - Aug 2020)\nDistributed storage group\nBytedance\n, Software Engineer Intern (March 2019 - July 2019)\nA starting project, later incubated to Xingfuli.\nSkills\nDatabase Systems (Expert)\nPresto, Velox, Index Structures, Persistent Memory\nHigh Performance Computing (Advanced)\nData Stream Processing, Sliding Windows, Sketch Algorithms\nProgramming Languages (Advanced)\nC++, Java, Python\nLanguages\nEnglish (Fluent)\nMandarin (Native)\nPublications\nNewton sketches: Estimating Node Intimacy in Dynamic Graphs Using Newton’s Law of Cooling\nChen, Q., Wang, K., Li, A., Wu, Y., Yang, T., & Cui, B. (2024). Newton sketches: Estimating Node Intimacy in Dynamic Graphs Using Newton's Law of Cooling.\nIEEE ICDE 2024\n.\nWhen Tree Meets Hash: Reducing Random Reads for Index Structures on Persistent Memories\nWang, K., Yang, G., Li, Y., Zhang, H., & Gao, M. (2023). When Tree Meets Hash: Reducing Random Reads for Index Structures on Persistent Memories.\nACM SIGMOD 2023\n.\nA Sketch Framework for Approximate Data Stream Processing in Sliding Windows\nGou, X., Zhang, Y., Hu, Z., He, L., Wang, K., Liu, X., Yang, T., Wang, Y., & Cui, B. (2022). A Sketch Framework for Approximate Data Stream Processing in Sliding Windows.\nIEEE TKDE 2022\n.\nRuntime Recovery of Web Applications under Zero-day ReDoS Attacks\nBai, Z., Wang, K., Zhu, H., Cao, Y., & Jin, X. (2021). Runtime Recovery of Web Applications under Zero-day ReDoS Attacks.\nIEEE Security&Privacy 2021\n.\nSliding Sketches: A Framework using Time Zones for Data Stream Processing in Sliding Windows\nGou, X., He, L., Zhang, Y., Wang, K., Liu, X., Yang, T., Wang, Y., & Cui, B. (2020). Sliding Sketches: A Framework using Time Zones for Data Stream Processing in Sliding Windows.\nSIGKDD 2020\n.\nInterests\nOutdoor Activities: Snowboarding, Basketball, Traveling\nIndoor Activities: Billiard, Singing"
  },
