    "homepage_text": "Xinyan Chen – East Asian Languages & Cultural Studies\nSkip to content\nXinyan Chen\nArea:\nEarly Medieval Chinese Literature\nEmail:\nxchen24@ucsb.edu\nAbout:\nXinyan Chen’s research interests center on early medieval Chinese literature, especially\nYuefu\n(Music Bureau) poetry from the late Han to the Sui dynasties. She is also interested in Six Dynasties literary culture, including the writings and intellectual legacy of the Seven Sages of the Bamboo Grove.\nSite Login\nDepartment of East Asian Languages & Cultural Studies\nHSSB 4001\nUniversity of California,\nSanta Barbara,\nCA 93106-7075"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Yuecong Xu::https://xuyu0010.wixsite.com/xuyu0010": {
    "homepage": "https://xuyu0010.wixsite.com/xuyu0010",
    "all_emails": [
      "yc.xu@nus.edu.sg"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/Lexiang-Xiong",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/xuyu0014/",
    "homepage_text": "Xu Yuecong's Homepage\ntop of page\nThis website was built on Wix. Create yours today.\nGet Started\nHomepage of Xu Yuecong\nXu Yuecong, PhD\nLecturer @ ECE Department, College of Design and Engineering, NUS, Singapore\nStaff Supervisor @ NUS Calibur, College of Design and Engineering, NUS, Singapore\n​\n​\nyc.xu at nus.edu.sg\nI am currently a Lecturer at the ECE department of the College of Design and Engineering (CDE), National University of Singapore (NUS). My current courses cover Computer Vision and Machine (Deep) Learning.\nI am currently also the Staff Supervisor for NUS Calibur Robotics (NUS Calibur), the only RoboMaster Team remaining for Singapore and South East Asia.\nI obtained my Ph. D. Degree from Nanyang Technological University, Singapore, under the supervision of\nProf. Mao Kezhi\nin 2021. My research focuses on video analytics and time-series analytics, with a special interest in applying transfer learning towards human action recognition as well as AIOT tasks. Previously, I obtained my Bachelor's Degree from NTU Singapore.\nI was a Research Scientist at the\nInstitute for Infocomm Research (I2R)\n, Agency for Science, Technology, and Research (A*STAR) Singapore, from July 2021 to January 2024.\nI was also a Part-Time Lecturer at the School of EEE, NTU Singapore from July 2021 to January 2024. My courses covered Computer Vision and Machine Learning.\nRecent News\n&\nUpdates\n​[2025 Season for RoboMaster] Updates for\nNUS Calibur Robotics\nin the 2025/2026 Season will be updated in this post. This will be my LAST Season after 10 years in the RoboMaster competition.\n​\n[2025/11] Our paper on memory-efficient and low-latency minute-long-video generation (\nDualParal\n)\nhas been accepted by\nAAAI 2026!\nThe ArXiv version is available [\nHere\n]. See you all in\nSingapore\nin January!\n[2025/06] Our paper on unlearning (concept erasure) in diffusion models  (\nSemantic Surgery\n)\nhas been accepted by\nNeurIPS 2025!\nThe ArXiv version is available [\nHere\n].\n[2025/06]\nNUS Calibur Robotics\nwon one First-place and one Second-place at\nRM-North America (RMUL). Congrats to the Team!\n[2025/06] Our paper on robust UDA for perturbed target point cloud (\nQCMB\n)\nhas been accepted by\nIROS 2025!\nThe ArXiv version is available [\nHere\n].\n[2024/09] I am officially appointed as the\nStaff Supervisor\nfor\nNUS Calibur Robotics\n(Team).\n[2024/08]\nOur paper on UDA for multivariate time-series (SEA++) has been accepted by\nIEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence (TPAMI, IF 20.8)!\nThe IEEXplore version is available [\nHere\n].\n[2024/07]\nOur survey on\nvideo unsupervised domain adaptation (VUDA)\nhas been accepted by ACM Computing Surveys (ACM CSUR, IF 28.0).\nThe paper is available with\nOpen Access\n.\nClick [\nHere\n] for details. The corresponding GitHub repository is also available. Click [\nHere\n] for more information.\n[2024/07]\nTwo papers on Multi-modal test-time adaptation (\nLatte\n) and Diffusion-based pose estimation (\nmmDiff\n) have been accepted by\nECCV 2024!\n​\n[2024/01] I am appointed as the Young Associate Editor for the\nJournal of Automation and Intelligence\n.\n[2024/01] I am joining\nNUS\nas a\nlecturer (Educator Track)\nwith the\nDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE)\n, College of Design and Engineering (CDE).\nResearch Interests\nMy current research mainly focuses on\nPerception and its Application in Robotics\n, with a special interest in\nVision-based and RF-based perception\n, where state-of-the-art AI models are proposed to analyze human actions and surroundings for robotic applications including but not limited to human-robot interaction, autonomous control and navigation, and security.\nMore specifically, I focus my study on how AI models are efficiently deployed in resource-restrained and environment-varying conditions (including adverse environment conditions) by applying the various learning techniques, such as transfer learning, continual learning, and few-shot learning. To achieve the above goals, I and my team are currently focusing on the following topics:\nHuman Action Recognition via On-board Non-Visual Perception.\n​\nRobust Policy Learning via Digital Cousin Creation and Sim2Real Transfer.\n​\nRobust Video Analysis\nAcross Differed Environments\nwith Less Labeled Data\n.\n​\nRobust 3D Semantic Segmentation Across Continuously Changing Scenes via Online (Test-time) Adaptation\n.\n​\n3D Reconstructing of Extremely Adverse Environments\n.\nContact\nbottom of page"
  },
