    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shang-wen-daniel-li-0109b579/",
    "homepage_text": "Daniel Li personal website\nShang-Wen (Daniel) Li\nResearch Manager\nFacebook AI (FAIR)\nAbout\nHi I'm Shang-Wen (Daniel) Li\n. I am a researcher focusing on\nlarge foundation models, multimodal, speech and language understanding\n. I earned my PhD from MIT CSAIL in 2016, supervised by\nProf. Victor Zue\n. I joined Amazon AWS, Amazon Alexa and Apple Siri since my graduation, and now I work at Facebook AI (FAIR) as a Research Manager. I am always looking for talented interns and team members passionate about deep learning, algorithms, and coding.\nNews\nGiving tutorial at ICASSP, NAACL, INTERSPEECH 2022\nMay, July, September 2022\nSelf-supervised Representation Learning for Speech Processing, with Abdelrahman Mohamed, Hung-yi Lee, Shinji Watanabe, Tara Sainath, Karen Livescu, Shu-wen Yang and Katrin Kirchhoff\nHosting workshop at AAAI 2022\nFeb 2022\nThe 2nd Self-supervised Learning for Audio and Speech Processing\nCo-organizing SUPERB benchmark and challenge\nAug 2021\nSpeech processing Universal PERformance Benchmark\nHosting workshop at ACL 2021\nAug 2021\nMeta learning and its application for Natural Language Understanding\nGiving tutorial at ACL 2021\nAug 2021\nMeta Learning and Its Applications to Natural Language Processing\nJoining Facebook AI (FAIR) as a Research Manager\nJune 2021\nGiving tutorial at ICASSP 2021\nJune 2021\nMeta learning and its application for human language processing\nHosting workshop at NeurIPS 2020\nDec 2020\nSelf-Supervised Learning for Speech and Audio Processing\nGiving tutorial at INTERSPEECH 2020\nOct 2020\nMeta learning and its application to human language processing\nJoining Amazon AWS as an Applied Scientist Manager\nJun 2019\nJoining Apple Siri as a Machine Learning Scientist\nJun 2018\nJoining Amazon Alexa as an Applied Scientist\nJan 2017\nEarning PhD degree from MIT CSAIL\nDec 2016\nEstablishing Cockatoo AI as Co-Founder and CTO\nJan 2015\nWorkshops\nAAAI 2022\nThe 2nd Self-supervised Learning for Audio and Speech Processing\nACL 2021\nMeta learning and its application for Natural Language Understanding\nNeurIPS 2020\nSelf-Supervised Learning for Speech and Audio Processing\nTalks\nTutorial\nSelf-supervised Representation Learning for Speech Processing at ICASSP, NAACL, INTERSPEECH 2022\n[website]\nTutorial\nMeta Learning and Its Applications to Natural Language Processing at ACL 2021\n[slides]\n[video]\nTutorial\nMeta learning and its application for human language processing at ICASSP 2021\nTutorial\nMeta learning and its application to human language processing at INTERSPEECH 2020\nPublications\nUpdated infrequently. Full list can be found at\ngoogle scholar\n.\nShuyang Li, Jin Cao, Mukund Sridhar, Henghui Zhu,\nShang-Wen Li\n, Wael Hamza, and Julian McAuley, Zero-shot Generalization in Dialog State Tracking through Generative Question Answering, in EACL, 2021.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nPo-Han Chi, Pei-Hung Chung, Tsung-Han Wu, Chun-Cheng Hsieh,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and Hung-yi Lee, Audio ALBERT: A Lite BERT for Self-supervised Learning of Audio Representation, in SLT, 2021.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\n, Jason Krone, Shuyan Dong, Yi Zhang, and Yaser Al-onaizan, Meta learning to classify intent and slot labels with noisy few shot examples, in SLT, 2021.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nCheng-I Lai, Jin Cao, Sravan Bodapati, and\nShang-Wen Li\n, Towards Semi-Supervised Semantics Understanding from Speech, in Self-Supervised Learning for Speech and Audio Processing workshop at NeurIPS, 2020.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nCheng-I Lai, Yung-Sung Chuang, Hung-Yi Lee,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and James Glass, Semi-Supervised Spoken Language Understanding via Self-Supervised Speech and Language Model Pretraining, in arXiv preprint arXiv:2010.13826, 2020.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nHongyin Luo,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and James Glass, Knowledge Grounded Conversational Symptom Detection with Graph Memory Networks, in Proceedings of the 3rd Clinical Natural Language Processing Workshop at EMNLP, 2020.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nJin Cao, Jun Wang, Wael Hamza, Kelly Vanee, and\nShang-Wen Li\n, Style Attuned Pre-training and Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning for Spoken Language Understanding, in INTERSPEECH 2020.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nHongyin Luo,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and James Glass, Prototypical Q Networks for Automatic Conversational Diagnosis and Few-Shot New Disease Adaption, in INTERSPEECH 2020.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nAndy T Liu,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and Hung-yi Lee, Tera: Self-supervised learning of transformer encoder representation for speech, in arXiv preprint arXiv:2007.06028, 2020.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nMaryam Fazel-Zarandi,\nShang-Wen Li\n, Jin Cao, Jared Casale, Peter Henderson, David Whitney, and Alborz Geramifard, Learning robust dialog policies in noisy environments, in Workshop on Conversational AI Today’s Practice Tomorrow’s Potential, NeurIPS, 2017.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\n, Improving learning experience in MOOCs with educational content linking, MIT PhD dissertation.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nChengjie Sun,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and Lei Lin, Thread structure prediction for MOOC discussion forum, in ICYCSEE 2016.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nXiangrong Zhang, Chen Li,\nShang-Wen Li\n, Victor Zue, Automated segmentation of MOOC lectures towards customized learning, in ICALT 2016.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\n, and Victor Zue, Linking MOOC courseware to accommodate diverse learner backgrounds, in SLaTE 2015.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\nand Victor Zue, Would linked MOOC courseware enhance information search?, in ICALT 2015.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\nand Piotr Mitros, Learnersourced recommendations for remediation, in ICALT 2015.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nSheng-syun Shen, Hung-yi Lee,\nShang-Wen Li\n, Victor Zue, and Lin-shan Lee, Structuring lectures in massive open online courses (moocs) for efficient learning by linking similar sections and predicting prerequisites, in INTERSPEECH 2015.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nJuho Kim, Philip J Guo, Carrie J Cai,\nShang-Wen Li\n, Krzysztof Z Gajos, and Robert C Miller, Data-driven interaction techniques for improving navigation of educational videos, in UIST 2014.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nJuho Kim,\nShang-Wen Li\n, Carrie J Cai, Krzysztof Z Gajos, and Robert C Miller, Leveraging video interaction data and content analysis to improve video learning, in CHI Workshop on Learning Innovations at Scale, 2014.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nYow-bang Wang,\nShang-Wen Li\n, and Lin-shan Lee, An experimental analysis on integrating multi-stream spectro-temporal, cepstral and pitch information for mandarin speech recognition, in IEEE transactions on audio, speech, and language processing 2013.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\n, , Liang-che Sun, and Lin-shan Lee, Improved tonal language speech recognition by integrating spectro-temporal evidence and pitch information with properly chosen tonal acoustic units, in INTERSPEECH 2011.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\n, Liang-che Sun, and Lin-shan Lee, Multi-stream spectro-temporal and cepstral features based on data-driven hierarchical phoneme clusters, in ICASSP 2011.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nShang-Wen Li\n, Liang-che Sun, and Lin-shan Lee, Improved phoneme recognition by integrating evidence from spectro-temporal and cepstral features, in INTERSPEECH 2010.\n[paper]\n[BibTeX]\nGet in Touch\nContact\nshangwel [at] meta [dot] com\n1 Hacker Way, Menlo Park, CA, 94025 US"
