    "homepage_text": "Home | Junxiao Wang\nJunxiao Wang\nHome\nCV\nResearch\nPublications\nTalks\nProjects\nTeaching\nCourses\nStudents\nJunxiao (Cody) Wang\nAssociate Professor\nHuman-Centered Artificial Intelligence (HCAI) Lab\nCyberspace Institute of Advanced Technology (CIAT)\nHuangpu Campus, Guangzhou University (GZHU)\nGuangzhou (Canton), Guangdong, China\nWork Email:\nPersonal Email:\nFind me at\nWhat I'm Doing\nTL;DR\na human-AI co-evolutionary paradigm, ensuring increasingly powerful AI systems align with humanity's perception.\nMoving beyond the limits of symbols and semantics to pioneer a new frontier in Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) and AI. The goal is to create a direct perception loop between humans and AI, enabling AI to sense human physiology, intentions, and creative sparks. This paradigm shift moves us from \"external command alignment\" to true \"internal experience integration\", unlocking unprecedented forms of collaboration.\nTL;DR\na distributed ML paradigm on low-Earth orbit (LEO) satellite constellation.\nDistributed ML on LEO constellations tackles the core bottleneck of limited downlink capacity by processing data at the edge. This shifts the paradigm from transmitting raw data to exchanging efficient model updates, enabling real-time collaborative learning across satellites. It reduces communication overhead and unlocks timely insights for critical applications like Earth observation, paving the way for autonomous and scalable space-based intelligence.\nIf you're interested in a research internship (remote), I welcome applicants of all levels (PhD/MSc/BSc). Don't hesitate to send me an email!\nRecent Activities\nReviewer, IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) 2026\nReviewer, International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) 2026\nReviewer, International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS) 2026\nReviewer, The Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS) 2025\nPC Member, European Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ECAI) 2025\nReviewer, International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV) 2025\nPC Member, International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI) 2025\nReviewer, IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) 2025\nReviewer, International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS) 2025\nAssociate Professor, Guangzhou University (GZHU), at Cyberspace Institute of Advanced Technology\nReviewer, International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI) 2024\nReviewer, European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV) 2024\nReviewer, International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS) 2024\nReviewer, International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV) 2023\nPostdoctoral Fellow, working with Assistant Prof. Di Wang, at Division of Computer, Electrical and Mathematical Sciences and Engineering (CEMSE), King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST)\nInvited Talk, Ritsumeikan University and CCF Dalian International Seminar\nPostdoctoral Fellow, working with IEEE Fellow, Prof. Song Guo, at Department of Computing (COMP), The Hong Kong Polytechnic University (PolyU)\nVisitor, working with Prof. Steve Uhlig, at School of Electronic Engineering and Computer Science (EECS), Queen Mary University of London (QMUL)\nPhD Candidate, supervised by IEEE Fellow, Prof. Keqiu Li, at School of Computer Science and Technology (CS), Dalian University of Technology (DUT)\nNews\nFeb 2026   Our paper entitled \"FedCART: Tackling Long-Tailed Distributions in Federated Adversarial Training via Classifier Refinement\" was accepted by CVPR2026.\nSep 2025   Our paper entitled \"Private Training Large-scale Models with Efficient DP-SGD\" was accepted by NeurIPS2025.\nSep 2025   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for CVPR2026.\nSep 2025   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for ICLR2026.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"On the Evolution of Federated Post-Training Large Language Models: A Model Accessibility View\" was available in arxiv.org.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"Enhancing Inductive Knowledge Graph Completion with Contextual Relation Topology Learning\" was accepted by Knowledge-Based Systems.\nAug 2025   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for AISTATS2026.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"Conversational Education at Scale: A Multi-LLM Agent Workflow for Procedural Learning and Pedagogic Quality Assessment\" was accepted by EMNLP2025.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"Federated Learning on Heterogeneous and Long-Tailed Data via Disentangled Representation\" was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"Trustworthy AI Psychotherapy: Multi-Agent LLM Workflow for Counseling and Explainable Mental Disorder Diagnosis\" was available in arxiv.org.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"A Survey on Zeroth-Order Optimization for Machine Learning\" was accepted by WISA2025.\nAug 2025   Our paper entitled \"Trustworthy AI Psychotherapy: Multi-Agent LLM Workflow for Counseling and Explainable Mental Disorder Diagnosis\" was accepted by CIKM2025.\nJul 2025   Our paper entitled \"Fine-Grained Vision-Language Modeling for Multimodal Training Assistants in Augmented Reality\" was available in arxiv.org.\nJul 2025   Our paper entitled \"Conversational Education at Scale: A Multi-LLM Agent Workflow for Procedural Learning and Pedagogic Quality Assessment\" was available in arxiv.org.\nJul 2025   Our paper entitled \"FlashDP: Private Training Large Language Models with Efficient DP-SGD\" was available in arxiv.org.\nJul 2025   Our paper entitled \"Scalable Zeroth-Order Fine-Tuning for Extremely Large Language Models with Limited GPU Memory\" was accepted by COLM2025.\nMay 2025   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for NeurIPS2025.\nApr 2025   I was invited to serve as a PC Member for ECAI2025.\nMar 2025   Our paper entitled \"ZO2: Scalable Zeroth-Order Fine-Tuning for Extremely Large Language Models with Limited GPU Memory\" was available in arxiv.org.\nFeb 2025   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for ICCV2025.\nJan 2025   Our paper entitled \"Federated learning with complete service commitment of data heterogeneity\" was accepted by Knowledge-Based Systems.\nDec 2024   Our paper entitled \"A Survey on Federated Long-Tailed Learning\" was accepted by Chinese Journal of Computers.\nDec 2024   Our paper entitled \"Enhancing Inductive Knowledge Graph Completion with Contextual Relation Topology Learning\" was available in SSRN.\nNov 2024   I was invited to serve as a PC Member for IJCAI2025.\nNov 2024   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for CVPR2025.\nOct 2024   Our paper entitled \"ZO-Offloading: Fine-Tuning LLMs with 100 Billion\nParameters on a Single GPU\" was accepted by AFM Workshop of NeurIPS2024.\nOct 2024   Our paper entitled \"FlashDP: Memory-Efficient and High-Throughput\nDP-SGD Training for Large Language Models\" was accepted by AFM Workshop of NeurIPS2024.\nSep 2024   Our paper entitled \"Towards Safe Concept Transfer of Multi-Modal\nDiffusion via Causal Representation Editing\" was accepted by NeurIPS2024.\nSep 2024   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for AISTATS2025.\nJul 2024   Our paper entitled \"Explore and Cure: Unveiling Sample Effectiveness with Context-Aware Federated Prompt Tuning\" was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing.\nMay 2024   Our paper entitled \"Autonomous Workflow for Multimodal Fine-Grained Training Assistants Towards Mixed Reality\" was accepted by ACL2024.\nMay 2024   Our paper entitled \"Autonomous Workflow for Multimodal Fine-Grained Training Assistants Towards Mixed Reality\" was available in arxiv.org.\nMay 2024   I started my research at Guangzhou University as an Associate Professor.\nApr 2024   Our paper entitled \"Exploring Amplified Heterogeneity Arising from Heavy-Tailed Distributions in Federated Learning\" was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing.\nApr 2024   Our paper entitled \"Towards Light Adaptation of Large Language Models For Personal Hardware\" was accepted by EdgeFM Workshop of MobiSys2024.\nApr 2024   Our paper entitled \"Rethinking Personalized Client Collaboration in Federated Learning\" was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing.\nMar 2024   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for IJCAI2024.\nJan 2024   Our paper entitled \"Faithful Vision-Language Interpretation via Concept Bottleneck Models\" was accepted by ICLR2024.\nJan 2024   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for ECCV2024.\nSep 2023   Our paper entitled \"Towards Test-Time Refusals via Concept Negation\" was accepted by NeurIPS2023.\nSep 2023   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for AISTATS2024.\nAug 2023   Our paper entitled \"PromptFL: Let Federated Participants Cooperatively Learn Prompts Instead of Models — Federated Learning in Age of Foundation Model\" was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing.\nJun 2023   Our paper entitled \"On Knowledge Editing in Federated Learning: Perspectives, Challenges, and Future Directions\" was available in arxiv.org.\nMay 2023   Our paper entitled \"Investigating Trojan Attacks on Pre-trained Language Model-powered Database Middleware\" was accepted by KDD2023.\nMay 2023   I was invited to serve as a Reviewer for ICCV2023.\nMar 2023   Our paper entitled \"DualMix: Unleashing the Potential of Data Augmentation for Online Class-Incremental Learning\" was available in arxiv.org.\nFeb 2023   Our paper entitled \"PMR: Prototypical Modal Rebalance for Multimodal Learning\" was accepted by CVPR2023.\nFeb 2023   I started my research at King Abdullah University of Science and Technology as a Postdoctoral Fellow.\nJan 2023   Our paper entitled \"pFedPrompt: Learning Personalized Prompt for Vision-Language Models in Federated Learning\" was accepted by WWW2023.\nNov 2022   Our paper entitled \"Demystify Self-Attention in Vision Transformers from a Semantic Perspective: Analysis and Application\" was available in arxiv.org.\nNov 2022   Our paper entitled \"PMR: Prototypical Modal Rebalance for Multimodal Learning\" was available in arxiv.org.\nNov 2022   Our paper entitled \"FedTune: A Deep Dive into Efficient Federated Fine-Tuning with Pre-trained Transformers\" was available in arxiv.org.\nAug 2022   Our paper entitled \"PromptFL: Let Federated Participants Cooperatively Learn Prompts Instead of Models — Federated Learning in Age of Foundation Model\" was available in arxiv.org.\nAug 2022   Our paper entitled \"Federated Unlearning: Guarantee the Right of Clients to Forget\" was accepted by IEEE Network.\nApr 2022   Our paper entitled \"Efficient Integrity Authentication Scheme for Large-scale RFID Systems\" was accepted by IEEE Transactions on Mobile Computing.\nApr 2022   Our paper entitled \"A Survey on Gradient Inversion: Attacks, Defenses and Future Directions\" was accepted by IJCAI2022.\nMar 2022   I was invited to give a talk on Ritsumeikan University & CCF Dalian International Seminar.\nFeb 2022   Our paper entitled \"Vertical Machine Unlearning: Selectively Removing Sensitive Information From Latent Feature Space\" was available in arxiv.org.\nJan 2022   Our paper entitled \"Federated Unlearning via Class-Discriminative Pruning\" was accepted by WWW2022.\nDec 2021   Our paper entitled \"Protect Privacy from Gradient Leakage Attack in Federated Learning\" was accepted by INFOCOM2022.\nOct 2021   Our paper entitled \"Federated Unlearning via Class-Discriminative Pruning\" was available in arxiv.org.\nMar 2021   I started my research at Hong Kong Polytechnic University as a Postdoctoral Fellow.\nFeb 2020   Our paper entitled \"Click-UP: Toward the Software Upgrade of Click-Based Modular Network Function\" was accepted by IEEE Systems Journal.\nSep 2018   I started my research at Queen Mary University of London as a Visitor.\nJun 2018   Our paper entitled \"CLICK-UP: Towards Software Upgrades of Click-driven Stateful Network Elements\" was accepted by Demo track of SIGCOMM2018.\nSep 2016   I started my research at Dalian University of Technology as a PhD Candidate.\nCopyright © 2017-now\nJunxiao Wang"
