    "homepage_text": "Dennis Wei\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nDennis Wei\nHome\nResearch Staff Member\nTrusted AI Department\nIBM Research AI\nThomas J. Watson Research Center\n1101 Kitchawan Road, Yorktown Heights, NY 10598\nEmail\n:  dwei [at] us [dot] ibm [dot] com\ndennislwei [at] gmail [dot] com\nMy research interests lie broadly in machine learning, signal processing, statistics, and optimization. Current interests center around trustworthy machine learning, including interpretability of machine learning models, algorithmic fairness, causal inference and graphical models, and\ndata science for social good\n. Past areas include health insurance, adaptive sampling, and sparse filter design.\nI was previously a post-doctoral research fellow in the\nEECS Department\nat the\nUniversity of Michigan\n, working with\nProf. Alfred Hero\n.  I received my Ph.D. in electrical engineering from the\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\n, where I was advised by\nProf. Alan Oppenheim\nin the\nDigital Signal Processing Group\n.\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Lei Guo::https://www.myresearch.top/ggroup/index.php/team/": {
    "homepage": "https://www.myresearch.top/ggroup/index.php/team/",
    "all_emails": [],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Team – Welcome to the Guo Lab\nToggle navigation\nSkip to content\nFollow us\ntwitter\ninstagram\nSearch for:\nToggle sidebar & navigation\nTeam\nMembers\nDr. Lei Guo, Principal Investigator\nDr. Lei Guo received her B.S. in chemistry from Lanzhou University, China, where she worked in Prof. Wei Wang’s lab. Lei pursued her Ph.D. in chemistry with Prof. Omar M. Yaghi at UC Berkeley and explored the field of reticular chemistry. Her Ph.D. work focused on the development of new synthetic and modification strategies for covalent organic frameworks (COFs). As her interests began to shift to the applications of these highly porous crystalline nanomaterials, Lei conducted her postdoctoral training under the supervision of Prof. Michael R. Hoffmann at Caltech, working on wastewater treatment.\nIn August 2022, Lei joined the Department of Civil Engineering at the University of Arkansas, Fayetteville, as an assistant professor.\nOutside lab, Lei loves cooking and enjoys outdoor activities and family time.\nAnne Lobitz (Annie), Ph.D. Candidate\nAnnie\nis a Ph.D. student studying civil and environmental engineering. She received her B.S. in Environmental Engineering from Louisiana State University in May 2023.\nAnnie\nis interested in all thing’s wastewater treatment, specifically emerging pollutants that lack treatment standards. She is very excited to be able to work on this with Dr. Guo’s lab group for the upcoming years!\nMinshu Chen, Ph.D. Candidate\nMinshu Chen is a Ph.D student studying Environmental Engineering. He received his\nB.S. from Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology (NUIST) in Mathematics and\nApplied Mathematics in 2022. He also earned his M.E. from Rice University in Chemical\nEngineering in 2024. Minshu joined the Guo Lab in August of 2024. He is interested in novel\nmethods for water treatment& energy recycling.\nAlex Steuber (AJ)\n, Undergraduate Student\nMy name is AJ am from central Arkansas and I attend the University of Arkansas Majoring in chemistry minoring in mathematics. Outside of class I enjoy spending time with my family and playing video games.\nMadeline Crisp\n, Undergraduate Student\nMadeline is an undergraduate student on the pre-med track majoring in biochemistry and minoring in Spanish.\nOutside of class she enjoys hiking, baking, and learning new languages.\nLauryn Peters, Undergraduate Student\nMy name is Lauryn Peters, and I am a junior undergraduate honors student. I am currently working on the modification of resins for use in flow-through water treatment systems as an extension of previous research conducted in this lab. This project builds on established work while exploring practical applications for improving water treatment efficiency.\nI am particularly interested in chemistry and how it relates to medicine, and I am excited by the ways chemical research can be applied to improve human health. Through my work in this lab, I hope to become more comfortable in a laboratory setting and gain experience with a variety of experimental techniques that will be valuable for my future career in medicine.\nTyler Maddox, Undergraduate Student\nI am Tyler Maddox, a Senior majoring in Chemistry & Biochemistry (B.S.) and Biology (B.A.) on the pre-medicine track. I am from Blythewood, SC, by way of Midlands Technical College. Outside of school, I love playing basketball, playing chess, and watching Arkansas Razorback games. I’m also a huge foodie—whether I’m cooking for my friends or trying new restaurants with them.\nBeyond food and school, I’ve worked as a Pharmacy Technician for almost three years, and I’m the youngest of eight children (five brothers and two sisters). I have a curiosity that tends to lead me down YouTube rabbit holes, usually about chess, chemistry, physics, math, biology/biochemistry, or impressive engineering feats.\nJoin the Guo Lab!\nLet’s play with molecular “LEGO”s, build materials, and address some environmental challenges!\nWe are looking for motivated graduate and undergraduate students to participate in highly interdisciplinary projects! Talk to Lei to learn more!\nAlumni\nBrianna Harris\nCody Wendland\nEmily Shea\nJason Thornhill\nLaurel Matinchev\nLauren Launhardt\nSamantha Garcia Marquez\nXinyue Hai\nGraduate Student\nUndergraduate Student\nUndergraduate Student\nUndergraduate Student\nUndergraduate Student\nUndergraduate Student\nUndergraduate Student\nGraduate Student\nInstagram\n…\n\n---\n\nContact – Welcome to the Guo Lab\nToggle navigation\nSkip to content\nFollow us\ntwitter\ninstagram\nSearch for:\nToggle sidebar & navigation\nContact\nGet in touch with us!\nWe are always open to new projects, creative ideas or collaboration opportunities. Feel free to get in touch with us!\nTogether, let’s build sustainability.\nDr. Lei Guo\n4160 Bell Engineering Center\nUniversity of Arkansas, Fayetteville\nFayetteville, AR 72701\nEmail: leiguo[at]uark[.]edu\nPhone: 1-479-575-2456\nInstagram\n…\n\n---\n\nContact – Welcome to the Guo Lab\nToggle navigation\nSkip to content\nFollow us\ntwitter\ninstagram\nSearch for:\nToggle sidebar & navigation\nContact\nGet in touch with us!\nWe are always open to new projects, creative ideas or collaboration opportunities. Feel free to get in touch with us!\nTogether, let’s build sustainability.\nDr. Lei Guo\n4160 Bell Engineering Center\nUniversity of Arkansas, Fayetteville\nFayetteville, AR 72701\nEmail: leiguo[at]uark[.]edu\nPhone: 1-479-575-2456\nInstagram\n…"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Qi Wu::https://wilsoncernwq.github.io/": {
