  "deep_homepage::Yifan Chen::https://yifanc96.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://yifanc96.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "yifanchen@math.ucla.edu"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Yifan Chen\nApplied Mathematics\nOffice: MS 7304\nEmail: yifanchen@math.ucla.edu\nProgramming:\nJulia, Python, MATLAB\nI am an Assistant Professor in\nUCLA Mathematics\nand affiliated with\nUCLA DataX\n. I was previously a Courant Instructor at the Courant Institute, New York University during 2023-2025. I received my Ph.D. from Caltech working with Profs.\nThomas Y. Hou\n,\nHouman Owhadi\nand\nAndrew M. Stuart\n. I obtained my B.S. in Pure and Applied Mathematics at Tsinghua University.\nMy research lies at the intersection of applied and computational mathematics, applied probability, and machine learning. I am motivated by mathematical and computational problems arising from scientific computing and data science applications that are high-dimensional, ill-conditioned, or involve heterogeneous and multiscale phenomena. I enjoy leveraging and developing stochastic methodologies for understanding and addressing these questions, such as in Monte Carlo, Bayesian inference, PDEs, and numerical linear algebra.\nMy recent work centers on analysis and design of efficient stochastic algorithms for dynamical sampling and generative modeling, driven by applications in uncertainty quantification and generative AI. Check out my\nResearch Page\nand\nGoogle Scholar\nfor more details. Feel free to reach out by email if you share similar interests!\nTopics sketch\nHigh dimensional sampling\nI work on understanding how high-dimensional sampling of probability distributions can be made feasible and developing efficient algorithms for applications in scientific computing and inverse problems.\nError/Bias in high dimensions:\nMotivated by molecular dynamics applications, we show that unadjusted Langevin algorithms can exhibit a phenomenon \"delocalization of bias\", which leads to a\nnearly dimension-independent bias scaling\nwhen the quantity of interest depends on few variables, under certain assumptions. In contrast, unbiased samplers must scale with dimension via power laws.\nImproved dimensional scaling for unbiased ensemble samplers:\nMy work introduces new derivative-free and derivative-based ensemble samplers with affine invariance that improve the popular\nemcee\npackage, especially in high dimensions. The new\naffine-invariant ensemble HMC\nachieves state-of-the-art power law scaling regarding dimension as single-chain counterparts while achieving affine invariance based on complementary ensembles and being embarrassingly parallel.\nGradient flows and variational inference:\nVariational, approximate approaches are often promising in high-dimensional applications compared to unbiased approaches. Our work builds a\ndesign framework for gradient flows in sampling\n, which clarifies the choices of energy functionals, metrics, and numerics. We focus on Fisher-Rao gradient flows as the promising diffeomorphism-invariant approach and establish\nfunctional inequalities in Fisher-Rao geometry\n. Parametric approximation of gradient flows connects to natural gradients in variational inference; see also\nWasserstein natural gradients\nand a connection to\nsequential-in-time neural Galerkin schemes\n. We develop a\nKalman-filter-type Gaussian mixture approximation\nof Fisher-Rao natural gradients for efficient, multimodal, derivative-free sampling in large-scale Bayesian inverse problems; see recent further improvement through\nspecialized high-dimensional quadrature\nand\nblack-box Monte Carlo\n, with an emphasis on stability and affine invariance. See also\nmean field variational inference with rotations\nand efficient normalizing flows.\nGenerative modeling and probabilistic inference\nGenerative AI has become a powerful tool for handling high-dimensional data. I work on understanding the mathematical foundations of generative modeling and its usage for probabilistic inference with applications in scientific domains.\nProbabilistic forecasting with generative modeling:\nWe develop probabilistic forecasting methods using\ngenerative diffusion from a point source\nwith applications in fluid and video forecasting. We prove that Föllmer's process—an entropy-minimizing dynamic connecting Dirac measures to target distributions and specific solutions to Schrödinger's bridges—minimizes a statistical estimation error (namely KL divergence on path space) between true and generated samples. This leads to a new variational characterization of the process in addition to the classical one offered by stochastic control; see the\ncompanion mathematics paper\n.\nNumerical designs of generative dynamics for scientific computing:\nWe study\nprincipled design of noise and interpolation schedules\nfor generative modeling of multiscale scientific data which are numerically ill-conditioned and have a wide range of Fourier spectrum, focusing on precise fine scale accuracy instead of coarse scale stability as in many function space generative models. For the design of interpolation schedules, we propose\noptimal averaged Lipschitzness\nas an alternative to optimal kinetic energy in optimal transport, which achieves much better dynamical numerical efficiency for ill-conditioned distributions.\nPosterior sampling with diffusion priors:\nI collaborate with scientists on developing rigorous probabilistic imaging methods (see\nPnP-MC\n,\nPnP-DM\n,\nSGDD\n) by integrating generative diffusion priors with efficient posterior sampling algorithms, with applications in black hole imaging. See also\ncrystal structure generation\n.\nStatistical numerical methods\nRecent machine learning approaches for PDEs and inverse problems often treat PDEs as data that describe the relationships between pointwise function evaluations and their derivatives at specific collocation points. By fitting all the data using neural network function approximators, these techniques lead to automatic machine learning-based solvers.\nCompared to neural networks, the statistical framework based on Gaussian processes (GPs) and its deterministic counterpart kernel and RKHS methods offer more interpretable and theoretically grounded function approximators, with deep connections to meshless collocation methods and built-in uncertainty quantification. I contribute to a pipeline of work on the analysis and efficient algorithms for GPs in PDEs and inverse problems.\nSolving nonlinear PDEs and inverse problems with GPs:\nWe present a rigorous, efficient, and reliable\nframework\nwith\nerror estimates\nand\nuncertainty quantification and active sampling of data points\n. Applications include various 2D nonlinear PDEs (semilinear; fully nonlinear; elliptic, parabolic and hyperbolic) and high dimensional HJB equations.\nHierarchical parameter learning:\nOur work proves the\nlarge data limit for hierarchical parameter learning\nof covariance kernels of GPs using either probabilistic empirical Bayes or approximation theoretic criteria, which also leads to new mathematical consistency results in spatial statistics.\nFast algorithms with sparse Cholesky:\nOur work develops\nnew adaptations of sparse Cholesky algorithms\nbased on screening effects in spatial statistics that compress the resulting kernel matrices—derived from pointwise evaluations of covariance kernels and their partial derivatives—with state-of-the-art near-linear complexity and new, simpler analysis based on a previously overlooked formula that directly connects sparsity of Cholesky factor to exponential decay of multiscale basis in numerical homogenization rathan than using many more intermediate approximation.\nFor fast kernel methods in high dimensions for scientific machine learning, see also\nrandomly pivoted Cholesky\n.\nMultiscale numerical methods\nPDEs in physical sciences present significant computational challenges, especially those involving heterogeneous media and high-frequency oscillations. These scenarios require substantial resources to resolve accurate physics.\nMultiscale methodology has long been advocated to address this problem by identifying low-complexity, homogenized models at coarse scales. I have focused on multiscale analysis and algorithms for provable approximation guarantees without suffering from the roughness of the media (there is no scale separation) and the wave pollution effect.\nExponentially convergent multiscale finite element methods:\nWe develop\nExpMsFEM\nthat extends previous work and achieves exponential convergence of accuracy without using partition of unity coupling (see\nthis paper\nfor 2D Helmholtz equations and\nthis paper\nfor rough elliptic equations). This method accurately estimates highly oscillatory solutions with a few specialized basis functions that capture coarse-scale behavior while efficiently incorporating fine-scale features through localized simulations.\nMultiscale upscaling with subsampled data:\nWe propose\na novel multiscale method\nusing subsampled coarse variables for upscaling rough elliptic equations, with a connection to scattered data approximation. The method contributes to a novel discussion of measurement scales in the popular local orthogonal decomposition and Gamblets multiscale methods. We develop the corresponding tight\nsubsampled Poincaré inequalities\nfor analysis of its accuracy.\nMultiscale ideas for numerical linear algebra:\nOur\nnew adaptation of the sparse Cholesky algorithm\nfor kernel matrices in PDE contexts with derivative-free and derivative-based measurements exploits the ordering of measurements from coarse to fine scales, where derivatives are treated as finer than derivative-free measurements to achieve sparsity.\nMultiscale methods efficiently compress matrices from discretized PDEs. However, matrices in data science applications may lack direct physical insights for compression and it may not be clear how a multiscale ordering of hiearchy can be leveraged. Randomized ordering could be used to deal with these high dimensional challenges, as demonstrated in\nrandomly pivoted Cholesky\n."
  },
