    "homepage_text": "Li Yang / 杨利\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nLi Yang / 杨利\nLi Yang, Ph.D.\nAdvanced Researcher\nat ZEW - L\neibniz Centre for European Economic Research\nResearch Fellow\nat\nWIL - The World Inequality Lab\nCurrently Visiting\nthe\nFaculty of Social Sciences at Hong Kong University\nCV\nZEW - Leibniz Centre for European Economic Research\nL 7, 1 68161 Mannheim, Germany\n+49 30 89789 - 360\nli.yang@zew.de\n@Google Scholar\nWorking Pa\np\ners\nNEW:\nThe Making of China and India in the 21st Century: Long-Run Human Capital Accumulation from 1900 to 2020\n, with Nitin Kumar Bharti,\nWorld Inequality Lab Working Paper\n, no. 2024/24.  [\nThe Economist\n;\nEPW\n;\nBloomberg\n;\nHindustan Times\n;\nRavish Kumar Official\n]\nNEW:\nWhen Facts Fail: Experimental Evidence on Perceptions and Preferences towards Chinese Investments in Germany\n,\nwith\nZhexun Mo, Katharina Kaeppel, and Carsten Schröder\n.\nStone Center Working Paper Series no. 115.\nNEW:\nIncome Inequality in South Korea, 1933-2022: Evidence from Distributional National Accounts\n,\nwith Sehyun Hong Nak Nyeon Kim and Zhexun Mo.\nWorld Inequality Lab Working Paper\n, no. 202\n4\n/\n03\n.\nNEW:\nEquality and Development: A Comparative and Historical Perspective 1800-2025,\nwith M. Andreescu, M. Arias-Osorio, L. Bauluz, N. Bharti, P. Bothe, P. Brassac, L. Chancel, J. Dietrich, C. Martinez-Toledado, M. Odersky, A. Somanchi et al\n.\nWorld Inequality Lab Working Paper\n, no. 2025/\n2\n5.\n[\nReplication Package\n]\nPublications\nNEW:\nHuman Capital, Unequal Opportunities, and Productivity Convergence: A Global Historical Perspective, 1800-2100,\nwith N. Bharti, A. Gethin, T. Jenmana, Z. Mo, and T. Piketty\n,\n2026.\nForthcoming\n,\nJournal of Public Economics\n.\n[\nwhce.world\n]\nNEW:\nProperty rights, fiscal capacity, and social capacity: The lasting impact of the Taiping Rebellion\n, with Weizheng Lai and L. Colin Xu, 202\n5\n.\nForthcoming\n,\nJournal of Economic Growth\n.\n[\nVoxEu.org\n]\nThe Impact of Communist Party Membership on Wealth Distribution and Accumulation in Urban China\n, with Matteo\nTarga\n,\nWorld Development,\nVolume (181), 2024, 106660.\nAnti-Corruption Campaign in China: An Empirical Investigation\n, with Branko Milanovic and Yaoqi Lin,\nEuropean Journal of Political Economy\n,\nVolume(85), 2024, 102559\n.\nIncome and Wealth Inequality in Hon\ng\n-Kong, 1981-2020: The Rise of Pluto-Communism?\n, with Thomas Piketty,\nThe World Bank Economic Review\n,\n2\n022, 36(4):\n803–834.\nFrom workers to capitalists in less than two generations: A study of Chinese urban elite transformation between 1988 and 2013\n, with Branko Milanovic and Filip Novokmet,\nThe British Journal of Sociology\n,\n2021, 72 (3): 478-513. [\nVoxEu.org\n]\nCapital Accumulation, Private Property and Rising Inequality in China, 1978-2015, with Thomas Piketty and Gabriel Zucman\n,\nAmerican Economic Review\n, 2019, 109 (7): 2469-2496. [\nVoxEU.org\n]\n[\nChinese translation\n]\nFrom Communism to Capitalism: Private versus Public Property and Inequality in China and Russia\n, with Filip Novokmet, Thomas Piketty, and Gabriel Zucman,\nAmerican Economic Association: Papers and Proceedings\n, May 2018, 108:1-6. [\nVoxEu.org\n]\nIncome Inequality and Ethnic Cleavages in Malaysia: Evidence from Distributional National Accounts (1984-2014)\n, with Muhammed A. Khalid,\nJournal of Asian Economics\n, 2021, 72, 101252.\nMemories of Colonial Law: The Inheritance of Human Capital and the Location of Joint Ventures in Early-Reform China\n, with Cheryl Long and Peter Murrell,\nChina Economic Review\n, Volume(58), 2019, 101284.\nTechnology Spillovers from Chinese Outward Direct Investment: The Case of Ethiopia\n, with Mebratu Seyoum and Renshui Wu,\nChina Economic Review\n, April 2015, Vol. 33 (1):35-49.\nBook Chapters and Other\nPublications\nMake in India’ or Miss the Moment? Economic&Political Weekly Corruption Fuels Inequality in China\n,\nwith\nNitin Kumar Bharti\n,\nEco\nnomic&Political Weekly\n,\nAugust\n202\n5, Vol XL, no. 31\n.\nHow Corruption Fuels Inequality in China\n,\nwith Branko Milanovic,\nForeign Affairs\n, September 11, 2024.\nDistributional National Accounts Guidelines: Methods and Concepts used in the World Inequality Database, WIL, 202\n4\n.\nInequality Perceptions and Redistributive Preferences: Literature Review and Proposal for the KLIPS Additional Survey\n, with Sunoong Hwang and Zhexun Mo, in\nPanel Data Quality Improvement Study (XIII)\n, Lee, Kyung-Hee et al., 32--93. Sejong City, South Korea: Korea Labor Institute, 2023.\nTowards equity and sustainability? China's pension system reform moves center stage\n,\nWorld Inequality Lab Working Paper\n, no. 2021/13.\nTeaching\nEconomic History of China on inequality (Short Course)\nFreie Universität Berlin, Germany                                                                              2022\nUniversidad Nacional de Colombia, Colombia                                                    2023\nXiamen University, China                                                                                                 2025\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\n\n---\n\nLi Yang / 杨利 - Bio\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nLi Yang / 杨利\nBiography\nLi Yang / 杨利 is\nan\nA\ndvanced\nR\nesearcher\nat\nZEW\n- Leibniz Centre for European Economic Research\n.\nHe is\nalso a\nresearch fellow\nof the\nWorld Inequality Lab\nat the\nParis School of Economics\n.\nPreviously, he was a\nresearcher in\nthe World Bank\nDEC research group\nin Washington D.C.\nfrom 2013 to 2017, a Marie Curie research fellow at\nParis School of Economics\nfrom 2018 to 2020\n. He was also\nthe coordinator for\nEast and South Asia at the\nWorld Inequality Lab\nfrom\n2018\nto\n2021\n.\nHis main research interests pertain to\nincome and wealth inequality\n,\neconomic history\nand\npolitical economy\n.\nHis research output has been published in leading scientific journals in both economics and sociology such as\nAmerican Economic Review\n,\nJournal of Economic Growth, Journal of Public Economics, World Development,\nthe World Bank Economic Review\n,\nEuropean Journal of Political Economy\nand\nthe\nBritish Journal of Sociology\n.\nOwing to their relevance for ongoing public debates, his findings have also widely been discussed in diverse\nmedia outlets\n, such as\nthe Economist, Foreign Affairs, Washington Post\n,\nthe Wall Street Journal\n,\nBloomberg\n, etc.\nLi Yang received his PhD in Economics in 2019 from\nXiamen University\n, China.  (\nCV\n)\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Kyungmin Lee::https://kyungminlee.org/": {
