    "homepage_text": "Fred Zhang's Homepage\nFred Zhang\nz0@berkeley.edu\nI am a research scientist at Google DeepMind, working on reasoning & RL. I have contributed to\nGemini 2.5\n,\nGemini 3.0\n,\nIMO '25🥇\n,\nICPC '25🥇\nand\nAlphaProof\n.\nI completed my Ph.D. from the\nTheory Group\nat Berkeley EECS in 2024, advised by\nJelani Nelson\n.\n        Prior to that, I received B.S. in Computer Science and B.S. in Mathematics from Duke University.\nLinks\n:\nGoogle Scholar\n/\nTwitter\nPublications\nshow recent\n/\nby date\n/\nby topic\nTopics:\nLanguage model\n/\nAlgorithmic statistics\n/\nSublinear algorithms\n/\nLearning-based algorithms\n/\nOthers\nGemini achieves gold-medal level at the International Collegiate Programming Contest World Finals\nGoogle DeepMind, ICPC 2025 Team (incl.\nFred Zhang\n).\nBlogpost\n, 2025\nAdvanced version of Gemini with Deep Think officially achieves gold-medal standard at the International Mathematical Olympiad\nGoogle DeepMind, IMO 2025 Team (incl.\nFred Zhang\nas key contributor).\nBlogpost\n, 2025 (media coverage:\nNYT\n,\nWSJ\n,\nArs Technica\n,\nReuters\n)\nGemini 2.5: Pushing the Frontier with Advanced Reasoning, Multimodality, Long Context, and Next Generation Agentic Capabilities\nGemini Team (incl.\nFred Zhang\n).\nTechnical report, 2025 (\narXiv\n)\nForecastBench: A Dynamic Benchmark of AI Forecasting Capabilities\nEzra Karger*, Houtan Bastani*, Chen Yueh-Han*, Zachary Jacobs, Danny Halawi,\nFred Zhang\n, Philip E. Tetlock (* Equal contribution).\nICLR 2025 (\narXiv\n,\ncode\n)\nApproaching Human-Level Forecasting with Language Models\nDanny Halawi*,\nFred Zhang\n*, Chen Yueh-Han*,\nJacob Steinhardt\n(* Equal contribution).\nNeurIPS 2024 (\narXiv\n,\nTwitter thread\n,\nblogpost\n,\ncode\n)\nTowards Best Practices of Activation Patching in Language Models: Metrics and Methods\nFred Zhang\n,\nNeel Nanda\n(contribution order).\nICLR 2024\n(\narXiv\n,\nTwitter thread\n)\nAdaptive Regret for Bandits Made Possible: Two Queries Suffice\nZhou Lu\n,\nQiuyi Zhang\n,\nXinyi Chen\n,\nFred Zhang\n,\nDavid P. Woodruff\n,\nElad Hazan\n(contribution order)\nICLR 2024\n(\narXiv\n)\nConstant Approximation for Individual Preference Stable Clustering\nAnders Aamand,\nJustin Y. Chen\n,\nAllen Liu\n,\nSandeep Silwal\n,\nPattara Sukprasert\n,\nAli Vakilian\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order)\nNeurIPS 2023\nSpotlight\nOn Robust Streaming for Learning with Experts: Algorithms and Lower Bounds\nDavid P. Woodruff\n,\nSamson Zhou\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nNeurIPS 2023\n(\narXiv\n)\nPrivately Estimating a Gaussian: Efficient, Robust and Optimal\nDaniel Alabi\n,\nPravesh K. Kothari\n, Pranay Tankala,\nPrayaag Venkat\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nSTOC 2023\n(\narXiv\n)\nBayesian Strategy-Proof Facility Location via Robust Estimation\nManolis Zampetakis\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nAISTATS 2023\n(\nPaper\n)\nRobust Algorithms on Adaptive Inputs from Bounded Adversaries\nYeshwanth Cherapanamjeri\n,\nSandeep Silwal\n,\nDavid P. Woodruff\n,\nQiuyi Zhang\n,\nFred Zhang\n,\nSamson Zhou\n(alphabetical order).\nICLR 2023\n(\narXiv\n)\nOnline Prediction in Sub-linear Space\nBinghui Peng\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nSODA 2023\nBest Student Paper\n(\narXiv\n,\ntalk at Google\n,\ntalk at TTIC\n)\nOptimal Query Complexities for Dynamic Trace Estimation\nDavid P. Woodruff\n,\nQiuyi Zhang\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order)\nNeurIPS 2022\n(\narXiv\n)\nFaster Fundamental Graph Algorithms via Learned Predictions\nJustin Y. Chen\n,\nSandeep Silwal\n,\nAli Vakilian\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nICML 2022\n(\narXiv\n)\nRobust and Heavy-Tailed Mean Estimation Made Simple, via Regret Minimization\nSamuel B. Hopkins\n,\nJerry Li\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nNeurIPS 2020\n(\narXiv\n)\nOptimal Robustness-Consistency Trade-offs for Learning-Augmented Online Algorithms\nAlexander Wei\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical order).\nNeurIPS 2020\n(\narXiv\n)\nA Fast Spectral Algorithm for Mean Estimation with Sub-Gaussian Rates\nZhixian Lei,\nKyle Luh\n,\nPrayaag Venkat\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical\n                    order).\nCOLT 2020\n(\narXiv\n)\nSGD on Neural Networks Learns Functions of Increasing Complexity\nPreetum Nakkiran\n,\nGal Kaplun\n,\nDimitris Kalimeris\n, Tristan Yang, Benjamin L. Edelman,\nFred Zhang\n,\nBoaz Barak\n(contribution order).\nNeurIPS 2019\nSpotlight\n(\narXiv\n)\nMinimum Cut and Minimum\nk\n-Cut in Hypergraphs via Branching Contractions\nKyle Fox\n,\nDebmalya Panigrahi\n,\nFred Zhang\n(alphabetical\n                    order).\nSODA 2019\n&\nACM Transactions on Algorithms (TALG)\n(\njournal version\n)\nNotes\nLeverage Score and Feature Dimensionality\n(comment on\nToy Models of Superposition\n, 2023)\nMirror Descent and Online Learning\n(lecture note, 2019)\nTensor Networks, Matrix Product States and Density Matrix Renormalization Group\n(\nblog post\nat\nWindows on Theory\n, 2018)\nGoodness-of-fit Testing and Structure Learning of the Ising Model\n(survey, 2018)\nThe Topological Approach Towards Graph Evasiveness\n(survey, 2017)\nBerkeley Food Opinions\n(2024)\nTeaching\nGraduate Student Instructor, UC Berkeley\nCS 294-165: Sketching Algorithms (\nFall 20\n)\nCS 170: Efficient Algorithms and Intractable Problems (\nSpring 20\n)\nUndergraudate Teaching Assistant, Duke University\nCOMPSCI 330: Design and Analysis of Algorithms (\nSpring 16\n,\nFall 16\n,\nSpring 17\n,\nFall 17\n,\nSpring 18\n)\nContact\nz0@berkeley.edu"
