    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Shang Gao | School of Natural Resources and the Environment\nSkip to main content\nSearch\nsearch\nsearch\nSearch\nmenu\nMenu\nhome\nHome\nclose\nClose\nSearch\nsearch\nMain Menu\nAbout\nchevron_right\nPeople\nchevron_right\nUndergraduate\nchevron_right\nGraduate\nchevron_right\nResearch\nExtension\nAlumni & Friends\nI am a\nSelect your audience\nchoose an option\nfuture undergraduate\ncurrent undergraduate\nfuture graduate\ncurrent graduate\nalumni\ncommunity member\nGo\nto the page for that group\nApply\nRequest Info\nProspective Undergraduates\nProspective Graduate Students\nGive\nShang Gao\nShang Gao\nhe/him\nAssistant Professor of Watershed Management and Ecohydrology\nENR2 N254\n1064 E Lowell St\n85721 Tucson, AZ\nshanggao@arizona.edu\nLinks\nGoogle Scholar\nResearch Gate\nDr. Shang Gao serves as Assistant Professor in the School of Natural Resources and the Environment. Before joining the University of Arizona, Shang worked at the University of Oklahoma (OU) as a postdoctoral research associate. He obtained his Ph.D. in civil engineering at University of Texas at Arlington (UTA). By training, Dr. Gao is a hydrologist studying two ends of the hydrologic spectrum, i.e., floods and droughts. He combinedly utilizes hydrologic models, remote sensing observations, and data-driven methods to understand the surface water dynamics. He has been actively working with federal agencies, municipal water utilities, and native indigenous communities to address water-related issues.\nResearch\nMy research group combinedly uses hydro-modeling and remote sensing to study hydrologic extremes, e.g., storms, floods, and droughts, their spatiotemporal variabilities and correspondong natural and anthropogenic drivers, as well as their changes under a warming climate. Interested students are welcome to contact me directly.\nTeaching\nI currently teach the Watershed Management WSM 462/562. My teaching interests include geospatial techniques, remote sensing and hydrometeorology, water sustainability under a changing climate.\nFaculty\nWatershed Management & Ecohydrology\nGeospatial Analysis & Remote Sensing\nExperience\nDegrees\nPh.D. in Water Resource Engineering, 2019, University of Texas at Arlington, Arlington, TX\nM.S. in Civil and Environmental Engineering, 2013, UC Irvine, Irvine, CA\nB.S. in Environmental Science, 2011, Nankai University, Tianjin, China\nkeyboard_arrow_up\nBack to top"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Gaoyue Zhou::https://gaoyuezhou.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://gaoyuezhou.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "zhougy_99@berkeley.edu",
      "gz2123@nyu.edu"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/gaoyuezhou",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Gaoyue Zhou\nGaoyue (Kathy) Zhou\nI am a second-year Computer Science Ph.D. student at\nNYU Courant\nadvised by Professors\nLerrel Pinto\nand\nYann\n                      LeCun\n.\nPrior to NYU, I was a MS in Robotics student at\nCMU Robotics\n                      Institute\nadvised by Professor\nAbhinav Gupta\n.\n                    I worked on enhancing robots' generalization capabilities and benchmarking. My thesis can be found\nhere\n.\nPrior to CMU, I did my undergraduate in Computer Science and Applied Mathematics at UC Berkeley.\n                    During my time at Berkeley, I had the pleasure to work with Professors\nSergey Levine\nand\nJohn DeNero\nin the\nBerkeley\n                      Artificial Intelligence Research (BAIR)\nLab.\nEmail\n/\nCV\n/\nGoogle Scholar\n/\nGithub\n/\nPhotography\nResearch\nI am broadly interested in Robotics, Reinforcement Learning, and NLP. My motivation is to build\n                    agents with cognitive capabilities that can learn and infer like humans.\nDINO-WM: World Models on Pre-trained Visual Features enable Zero-shot Planning\nGaoyue Zhou\n, Hengkai Pan, Yann LeCun, Lerrel Pinto\nICML 2025\narXiv\n|\ncode\n|\nproject page\nWe introduce a simple yet effective method for constructing versatile world models with pre-trained DINOv2 that generalize to complex environment dynamics, which enables zero-shot solutions at test time for arbitrary goals.\nNavigation World Models\nAmir Bar,\nGaoyue Zhou\n, Danny Tran, Trevor Darrell, Yann LeCun\nCVPR 2025 (Best Paper Honorable Mention)\narXiv\n|\ncode\n|\nproject page\nWe train a video world model across diverse environments and embodiments, demonstrating its ability to plan novel trajectories in both known and unknown environments.\nTrain Offline, Test Online: A Real Robot Learning Benchmark\nGaoyue Zhou*\n, Victoria Dean*, Mohan Kumar Srirama, Aravind Rajeswaran, Jyothish Pari,\n                  Kyle Hatch, Aryan Jain, Tianhe Yu, Pieter Abbeel, Lerrel Pinto, Chelsea Finn, Abhinav Gupta\nAccepted to ICRA 2023\nBest Paper Award at NeurIPS WBRC 2022\nOpenReview\n|\nvideo\n|\nproject page\nWe introduce a new benchmark for tabletop manipulation: Train Offline, Test Online (TOTO) and\n                    showed performance of state-of-the-art models on two tasks.\nReal World Offline Reinforcement Learning with Realistic Data Source\nGaoyue Zhou*\n, Liyiming Ke*, Siddhartha Srinivasa, Abhinav Gupta, Aravind Rajeswaran,\n                  Vikash Kumar\nAccepted to ICRA 2023 and 3 NeurIPS 2022 workshops\narXiv\n|\nvideo\n|\nproject page\nIn this work, we evaluate Offline RL algorithms' performance on real robot, on in-domain tasks and\n                    transfer learning settings.\nPutting the Con in Context: Identifying Deceptive Actors in the Game of Mafia\nSamee Ibraheem*,\nGaoyue Zhou*\n, John DeNero\nAnnual Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics\n                    (NAACL), 2022\nOral Presentation\narXiv\n|\nvideo\n|\nproject page\nIn this paper, we collect and release a dataset for identifying deceptive actors through the game\n                    of Mafia, as well as train models to identify such actors and reveal features of their language.\nParrot: Data-Driven Behavioral Priors for Reinforcement Learning\nAvi Singh*, Huihan Liu*,\nGaoyue Zhou\n, Albert Yu, Nicholas Rhinehart, Sergey Levine\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2021\nOral Presentation (top 1.8% of submissions)\narXiv\n|\nvideo\n|\nproject page\nWe proposed a method for pre-training a behavioral prior for reinforcement learning using data\n                    from a diverse range of tasks, and used this behavioral prior to speed up learning of new tasks.\nModeling Eva Hild's Sculpture \"Wholly\"\nAdvised by Prof. Carlo H. Séquin\ntech report\nWe proposed various ways to use CAD Tools for the Procedural Generation of 2-Manifold Sculpture\n                    Geometries with high-level control.\nTeaching\n10725A: Convex Optimization\n, Fall 2022\nCS 189: Introduction to Machine Learning\n, Fall 2020\nEECS 16B (Designing Information Devices and Systems II)\n, Fall 2019, Spring 2020, Spring 2021\nWebsite template\n\n---\n\nAbout - Gaoyue's\nCREDITS\nThe posts were written and exported as markdown ready for publication with\nDillinger\n, a useful online editor.\nAll the commits were performed with the help of\nSource Tree\napp.\nThe photos are organised in galleries with\nISOTOPE\n.\nThe search overlay function code lies in\nSUPER SEARCH\n.\n\n---\n\nAbout - Gaoyue's\nCREDITS\nThe posts were written and exported as markdown ready for publication with\nDillinger\n, a useful online editor.\nAll the commits were performed with the help of\nSource Tree\napp.\nThe photos are organised in galleries with\nISOTOPE\n.\nThe search overlay function code lies in\nSUPER SEARCH\n."
  },
  "deep_homepage::Mufeng Tang::https://c16mftang.github.io/": {
    "homepage": "https://c16mftang.github.io/",
    "all_emails": [
      "mufeng.tang@ndcn.ox.ac.uk"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/C16Mftang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/mufeng-tang-115a64139/",
    "homepage_text": "Mufeng Tang\nMufeng\nTang\nGithub\nLinkedin\nHi, I'm Mufeng Tang (唐沐丰)\nI'm an Applied Scientist at Amazon, based in London, UK. At the moment, I work with LLMs.\nPreviously, I was a Ph.D. student in Computational Neuroscience and Machine Learning at University of\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tOxford, working with\nProf. Rafal\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tBogacz\nand\nProf. Helen\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tBarron\n.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI use neural networks to understand how our brain learns to behave and represent, \r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tand use our knowledge of the brain to improve neural network training.\nPrior to my PhD, I obtained my master's degree in Statistics at the University of Chicago.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tI worked with\nProf. Yali\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tAmit\nand\nProf.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tJason MacLean\nat UChicago on biologically plausible neural networks.\nI was born and raised in Chongqing, China.\nMy CV\nGet in touch\nIf you want to have a chat, contact me through the following ways:\nmufeng.tang@ndcn.ox.ac.uk\n(44) 07497865905\nMRC Brain Network Dynamics Unit\nUniversity of Oxford, Mansfield Road, Oxford OX1 3TH\n\n---\n\nMUFENG TANG - ABOUT\nMUFENG\nTANG\nTwitter\nInstagram\nGithub\nLinkedin\nAbout me\nA Little Biography\nI’m currently based in Oxford, UK, pursuing my Ph.D. (or D.Phil., as locals likes calling it) in computational neuroscience at the MRC Brain Network Dynamics Unit, supervised by\nProf. Rafal Bogacz\n. \n                                        I have a general interest in using machine learning as a tool to understand how the brain works, and reversely, applying the knowledge of neuroscience to the development of artificial intelligence, and I'm using predictive coding to achieve these goals\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t (see\nhere\nfor a more detailed description of my research interests).\nPrior to my D.Phil., I spent two years at the University of Chicago, Illinois, doing a master's degree in statistics. I worked with\nProf. Yali Amit\non bio-plausible self-supervised learning, and\nProf. Jason MacLean\non Spiking Neural Networks there. \n                                        The photo above was taken in winter 2021, when the degree in Chicago dropped below -20 Celsius and the Michigan Lake was frozen (so was I).\nBefore I went to the US, I did my undergraduate degree at University College London, where I had a lot fun exploring a wide range\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tof disciplines and subjects. These include architecture and urban planning, in which I still have a strong interest even though I work with equations\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tand codes every day. My final-year project at UCL combined my interests in urban studies and data science. I created a sentiment dataset of Twitter \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tby the emojis they contain, and used this dataset to train a sentiment classifier that predicts the congestion areas of London based on Tweets coming\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tout of these areas.\nI spent the first 18 years of my life in\nChongqing (重庆)\n, a city in southwestern China with \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tstunning views of mountains and rivers. I am also proudly a speaker of the local dialect Chongqing Hua (重庆话), which sounds (and sometimes writes)\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tquite differently from Mandarin.\nOuside of academia, I'm a lover of basketball (Brooklyn Nets) and dota2, as well as casual walking and photo-taking.\nGet in touch\nIf you want to talk about science, dota2 or life in general, contact me through the following ways:\nmufeng.tang@ndcn.ox.ac.uk\n(44) 07497865905\nMRC Brain Network Dynamics Unit\nUniversity of Oxford, Mansfield Road, Oxford OX1 3TH\n\n---\n\nMUFENG TANG - ABOUT\nMUFENG\nTANG\nTwitter\nInstagram\nGithub\nLinkedin\nAbout me\nA Little Biography\nI’m currently based in Oxford, UK, pursuing my Ph.D. (or D.Phil., as locals likes calling it) in computational neuroscience at the MRC Brain Network Dynamics Unit, supervised by\nProf. Rafal Bogacz\n. \n                                        I have a general interest in using machine learning as a tool to understand how the brain works, and reversely, applying the knowledge of neuroscience to the development of artificial intelligence, and I'm using predictive coding to achieve these goals\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t (see\nhere\nfor a more detailed description of my research interests).\nPrior to my D.Phil., I spent two years at the University of Chicago, Illinois, doing a master's degree in statistics. I worked with\nProf. Yali Amit\non bio-plausible self-supervised learning, and\nProf. Jason MacLean\non Spiking Neural Networks there. \n                                        The photo above was taken in winter 2021, when the degree in Chicago dropped below -20 Celsius and the Michigan Lake was frozen (so was I).\nBefore I went to the US, I did my undergraduate degree at University College London, where I had a lot fun exploring a wide range\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tof disciplines and subjects. These include architecture and urban planning, in which I still have a strong interest even though I work with equations\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tand codes every day. My final-year project at UCL combined my interests in urban studies and data science. I created a sentiment dataset of Twitter \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tby the emojis they contain, and used this dataset to train a sentiment classifier that predicts the congestion areas of London based on Tweets coming\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tout of these areas.\nI spent the first 18 years of my life in\nChongqing (重庆)\n, a city in southwestern China with \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tstunning views of mountains and rivers. I am also proudly a speaker of the local dialect Chongqing Hua (重庆话), which sounds (and sometimes writes)\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tquite differently from Mandarin.\nOuside of academia, I'm a lover of basketball (Brooklyn Nets) and dota2, as well as casual walking and photo-taking.\nGet in touch\nIf you want to talk about science, dota2 or life in general, contact me through the following ways:\nmufeng.tang@ndcn.ox.ac.uk\n(44) 07497865905\nMRC Brain Network Dynamics Unit\nUniversity of Oxford, Mansfield Road, Oxford OX1 3TH"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Pengfei Liu::https://liu.eas.gatech.edu/dr-liu/": {
