    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/ceciliahuang1/",
    "homepage_text": "Xinmeng Huang (Cecilia) – The Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC)\nSkip to the content\nAdvancing The Science of Networks in Communities\nXinmeng Huang (Cecilia)\nPredoctoral Researcher\nEmail:\nCeciliahuang@u.northwestern.edu\nLinkedIn:\nCecilia Huang\nResearch Interests\n:  Networks, Local/Global Properties, Collaborations (Micro/Macro), Purpose-oriented Networks,  Human-AI Interactions/Teaming, Network Structure, Team Process and Dynamics, Applied Research.\nKeywords:\nNetworks, Network Theory, Organizations, Communications, Collaborations, Team, Dynamics, AI, Impact Networks.\nEducation:\nB.A. in Drama, University of Virginia\nM.S. in Leadership for Creative Enterprises, Northwestern University School of Communication; Minor in Entrepreneurship, Northwestern McCormick School of Engineering\nProject Contributions:\nProject TEAMS: Analyzing local network clustering coefficient, bidirectional trust (ties), and performance in complex hybrid cross-functional team/multi-teams networks.\nSearch for:\nSearch\nRecent Posts\nHouston, We Have a Solution: How NASA and Northwestern Are Redefining Teamwork\nDr. Noshir Contractor to Deliver Inaugural Keynote at MU–ICA Conference 2025\nNoshir Contractor Keynotes ICA 2025 on Generative AI and Research\nHow We Work Matters More Than Where We Work — New CSCW 2025 Research\nSONIC Alum Spotlight: Anup S on Innovation, AI, and Entrepreneurship\nRead More\nRecent Tweets\nSONIC Research Group\nFollow\nThe Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC) research group advances social network theories, methods, and tools.\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n4 Mar\nThis Friday, March 6, join Creative Agency in the Age of AI at Northwestern from 9 a.m.–5 p.m. for a day of panels and conversation on human-AI collaboration. Organized by Duri Long, Noshir Contractor (@noshir), and Karan Ahuja.\nMore info: https://www.hci.northwestern.edu/news-events/colloquia-special-events/hci-d-ai-symposia-2026/creative-agency-in-the-age-of-ai.html\nReply on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nRetweet on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nLike on Twitter 2029260777283010754\n4\nTwitter\n2029260777283010754\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n17 Feb\nCongrats to NU undergrads Shraeya Iyer, Jeffrey Tu, & Isabel Su—presenting at NetSciX (Feb 17–20) in New Zealand, supported by @BuffettInst scholarship!\nTalk: A Multiplex Lens on Influencer Performance, Group Synergy, and Network Centralization\nCo-Authors: Megan Chan & @noshir\n3\nReply on Twitter 2023802594578292796\nRetweet on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n2\nLike on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n4\nTwitter\n2023802594578292796\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n11 Feb\nNew in Research Policy with Noshir Contractor (@noshir), Pamela Hinds, Aaron Schecter, and Elisa Mattarelli: Want better ideas in open innovation? It matters who gives you feedback — and who they're connected to.\nCheck it out: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048733325001611\nReply on Twitter 2021694237507469461\nRetweet on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n2\nLike on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n5\nTwitter\n2021694237507469461\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n3 Feb\nSIAM News: Diego Gómez-Zará & Noshir Contractor (@noshir) on AI for better, more representative teams.\nIn MyDreamTeam (380+ participants), creativity was highest when people chose teammates—with representation-aware AI nudges.\nReply on Twitter 2018723094722912420\nRetweet on Twitter 2018723094722912420\nLike on Twitter 2018723094722912420\n3\nTwitter\n2018723094722912420\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n9 Jan\nIn an in-press study, Leslie DeChurch, Alina Lungeanu, Megan Chan, and Noshir Contractor analyze data from NASA space analog teams and Ancient Rome, finding that dyadic shared leadership is uniquely unstable, while solo or 3+ leader structures persist.\nhttps://lnkd.in/eK-2sU-W\nReply on Twitter 2009715140678340744\nRetweet on Twitter 2009715140678340744\n2\nLike on Twitter 2009715140678340744\n3\nTwitter\n2009715140678340744\nLoad More...\nClose Menu\n\n---\n\nContact Us – The Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC)\nSkip to the content\nAdvancing The Science of Networks in Communities\nContact Us\nFor all inquiries please contact the director\nNoshir Contractor.\nEmail:\nnosh@northwestern.edu\nAddress:\nSONIC Lab\nNorthwestern University\nFrances Searle Building, Room 1.459\n2240 Campus Drive\nEvanston, IL 60208\nSONIC Laboratory is located in the\nFrances Searle Building\nat 42.058N Lat, 87.674W Long.\nPlease note the Searle Building is\nwithin\nthe Evanston campus of Northwestern on Campus Drive and not directly on Sheridan Road.\nMaps to SONIC Laboratory:\nGoogle Map to SONIC Laboratory\nGoogle map to 2240 Campus Drive Evanston, IL\nNorthwestern University Map to SONIC Laboratory\nNorthwestern University’s interactive map to the Frances Searle Building\nSearch for:\nSearch\nRecent Posts\nHouston, We Have a Solution: How NASA and Northwestern Are Redefining Teamwork\nDr. Noshir Contractor to Deliver Inaugural Keynote at MU–ICA Conference 2025\nNoshir Contractor Keynotes ICA 2025 on Generative AI and Research\nHow We Work Matters More Than Where We Work — New CSCW 2025 Research\nSONIC Alum Spotlight: Anup S on Innovation, AI, and Entrepreneurship\nRead More\nRecent Tweets\nSONIC Research Group\nFollow\nThe Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC) research group advances social network theories, methods, and tools.\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n4 Mar\nThis Friday, March 6, join Creative Agency in the Age of AI at Northwestern from 9 a.m.–5 p.m. for a day of panels and conversation on human-AI collaboration. Organized by Duri Long, Noshir Contractor (@noshir), and Karan Ahuja.\nMore info: https://www.hci.northwestern.edu/news-events/colloquia-special-events/hci-d-ai-symposia-2026/creative-agency-in-the-age-of-ai.html\nReply on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nRetweet on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nLike on Twitter 2029260777283010754\n4\nTwitter\n2029260777283010754\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n17 Feb\nCongrats to NU undergrads Shraeya Iyer, Jeffrey Tu, & Isabel Su—presenting at NetSciX (Feb 17–20) in New Zealand, supported by @BuffettInst scholarship!\nTalk: A Multiplex Lens on Influencer Performance, Group Synergy, and Network Centralization\nCo-Authors: Megan Chan & @noshir\n3\nReply on Twitter 2023802594578292796\nRetweet on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n2\nLike on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n4\nTwitter\n2023802594578292796\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n11 Feb\nNew in Research Policy with Noshir Contractor (@noshir), Pamela Hinds, Aaron Schecter, and Elisa Mattarelli: Want better ideas in open innovation? It matters who gives you feedback — and who they're connected to.\nCheck it out: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048733325001611\nReply on Twitter 2021694237507469461\nRetweet on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n2\nLike on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n5\nTwitter\n2021694237507469461\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n3 Feb\nSIAM News: Diego Gómez-Zará & Noshir Contractor (@noshir) on AI for better, more representative teams.\nIn MyDreamTeam (380+ participants), creativity was highest when people chose teammates—with representation-aware AI nudges.\nReply on Twitter 2018723094722912420\nRetweet on Twitter 2018723094722912420\nLike on Twitter 2018723094722912420\n3\nTwitter\n2018723094722912420\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n9 Jan\nIn an in-press study, Leslie DeChurch, Alina Lungeanu, Megan Chan, and Noshir Contractor analyze data from NASA space analog teams and Ancient Rome, finding that dyadic shared leadership is uniquely unstable, while solo or 3+ leader structures persist.\nhttps://lnkd.in/eK-2sU-W\nReply on Twitter 2009715140678340744\nRetweet on Twitter 2009715140678340744\n2\nLike on Twitter 2009715140678340744\n3\nTwitter\n2009715140678340744\nLoad More...\nClose Menu\n\n---\n\nContact Us – The Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC)\nSkip to the content\nAdvancing The Science of Networks in Communities\nContact Us\nFor all inquiries please contact the director\nNoshir Contractor.\nEmail:\nnosh@northwestern.edu\nAddress:\nSONIC Lab\nNorthwestern University\nFrances Searle Building, Room 1.459\n2240 Campus Drive\nEvanston, IL 60208\nSONIC Laboratory is located in the\nFrances Searle Building\nat 42.058N Lat, 87.674W Long.\nPlease note the Searle Building is\nwithin\nthe Evanston campus of Northwestern on Campus Drive and not directly on Sheridan Road.\nMaps to SONIC Laboratory:\nGoogle Map to SONIC Laboratory\nGoogle map to 2240 Campus Drive Evanston, IL\nNorthwestern University Map to SONIC Laboratory\nNorthwestern University’s interactive map to the Frances Searle Building\nSearch for:\nSearch\nRecent Posts\nHouston, We Have a Solution: How NASA and Northwestern Are Redefining Teamwork\nDr. Noshir Contractor to Deliver Inaugural Keynote at MU–ICA Conference 2025\nNoshir Contractor Keynotes ICA 2025 on Generative AI and Research\nHow We Work Matters More Than Where We Work — New CSCW 2025 Research\nSONIC Alum Spotlight: Anup S on Innovation, AI, and Entrepreneurship\nRead More\nRecent Tweets\nSONIC Research Group\nFollow\nThe Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC) research group advances social network theories, methods, and tools.\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n4 Mar\nThis Friday, March 6, join Creative Agency in the Age of AI at Northwestern from 9 a.m.–5 p.m. for a day of panels and conversation on human-AI collaboration. Organized by Duri Long, Noshir Contractor (@noshir), and Karan Ahuja.\nMore info: https://www.hci.northwestern.edu/news-events/colloquia-special-events/hci-d-ai-symposia-2026/creative-agency-in-the-age-of-ai.html\nReply on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nRetweet on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nLike on Twitter 2029260777283010754\n4\nTwitter\n2029260777283010754\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n17 Feb\nCongrats to NU undergrads Shraeya Iyer, Jeffrey Tu, & Isabel Su—presenting at NetSciX (Feb 17–20) in New Zealand, supported by @BuffettInst scholarship!\nTalk: A Multiplex Lens on Influencer Performance, Group Synergy, and Network Centralization\nCo-Authors: Megan Chan & @noshir\n3\nReply on Twitter 2023802594578292796\nRetweet on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n2\nLike on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n4\nTwitter\n2023802594578292796\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n11 Feb\nNew in Research Policy with Noshir Contractor (@noshir), Pamela Hinds, Aaron Schecter, and Elisa Mattarelli: Want better ideas in open innovation? It matters who gives you feedback — and who they're connected to.\nCheck it out: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048733325001611\nReply on Twitter 2021694237507469461\nRetweet on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n2\nLike on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n5\nTwitter\n2021694237507469461\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n3 Feb\nSIAM News: Diego Gómez-Zará & Noshir Contractor (@noshir) on AI for better, more representative teams.\nIn MyDreamTeam (380+ participants), creativity was highest when people chose teammates—with representation-aware AI nudges.\nReply on Twitter 2018723094722912420\nRetweet on Twitter 2018723094722912420\nLike on Twitter 2018723094722912420\n3\nTwitter\n2018723094722912420\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n9 Jan\nIn an in-press study, Leslie DeChurch, Alina Lungeanu, Megan Chan, and Noshir Contractor analyze data from NASA space analog teams and Ancient Rome, finding that dyadic shared leadership is uniquely unstable, while solo or 3+ leader structures persist.\nhttps://lnkd.in/eK-2sU-W\nReply on Twitter 2009715140678340744\nRetweet on Twitter 2009715140678340744\n2\nLike on Twitter 2009715140678340744\n3\nTwitter\n2009715140678340744\nLoad More...\nClose Menu\n\n---\n\nHome – The Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC)\nSkip to the content\nAdvancing The Science of Networks in Communities\nHome\nThe Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC) research group\nThe Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC) research group advances social network theories, methods, and tools to better understand and meet the needs of diverse communities. SONIC develops cutting-edge techniques to study and improve social and knowledge networks in distributed working groups, online communities, virtual teams, and other large communities. Using a comprehensive methodology, SONIC researchers model, assess, validate, and enable the emergence of social and knowledge networks.\nSONIC helps communities discover their existing communication and knowledge networks, diagnose their network’s health by measuring its robustness, connectedness, bottlenecks, etc., and design the social incentives and technical infrastructure needed for networks to function at optimal effectiveness.\nA Hierarchical Multidimensional Network-based Approach for Multi-Competitor Product Design\nNASA Crews\nNASA Team Task Switching\nNetwork Canvas\nNASA US-Russian HERA\nSafebets and Risky Propositions\nSchneider\nNASA Scale\nScience of Team Science\nThe Signatures of Success in Human-Agent Teams\nRADAR\nTeam DNA\nWearable Devices for Monitoring Social Networks\nSearch for:\nSearch\nRecent Posts\nHouston, We Have a Solution: How NASA and Northwestern Are Redefining Teamwork\nDr. Noshir Contractor to Deliver Inaugural Keynote at MU–ICA Conference 2025\nNoshir Contractor Keynotes ICA 2025 on Generative AI and Research\nHow We Work Matters More Than Where We Work — New CSCW 2025 Research\nSONIC Alum Spotlight: Anup S on Innovation, AI, and Entrepreneurship\nRead More\nRecent Tweets\nSONIC Research Group\nFollow\nThe Science of Networks in Communities (SONIC) research group advances social network theories, methods, and tools.\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n4 Mar\nThis Friday, March 6, join Creative Agency in the Age of AI at Northwestern from 9 a.m.–5 p.m. for a day of panels and conversation on human-AI collaboration. Organized by Duri Long, Noshir Contractor (@noshir), and Karan Ahuja.\nMore info: https://www.hci.northwestern.edu/news-events/colloquia-special-events/hci-d-ai-symposia-2026/creative-agency-in-the-age-of-ai.html\nReply on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nRetweet on Twitter 2029260777283010754\nLike on Twitter 2029260777283010754\n4\nTwitter\n2029260777283010754\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n17 Feb\nCongrats to NU undergrads Shraeya Iyer, Jeffrey Tu, & Isabel Su—presenting at NetSciX (Feb 17–20) in New Zealand, supported by @BuffettInst scholarship!\nTalk: A Multiplex Lens on Influencer Performance, Group Synergy, and Network Centralization\nCo-Authors: Megan Chan & @noshir\n3\nReply on Twitter 2023802594578292796\nRetweet on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n2\nLike on Twitter 2023802594578292796\n4\nTwitter\n2023802594578292796\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n11 Feb\nNew in Research Policy with Noshir Contractor (@noshir), Pamela Hinds, Aaron Schecter, and Elisa Mattarelli: Want better ideas in open innovation? It matters who gives you feedback — and who they're connected to.\nCheck it out: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048733325001611\nReply on Twitter 2021694237507469461\nRetweet on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n2\nLike on Twitter 2021694237507469461\n5\nTwitter\n2021694237507469461\nSONIC Research Group\n@sonicnu\n·\n3 Feb\nSIAM News: Diego Gómez-Zará & Noshir Contractor (@noshir) on AI for better, more representative teams.\nIn MyDreamTeam (380+ participants), creativity ...[truncated]"
