    "homepage_text": "Yue Li | Yue Li's Homepage\nYue Li\n李越\nI am a fifth-year Ph.D. student from the\nDepartment of Statistics & Data Science\nat\nCarnegie Mellon University\n.\nI’m very fortunate to be advised by Professor\nKathryn Roeder\nand Professor\nYuting Wei\n. Prior to CMU, I did my undergraduate in\nSchool of Mathemetical Sciences\nat\nPeking University\n, where I worked with Professor\nWei Lin\nand Professor\nHao Ge\n.\nI am happy to spend Fall 2021 in UC Berkeley, participating in the\nComputational Complexity of Statistical Inference\nprogram at Simons Institute for the Theory of Computing.\nEmail:\nyuel5 [@] andrew [dot] cmu [dot] edu\nCurrent Research Interest\nI am broadly interested in the theoretical and applied parts of statistics, including\nHigh-dimensional statistics inference;\nStatistical genetics;\nTransfer learning,\namong others.\nPreprints\nMinimum l1-norm interpolators: Precise asymptotics and multiple descent\nYue Li\n, Yuting Wei\nSubmitted, 2021\nPublications\nIntegration and transfer learning of single-cell transcriptomes via cFIT\nMinshi Peng,\nYue Li\n, Brie Wamsley, Yuting Wei, and Kathryn Roeder\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 118.10 (2021).\nRandomized tests for high-dimensional regression: A more efficient and powerful solution\nYue Li\n,\nIlmun Kim\n, and Yuting Wei\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems 33 (2020).\nSee our NeurIPS presentation\nhere\n.\nSCRIBE: a new approach to dropout imputation and batch effects correction for single-cell RNA-seq data\nYiliang Zhang, Kexuan Liang, Molei Liu,\nYue Li\n, Hao Ge, and Hongyu Zhao\nMachine Learning in Computational Biology (MLCB) 2019.\nOthers\nA short memo on using servers for computing.\nSome Python examples.\n\n---\n\nYue Li | Yue Li's Homepage\nYue Li\nXMin\nis the first Hugo theme I have designed. The original reason that I wrote it was I needed a minimal example of Hugo themes when I was writing the\nblogdown\nbook. Basically I wanted a simple theme that supports a navigation menu, a home page, other single pages, lists of pages, blog posts, categories, tags, and RSS. That is all. Nothing fancy. In terms of CSS and JavaScript, I really want to keep them minimal. In fact, this theme does not contain any JavaScript code at all, although on this example website I did introduce some JavaScript code (still relatively simple anyway). The theme does not contain any images, either, and is pretty much a plain-text theme.\nThe theme name “XMin” can be interpreted as “\nX\nie’s\nMin\nimal theme” (Xie is my last name) or “e\nX\ntremely\nMin\nimal theme”.\n\n---\n\nYue Li | Yue Li's Homepage\nYue Li\nXMin\nis the first Hugo theme I have designed. The original reason that I wrote it was I needed a minimal example of Hugo themes when I was writing the\nblogdown\nbook. Basically I wanted a simple theme that supports a navigation menu, a home page, other single pages, lists of pages, blog posts, categories, tags, and RSS. That is all. Nothing fancy. In terms of CSS and JavaScript, I really want to keep them minimal. In fact, this theme does not contain any JavaScript code at all, although on this example website I did introduce some JavaScript code (still relatively simple anyway). The theme does not contain any images, either, and is pretty much a plain-text theme.\nThe theme name “XMin” can be interpreted as “\nX\nie’s\nMin\nimal theme” (Xie is my last name) or “e\nX\ntremely\nMin\nimal theme”."
  },
