    "github": "https://github.com/FDU-INC",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Zhe Chen\nMENU\nAbout Me\nPublications\nExperiences\nPersonal\nZhe Chen\nZhe Chen (陈哲)\nPh.D. in Computer Science\nEmail: zhechen#fudan.edu.cn\n[\nGoogle Scholar\n]\nAbout Me\nDr. Zhe Chen is an assistant professor within the School of Computer Science at Fudan University, and the Co-Founder of SpaceAIC Ltd. Inc\n. He obtained his Ph.D. degree in Computer Science from Fudan University, China, with a 2019 ACM SIGCOMM China Doctoral Dissertation Award. Before joining AIWiSe, he worked as a research fellow in NTU for several years, and his research achievements, along with his efforts in launching products based on them, have thus earned him 2021 ACM SIGMOBILE China Rising Star Award recently. His current research interests including wireless networking, deep learning, mobile and pervasive computing, and embedded systems. More information can be found\nhere\n.\nOpenings\n: I am looking for highly self-motivated Ph.D./master students, research interns, and PostDoc researchers to join our group. If you are interested, please drop me an email with your CV and transcripts. See details\nhere\n.\nResearch Interests\nSatellite Internet\nResource-efficient AI systems\nWireless sensing\nRecent News\nFebruary 2026: Our new paper accepted at\nACM/IEEE DAC 2026\n!\nNovember 2025: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE TPDS\n!\nSeptember 2025: Our two new papers conditionally accepted at\nACM/IEEE SenSys 2026\n!\nAugust 2025: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nIEEE ICNP 2025\n!\nJuly 2025: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nACM MM 2025\n!\nMarch 2025: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nACM MobiSys 2025\n!\nFebruary 2025: We release the new source code of our\nSplitFM\nproject!!!\nSplitFM\nis an open-source framework comprised of Split Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (i.e.,\nSplitLoRA\n) and Inference (i.e.,\nSplitInfer\n) for foundation models.\nSplitFM\nframework supports four foundation model: DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2-V, LLlama3, and GTP-2.\nDecember 2024: Our two new paper accepted at\nIEEE INFOCOM 2025\n!\nNovember 2024: Our three paper accepted at\nIEEE TMC\n!\nJuly 2024: We release the source code of our\nSplitLoRA\nproject!\nJuly 2024: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE TDSC\n!\nJune 2024: Our\nSplitLoRA\nproject, a split parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework for Large Language Models (LLMs) has been launched, and we will release the source code soon.\nJune 2024: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nACM MobiCom 2024\n!\nFebruary 2024: Our new paper accepted at\nACM MobiSys 2024\n!\nDecember 2023: Our two new paper accepted at\nIEEE INFOCOM 2024\n!\nAugust 2023: Our new paper accepted at\nACM CCS 2023\n!\nAugust 2023: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE JSAC\n!\nAugust 2023: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE IoT-J\n!\nJuly 2023: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE/CVF ICCV 2023\n!\nJuly 2023: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE S&P 2024\n!\nJune 2023: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nACM MobiCom 2023\n!\nDecember 2022: Our two new paper accepted at\nIEEE INFOCOM 2023\n!\nNovember 2022: Our new paper accepted at\nACM TOSN\n!\nNovember 2022: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nACM MobiCom 2023\n!\nOctober 2022: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE Communications Magazine\n!\nSeptember 2022: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE TNSE\n!\nSeptember 2022: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE TVT\n!\nSeptember 2022: Our new paper accepted at\nACM SenSys 2022\n!\nAugust 2022: Our new paper accepted at\nIEEE TMC 2022\n!\nJune 2022: Our new paper conditionally accepted at\nACM MobiCom 2022\n!\nDecember 2021: Our two new papers accepted at\nIEEE INFOCOM 2022\n!\nOctober 2021: Our new paper accepted at\nACM SenSys 2021\n!\nAugust 2021: Our three new papers accepted at\nACM MobiCom 2021\n!\nVisitors"
  },
