    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Chun Yuan  |  UW Center for Cardiovascular Biology\nSkip to main content\nUniversity of Washington\nCenter for\nCardiovascular Biology\nFaculty\nEMAIL:\ncyuan@\nPH:\n206-616-9346\nChun Yuan\nSouth Lake Union\nChun Yuan, Ph.D., is a Professor in the Departments of Radiology, Electrical Engineering, and Bioengineering; the Co-Director of the Vascular Imaging Lab since 1997 and Director of the Bio-Molecular Imaging Center at the University of Washington since 2006. He received his B.S. in physics in 1982 at Beijing Normal University and his Ph.D. in Biomedical Physics at the University of Utah in 1988. He has pioneered multiple high-resolution MRI techniques to detect vulnerable atherosclerotic plaques and led numerous MRI studies examining carotid atherosclerosis. He is the Director of the newly formed Center for BioMedical Imaging Research in Tsinghua University in Beijing. He is also a member of the editorial board for the Journals: JACC CV imaging, Journal of Cardiovascular MR, and the Journal of Geriatric Cardiology.\nDr. Yuan is a fellow of the American Heart Association and the ISMRM. He is a member of the program committee of the AHA Council on Cardiovascular Research and Intervention and of the award committee of the ISMRM. Because of his expertise in the field of cardiovascular imaging and magnetic resonance, he has been an invited speaker, lecturer, moderator or faculty for many international and US meetings in the imaging field. He has over 250 articles in peer-reviewed journals and is a grant reviewer for the National Institutes of Health, Canada Research Chairs, Scottish Clinical Academic Fellowship, VA Merit Grant, Canadian Institute of Health Research, Wellcome Trust, AHA and RSNA. His research is supported by several NIH and private grants and he supervises several postdoctoral and foreign fellows and research scientists, as well as teaches courses in Bioengineering and Radiology.\nUniversity of Washington\nBe boundless\nConnect with us:\n© 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA\n\n---\n\nAbout the CCB  |  UW Center for Cardiovascular Biology\nSkip to main content\nUniversity of Washington\nCenter for\nCardiovascular Biology\nAbout the CCB\nThe Center for Cardiovascular Biology (CCB\n) at the University of Washington is a multidisciplinary research community focused on injury and repair of the human heart. Founded in 2003, the CCB fosters collaborations that explore the molecular mechanisms of disease and translate discoveries into potential treatments for the world’s leading cause of death. The center’s 28 core faculty members represent seven departments from the schools of Medicine and Engineering and operates the university’s longest-running seminar series – the CCB Breakfast Club.\nWho We Are\nWe are a diverse group of scientists with a common goal: to improve human health through collaborative cardiovascular research. We are biologists, engineers, and clinicians. We are problem-solvers, public servants, and early adopters. We are a training village. We are experts across scales and spaces. We are passionate about health equity and sharing knowledge. We are restless and relentless.\nMission and Vision\nEach year, heart disease contributes to 18 million deaths worldwide and drains more than $200 billion from the U.S. economy. Yet, there has been just one approved drug in twenty years. We embrace this  call to action.\nToday, the CCB is poised to build on the University of Washington’s legacy of leadership in cardiovascular research. We are attacking the problem at the point of impact, leveraging advances in technology, building a pipeline from the lab to the clinic and the private sector, publishing game-changing research, partnering with peer institutes, and recruiting and retaining talent at all levels who share our commitment to basic science and community health.\nUniversity of Washington\nBe boundless\nConnect with us:\n© 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA\n\n---\n\nAbout the CCB  |  UW Center for Cardiovascular Biology\nSkip to main content\nUniversity of Washington\nCenter for\nCardiovascular Biology\nAbout the CCB\nThe Center for Cardiovascular Biology (CCB\n) at the University of Washington is a multidisciplinary research community focused on injury and repair of the human heart. Founded in 2003, the CCB fosters collaborations that explore the molecular mechanisms of disease and translate discoveries into potential treatments for the world’s leading cause of death. The center’s 28 core faculty members represent seven departments from the schools of Medicine and Engineering and operates the university’s longest-running seminar series – the CCB Breakfast Club.\nWho We Are\nWe are a diverse group of scientists with a common goal: to improve human health through collaborative cardiovascular research. We are biologists, engineers, and clinicians. We are problem-solvers, public servants, and early adopters. We are a training village. We are experts across scales and spaces. We are passionate about health equity and sharing knowledge. We are restless and relentless.\nMission and Vision\nEach year, heart disease contributes to 18 million deaths worldwide and drains more than $200 billion from the U.S. economy. Yet, there has been just one approved drug in twenty years. We embrace this  call to action.\nToday, the CCB is poised to build on the University of Washington’s legacy of leadership in cardiovascular research. We are attacking the problem at the point of impact, leveraging advances in technology, building a pipeline from the lab to the clinic and the private sector, publishing game-changing research, partnering with peer institutes, and recruiting and retaining talent at all levels who share our commitment to basic science and community health.\nUniversity of Washington\nBe boundless\nConnect with us:\n© 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
  },
