    "homepage_text": "Qingqi Zhang - Harvard EPS\nSkip to content\nSearch for:\nGraduate Student\nQingqi Zhang\nGraduate Student\nResearch Areas\nClimate, Atmosphere, and Oceans\nResearch Group\nKuang Group\nQingqi is a graduate student in the Kuang Group.\nContact Information\nqingqizhang@fas.harvard.edu\n\n---\n\nContact - Harvard EPS\nSkip to content\nSearch for:\nContact\nVisit Us!\nDepartment of Earth and Planetary Sciences\n20 Oxford Street\nCambridge, MA 02138\nTel (617) 495-2351\nDirections\nIf you have questions or would like more information about EPS, please contact\nAndrea-Marie Moore\nor\nCampbell Halligan\n. We would love to hear from you!\nThe entrance to the Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences is located at 20 Oxford Street (\nHoffman Lab\n), Cambridge, Massachusetts.\nWe strongly urge using\npublic transportation\n. However, if you are planning to drive, please be advised that parking near the department is very limited and in the case of university-owned lots, requires a pre-paid day permit. Please read the parking section below about where and how to park near the department.\nBy Public Transportation\nTaking the\nMBTA Subway (or the “T”)\nTake the Alewife Red Line to the Harvard Square stop. Exit through the turnstyles and up the stairway that leads to Harvard Yard, and walk into Harvard Yard to the statue of John Harvard. From here, take the path to your left, exit the yard, and veer slightly to the right. The Science Center (a white granite structure with a rock formation in front) will be on your left, and Memorial Hall (a large red brick building with a tall steeple) will be on your right. If Memorial Hall is on your left, you are walking in the wrong direction. This will take you to the intersection of Kirkland and Oxford. Proceed left (westward) down Oxford Street. The main entrance to the department is on the right side of Oxford, at 20 Oxford Street.\nBy Car\nParking Information\nOn-Street Metered Parking\nThere are limited 2-hour metered spaces available on Oxford and Kirkland Streets.\nCommercial Parking\n: Garages are in Harvard Square, but please be advised that the Department cannot validate parking for those businesses.\nUniversity Day-Permit Parking\n: Limited parking is available for department visitors in nearby University-operated parking garages. Day permits can be purchased up to two weeks in advance, whether online or in person at the Campus Service Center, located at the Smith Center, 8th floor, 1350 Massachusetts Avenue.\nPurchasing a Parking Pass Online\nA limited number of Daily Visitor Parking Permits are\navailable for purchase online\n. All sales are final (no refunds or exchanges). Before proceeding to the online form, please note the following:\nIf you are a first-time user, you will need to register by creating a username and password. Please have your car’s license plate number ready. No permit can be purchased without a current plate number. We recommend requesting a permit for the 52 Oxford Street Garage. You will be asked for the date of the permit. Be sure to select the correct date (permits are not transferable). You will need to choose the Earth and Planetary Sciences department from a drop-down list. You will need to enter the Department of Earth and Planetary Science’s department code: 3027.\nDriving Directions\nFrom the North\nIf you are coming by car from the north, take route 93 or 1 south toward Boston. Take the exit for Storrow Drive-Cambridge. Follow Storrow Drive west for approximately 4 miles. After approximately 2 miles, Storrow Drive becomes Soldiers Field road. You will not notice any change. Stay in the right-hand lane to avoid the many left-hand exits off Storrow Drive until you reach the exit marked Harvard Square/Cambridge. Take the exit and go over the Charles River to the set of lights at Memorial Drive. Go straight ahead through the lights. You will now be on JFK Street and are entering Harvard Square. After you pass the intersection of JFK and Mt Auburn Street, you will come to another set of lights where The Coop is on the left, and Out of Town News and the subway “T” stop are on your right. Go straight (veering slightly to the right) through these lights. You will then approach another fork in the road. Stay to the left of the fork, and then immediately get into the right-hand lane. This will take you onto Massachusetts Avenue, headed north. Go approximately 0.2 tenths of a mile past the Harvard Law School to Everett Street on your right. At the light at Everett Street, turn right and go to the next set of lights at Oxford Street. Take a right onto Oxford Street. Metered parking spaces are on the right.\nFrom the South\nTake route 3 (South East Expressway) north and follow the above directions for Storrow Drive West.\nFrom the West\nTake route 90 (Massachusetts Turnpike) east. Get off the Cambridge/Allston exit (#18) and follow signs for Cambridge, bearing to the right. As you come off of the Turnpike exit, a 10-story hotel is on your right. Begin to move to the left lane as you pass the hotel. There is a traffic light at the corner of the hotel. Go through this light, but be prepared to make a left before the bridge (do not cross over the bridge). You will now be on an entrance ramp leading onto Storrow Drive with very rapid traffic. Follow Storrow Drive for approximately half a mile. Stay in the right-hand lane, which will become the Harvard Square/Cambridge exit on your right. Follow the directions given above for coming from the north starting with the sign Take the exit and go over the Charles River.\n\n---\n\nContact - Harvard EPS\nSkip to content\nSearch for:\nContact\nVisit Us!\nDepartment of Earth and Planetary Sciences\n20 Oxford Street\nCambridge, MA 02138\nTel (617) 495-2351\nDirections\nIf you have questions or would like more information about EPS, please contact\nAndrea-Marie Moore\nor\nCampbell Halligan\n. We would love to hear from you!\nThe entrance to the Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences is located at 20 Oxford Street (\nHoffman Lab\n), Cambridge, Massachusetts.\nWe strongly urge using\npublic transportation\n. However, if you are planning to drive, please be advised that parking near the department is very limited and in the case of university-owned lots, requires a pre-paid day permit. Please read the parking section below about where and how to park near the department.\nBy Public Transportation\nTaking the\nMBTA Subway (or the “T”)\nTake the Alewife Red Line to the Harvard Square stop. Exit through the turnstyles and up the stairway that leads to Harvard Yard, and walk into Harvard Yard to the statue of John Harvard. From here, take the path to your left, exit the yard, and veer slightly to the right. The Science Center (a white granite structure with a rock formation in front) will be on your left, and Memorial Hall (a large red brick building with a tall steeple) will be on your right. If Memorial Hall is on your left, you are walking in the wrong direction. This will take you to the intersection of Kirkland and Oxford. Proceed left (westward) down Oxford Street. The main entrance to the department is on the right side of Oxford, at 20 Oxford Street.\nBy Car\nParking Information\nOn-Street Metered Parking\nThere are limited 2-hour metered spaces available on Oxford and Kirkland Streets.\nCommercial Parking\n: Garages are in Harvard Square, but please be advised that the Department cannot validate parking for those businesses.\nUniversity Day-Permit Parking\n: Limited parking is available for department visitors in nearby University-operated parking garages. Day permits can be purchased up to two weeks in advance, whether online or in person at the Campus Service Center, located at the Smith Center, 8th floor, 1350 Massachusetts Avenue.\nPurchasing a Parking Pass Online\nA limited number of Daily Visitor Parking Permits are\navailable for purchase online\n. All sales are final (no refunds or exchanges). Before proceeding to the online form, please note the following:\nIf you are a first-time user, you will need to register by creating a username and password. Please have your car’s license plate number ready. No permit can be purchased without a current plate number. We recommend requesting a permit for the 52 Oxford Street Garage. You will be asked for the date of the permit. Be sure to select the correct date (permits are not transferable). You will need to choose the Earth and Planetary Sciences department from a drop-down list. You will need to enter the Department of Earth and Planetary Science’s department code: 3027.\nDriving Directions\nFrom the North\nIf you are coming by car from the north, take route 93 or 1 south toward Boston. Take the exit for Storrow Drive-Cambridge. Follow Storrow Drive west for approximately 4 miles. After approximately 2 miles, Storrow Drive becomes Soldiers Field road. You will not notice any change. Stay in the right-hand lane to avoid the many left-hand exits off Storrow Drive until you reach the exit marked Harvard Square/Cambridge. Take the exit and go over the Charles River to the set of lights at Memorial Drive. Go straight ahead through the lights. You will now be on JFK Street and are entering Harvard Square. After you pass the intersection of JFK and Mt Auburn Street, you will come to another set of lights where The Coop is on the left, and Out of Town News and the subway “T” stop are on your right. Go straight (veering slightly to the right) through these lights. You will then approach another fork in the road. Stay to the left of the fork, and then immediately get into the right-hand lane. This will take you onto Massachusetts Avenue, headed north. Go approximately 0.2 tenths of a mile past the Harvard Law School to Everett Street on your right. At the light at Everett Street, turn right and go to the next set of lights at Oxford Street. Take a right onto Oxford Street. Metered parking spaces are on the right.\nFrom the South\nTake route 3 (South East Expressway) north and follow the above directions for Storrow Drive West.\nFrom the West\nTake route 90 (Massachusetts Turnpike) east. Get off the Cambridge/Allston exit (#18) and follow signs for Cambridge, bearing to the right. As you come off of the Turnpike exit, a 10-story hotel is on your right. Begin to move to the left lane as you pass the hotel. There is a traffic light at the corner of the hotel. Go through this light, but be prepared to make a left before the bridge (do not cross over the bridge). You will now be on an entrance ramp leading onto Storrow Drive with very rapid traffic. Follow Storrow Drive for approximately half a mile. Stay in the right-hand lane, which will become the Harvard Square/Cambridge exit on your right. Follow the directions given above for coming from the north starting with the sign Take the exit and go over the Charles River.\n\n---\n\nAbout - Harvard EPS\nSkip to content\nSearch for:\nAbout\nDrawing on a range of scientific disciplines, we conduct innovative & interdisciplinary research that helps answer fundamental questions about our world. With exceptional faculty, state-of-the-art facilities, and a collaborative environment, we offer students the opportunity to take on the most pressing issues of our time.\nWelcome!\nOur research and teaching covers topics ranging from earthquakes and volcanic eruptions to heat waves, floods, and droughts. At the heart of our work is the goal of understanding the fundamental processes that drive these natural phenomena and shape Earth’s systems. Our approach integrates insights from physics, chemistry, biology, statistics, and mathematics, and our focus areas span from Earth’s core to the stratosphere and onto other planets.\nWe hope you find this website helpful as you explore who we are and what we do. Please feel free to reach out if you have any questions or need more information.\nAll the best,\nPeter Huybers\nChair, Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences\nphuybers@fas.harvard.edu\nOur Mission\nOur department is at the forefront of scientific discovery about the Earth system, helping to address pressing societal issues through research and education. We embrace our central role at the University as an integrator of the physical and life sciences, pure and applied, and seek to further strengthen our interactions with those that share a sense of stewardship for the Earth and the environment.\nExplore With US\nOur Values\nThe Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences (EPS) and the program in Environmental Science and Engineering (ESE) constitute a vibrant academic, professional, and social environment for those interested in the study of Earth and Planetary Sciences and Environmental Science and Engineering.\nA healthy and positive work environment is an essential foundation for teaching, learning, and undertaking cutting-edge research. Where we work and how we interact with those around us are an important influence on the quality of our work. Having a diverse and inclusive community, where every member is valued and treated with respect, is a central goal. All members of our community should have a rewarding experience with equal opportunities for success.\nTo this end, a representative committee is in the process of soliciting community feedback to elaborate on how we uphold these values.\nLearn More About Our Values\nJoin Us\nJoin the EPS community and take a look at our employment opportunities.\nEmployment Opportunities\n\n---\n\nAbout - Harvard EPS\nSkip to content\nSearch for:\nAbout\nDrawing on a range of scientific disciplines, we conduct innovative & interdisciplinary research that helps answer fundamental questions about our world. With exceptional faculty, state-of-the-art facilities, and a collaborative environment, we offer students the opportunity to take on the most pressing issues of our time.\nWelcome!\nOur research and teaching covers topics ranging from earthquakes and volcanic eruptions to heat waves, floods, and droughts. At the heart of our work is the goal of understanding the fundamental processes that drive these natural phenomena and shape Earth’s systems. Our approach integrates insights from physics, chemistry, biology, statistics, and mathematics, and our focus areas span from Earth’s core to the stratosphere and onto other planets.\nWe hope you find this website helpful as you explore who we are and what we do. Please feel free to reach out if you have any questions or need more information.\nAll the best,\nPeter Huybers\nChair, Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences\nphuybers@fas.harvard.edu\nOur Mission\nOur department is at the forefront of scientific discovery about the Earth system, helping to address pressing societal issues through research and education. We embrace our central role at the University as an integrator of the physical and life sciences, pure and applied, and seek to further strengthen our interactions with those that share a sense of stewardship for the Earth and the environment.\nExplore With US\nOur Values\nThe Department of Earth and Planetary Sciences (EPS) and the program in Environmental Science and Engineering (ESE) consti...[truncated]"
