    "homepage_text": "Dr.. Tai Sing Lee's Homepage\nDr. Tai Sing Lee\nProfessor in Computer Science\nComputer Science Department\nand Center for the Neural Basis of Cognition\nCarnegie Mellon University\nPittsburgh, PA\n15213\nOffice: Mellon Institute Rm 115\nPhone: 412-268-1060\nEmail: tai at cnbc dot cmu dot edu\nFaculty member in the\nComputer Science Department\n,\nCenter for the Neural Basis of \nCognition\n, and\nMachine Learning Department\n, Carnegie Mellon University, and \nadjunct faculty member of the\nDepartment of Neuroscience, University of Pittsburgh\n.\nDirector of Lee Laboratory for Biological and Machine Intelligence Research at Carnegie Mellon\nDirector of Intercolledge Undergraduate Minor in Neural Computation, Carnegie Mellon University.\nCoordinator of\nUndergraduate Training fellowship in Computational Neuroscience (Program in Neural Computation).\nDirector of Peking University - Carnegie Mellon University Summer Program in Computer Science, School of Computer Science,\nDirector of Tsinghua University - Carnegie Mellon University Summer Program in Computer Science, School of Computer Science\nResearch interests\n:\ncomputational neuroscience,\ncomputational vision, neurophysiology of the primate visual systems,\nactive and adaptive vision, hierarchical coding and inference, \nmid-level vision, development of infant vision, learning and adaptation, structure of neural codes.\nCourses and Seminars\nArtificial Intelligence (15-381/681)\nFall 2017\nMachine Learning from Neural Cortical Circuits (15-874)\nSpring 2016\nNeural Computation (15-386/686)\nSpring 2023\nSpring 2024\nSpring 2025\nSpring 2026\nComputer Vision (15-385/685)\nSpring 2011\n,\nSpring 2013.\nComputational Perception (86-375/675) (15-387)\nFall 2020\n,\nFall 2021\nFall 2022\n,\nFall 2023\nFall 2024\n,\nFall 2025\nComputational Neuroscience of Vision (86-712)\nFall 2012\nAdvanced Topics in Computational Neuroscience\n(2002-2008) with Mike Lewicki\nLee Lab meeting Presentations\nProjects\nProject MICrONS at Carnegie Mellon.\nCarnegie Mellon joins IARPA project to reverse engineering brain algorithms .\nEducation\nS.B. Engineering Physics, Harvard College (1986)\n.\nPh.D. Engineering Sciences, Harvard University (1993)\n.\nPh.D. Medical Physics and Engineering, Harvard-MIT Division of Health Science and Technology (1993)\nPostdoc. Biomedical Physics and Neurophysiology, Harvard University (DAS) and Massachusetts Institute of Technology (BCS) (1993-96)\n.\nHonors\nSociety of Harvard Engineers and Scientists Honor Scholar (1985)\n.\nHarvard-MIT HST Fellowship Award (1986)\n.\nMcDonnell Pew Foundation Young Investigator Research Award (1996)\n.\nNSF Faculty Career Development (CAREER) Award (2000)\n.\nICCV  Helmholtz prize (2013)\n.\nCurrent Postdoctoral Fellows\nCurrent Ph.D. and M.S. Students\nGe (Summer) Huang\nNeural Computation, Carnegie Mellon. (on leave)\nTianqin Li\nComputer Science, Carnegie Mellon.\nZhiwen Bian\nComputational Biology, Carnegie Mellon.\nJinyi Ye\nComputational Biology, Carnegie Mellon.\nXirui Liu\nComputational Biology, Carnegie Mellon.\nYue Li\nArtificial intelligence engineeering, Carnegie Mellon.\nYingjue Bian\nBiomedical Engineering, Carnegie Mellon.\nDongfeng Li\nBiomedical Engineering, Carnegie Mellon.\nYize Wu\nBiomedical Engineering, Carnegie Mellon.\nDavid Wang\nComputer Science, Carnegie Mellon.\nYuxuan Zhu\nComputaitonal Biology, Carnegie Mellon.\nHaifei Guo\nBiomedical Engineering, Carnegie Mellon.\nCurrent Undergraduate Research Fellows\nIssac Lin\n, Computer Science, Carnegie Mellon, CMU\nAlan Ramirez\n, Computer Science, Carnegie Mellon, CMU\nGeorge Liu\n, Computer Science, Carnegie Mellon, CMU\nSoham Raychaudhuri\n, Computer Science, Carnegie Mellon, CMU\nRonav Jaiwal\nComputer Science, Carnegie Mellon.\nHusain Raja\nComputer Science, Carnegie Mellon.\nOm Kathira\nNeuroscience, Carnegie Mellon.\nFormer Ph.D. and M.S. Students\nStella Yu\n, Ph.D. Robotics 2002, CMU. Assistant professor in computer science, Boston college, MA.\nBrian Potetz\n, Ph.D. Computer Science, 2008, CMU. Assistant professor in computer science, University of \nKansas. Google Research CA.\nRyan Kelly\n, Ph.D. Computer Science, 2010, CMU. Google Research New York.\nTom Stepleton\n, Ph.D. Robotics 2010, CMU. Deep Mind, London.\nXiong Li\n, Ph.D. Automation 2013, Shanghai JiaoTong. Start-up. China\nYimeng Zhang\n, Ph.D. Computer Science, 2021. Carnegie Mellon. Pinterest.\nAndrew Luo\nNeural Computation, 2021. Ph.D. program, Carnegie Mellon.\nLiyuan Liang\nNeural Computation, 2021. Ph.D. program, Carnegie Mellon.\nRichard Romero\n, M.S. Computer Science. 1999. Co-Founder,\nEizel Technology\nRyan Poplin\n, M.S. in Neural Computation. 2009. Broad Institute, Boston. Google Research\nGaya Mohankumar\n, M.S. 2017. in Biomedical engineering, Boston.\nEsha Uboweja\n, M.S. 2015. in Computer Science and Computer Vision, Amazon, Google Research.\nShefali Umrania\n, M.S. 2017. in Computational Biology, Boston.\nJingyuan Li\n, M.S. in Biomedical engineering, 2019. Ph.D. program in Biomedical Engineering, University of Washington.\nYuanyuan Wei\n, M.S. in EECS, Ph.D. program in Biomedical Engineering, 2019. SUSTech- University of British Columbia.\nMert Inan\nM.S. Computational Biology, 2019. Carnegie Mellon. Ph.D. program in University of Pittsburgh\nJiaxin Wang\nM.S. Computaitonal Biology, 2021. Carnegie Mellon.\nYusen Zhu\nM.S. Biomedical Engineering, 2021. Carnegie Mellon. Ph.D. program at George Tech\nXiaoqi Zhang\nM.S. Computaitonal Biology, 2021. Carnegie Mellon. Ph.D. program at Purdue\nSicheng Dai\nM.S. Biomedical Engineering, 2022. Carnegie Mellon. Ph.D Program Chinese Academy of Sciences.\nMirudhula Mukundan\nM.S. Comutational Biology 2022, Carnegie Mellon. Biotech\nZitong Wang\nMS. Biomedical Engineering, 2023. Carnegie Mellon. Ph.D. Program University of Pittsburgh.\nYung Ying Chen\nMS. Biomedical Engineering, 2023. Carnegie Mellon. Ph.D. Program at Biomedical Engineering, GeorgiaTech\nZiqi Wen\nM.S.