    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/baio002",
    "homepage_text": "Yikun Bai\nYikun\nBai\nPhysical AI Postdoc Fellow @\nPurdue University\n. West Lafayette, IN, U.S.\nMath Department, Purdue University\nWest Lafayette, IN, U.S.\nAbout Me\nI am currently a Physical AI Postdoc Fellow at Purdue University, supervised by Dr.\nGuang Lin\nand Dr.\nRuqi Zhang\n. Prior to Purdue, I was a postdoc in the Department of Computer Science at Vanderbilt University (2022-2025), supervised by Dr.\nSoheil Kolouri\n. I received my Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering from the University of Delaware in 2022, where I studied machine learning and statistics under the guidance of Dr.\nDominique Guillot\n.\nCurrent Research Interests\nDiscrete and Continuous Flow Matching / Diffusion Models:\nI study both discrete-time and continuous-time formulations of flow matching and diffusion models, with a focus on probability path design, transport dynamics, and scalable training/sampling algorithms for high-dimensional or non-Euclidean data.\nReinforcement Learning for Generative Models:\nI am interested in using RL methods to improve generative modeling pipelines, including reward-driven fine-tuning, policy optimization for generation quality/control, and learning strategies that connect exploration with robust sample synthesis.\nReinforcement Learning for Decision-Making Over Time (toward quant research):\nI work on RL for sequential and temporal decision problems under uncertainty, with long-term interests in applications aligned with quantitative researcher roles in hedge funds and related data-driven decision-making jobs.\nNews\nJan 8, 2025\nFour papers accapted by\nICLR 2024\n.\nLinear Spherical Sliced Optimal Transport: A Fast Metric for Comparing Spherical Data\nExpected sliced transport plans\nLinear Partial Gromov-Wasserstein Embedding\nPartial Gromov Wasserstein\nJun 1, 2024\nOne paper\nStereographic Spherical Sliced Wasserstein Distances\naccapted by\nICML2024\nNov 1, 2023\nOur paper\nLCOT: Linear circular optimal transport\nhas been accapted by\nICLR2023\n.\nOct 1, 2023\nPresentation at\nKorea Institute for Adavanced study\n-\nMore Infor\nAug 1, 2023\nOur paper\nPTLp: Partial Transport Lp Distances\nhas been accapted by\nNeurips2023\nOT workshop.\n\"Glory to my Lord\"\nYou can even add a little note about which of these is the best way to reach you.\n\n---\n\ncv | Yikun Bai\ncv\nTable of contents\nGeneral Information\nWork Experience\nEducation\nHonors and Awards\nTechnical Skills\nResearch Interests\nProfessional Activities\nOther Interests\nGeneral Information\nFull Name\nYikun Bai\nLanguages\nEnglish, Mandarin\nEmail\nlaobai312@gmail.com\nAddress\nWest Lafayette, IN, USA\nPhone\n+1 (302) 244-8486\nWork Experience\n2026 - present\nPhysical AI Postdoc Fellow\nPurdue University, West Lafayette, IN\nDiscrete/Continuous flow matching and diffusion models\nReinforcement learning methods for agentic AI systems\n2022 - 2025\nPostdoctoral Research Assistant\nDepartment of Computer Science, Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN\nResearch on computational optimal transport and its applications\nUnbalanced optimal transport theory and algorithms\nMachine learning applications in computer vision and data science\nCollaboration with interdisciplinary research teams\nEducation\n2017 - 2022\nPh.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering\nUniversity of Delaware, Newark, DE\nGPA 4.0/4.0\nDissertation: Optimal Transport in Information Theory and Machine Learning\nRelevant Coursework\nInformation Theory and Coding\nMachine Learning and Pattern Recognition\nStatistical Learning Theory\nConvex Optimization\n2017 - 2019\nM.S. in Applied Mathematics\nUniversity of Delaware, Newark, DE\nGPA 4.0/4.0\nTransferred to Ph.D. program in ECE\nRelevant Coursework\nNumerical Analysis\nPartial Differential Equations\nProbability Theory and Stochastic Processes\nFunctional Analysis\n2007 - 2011\nB.S. in Medical Imaging\nMudanjiang Medical University, Mudanjiang, China\nHonors and Awards\n2025\nAdvanced Research in Physical AI Fellowship (Purdue University)\nGoogle Cloud Research Award\nLambda AI Research Grant\n2023\nSEAM Travel Grant (Clemson University)\nOutstanding Postdoc Award (Vanderbilt University)\n2022\nOutstanding Ph.D. Dissertation Award (University of Delaware)\nGraduate Research Excellence Award\n2021\nECE Seminar Best Presentation Award (University of Delaware)\nGraduate Student Travel Grant\n2020\nDean's List for Academic Excellence\n2017-2022\nGraduate Research Assistantship (University of Delaware)\nTechnical Skills\nProgramming Languages\nPython (NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib, Pandas)\nMATLAB\nR\nC/C++\nMachine Learning Frameworks\nPyTorch\nTensorFlow\nScikit-learn\nKeras\nMathematical Software\nMathematica\nMaple\nLaTeX\nGit/GitHub\nResearch Interests\nFlow Matching\nContinuous-time generative modeling\nProbability path design and transport dynamics\nScalable training and sampling algorithms\nDiffusion Models\nDiscrete and continuous diffusion processes\nScore-based modeling and inverse problems\nTheory and applications in scientific machine learning\nReinforcement Learning\nPolicy optimization and value-based methods\nModel-based reinforcement learning\nDecision-making under uncertainty\nProfessional Activities\n2022-present\nMember of SIAM (Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics)\nMember of IEEE Signal Processing Society\nOther Interests\nHobbies:\nAnime, Hiking, Reading\nSports:\nBasketball, Swimming\nLanguages:\nNative Mandarin, Fluent English"
