  "deep_homepage::Jin Huang::https://sites.northwestern.edu/jinhuang/": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.northwestern.edu/jinhuang/",
    "all_emails": [
      "jinhuang@northwestern.edu"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/jin-huang-672411130?utm_source=share&utm_campaign=share_via&utm_content=profile&utm_medium=ios_app",
    "homepage_text": "Homepage | Jin Huang\nSkip to main content\nNorthwestern University\nParent Unit Name\nJin Huang\nSearch this site\nSearch\nMenu\nSearch\nSearch this site\nSearch\nWelcome to my website\nHomepage\nJin Huang, Ph.D.\nI am an IIN Postdoctoral Fellow at the\nDepartment of Chemistry\nand the\nInternational Institute for Nanotechnology\nat Northwestern University supervised by\nProf. Chad A. Mirkin\n. I earned my Ph.D. in\nMaterials Science and Engineering\nfrom UCLA in 2021 under\nProf. Yu Huang\n’s supervision. Prior to UCLA, I completed my Bachelor’s degree in the School of Materials Science and Engineering at Wuhan University of Technology, under the supervision of\nProf. Rong Tu\n, in 2016.\nDuring my Ph.D. term with Prof. Yu Huang at UCLA, I focused on: (1) leveraging fundamental insights and experimental techniques (\nin situ\nspectroscopy and other advanced characterization methods) to develop experimental descriptors that elucidate design rules for electrocatalysts, (2) developing surface modification strategies to modulate the physisorption of reaction species to enhance reaction kinetics, and (3) developing synthesis methods for novel nanostructures as highly efficient electrocatalysts. Specifically, I developed a\nbinary experimental descriptor (BED)\n, , which integrates theoretical modeling with spectroscopic features to guide the design of active and stable oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) catalysts in proton-exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs). Based on this BED, I successfully designed and synthesized promising catalyst candidates, including\nslow-dealloyed PtNiCo\nthat achieved high activity and stability simultaneously,\nultrathin PtCo nanowires\nshowed record-high mass activity in fuel cells, and\ncore-shell tetrahedra PtCuNi\nthat functioned as efficient and multifunctional catalysts, and stable nanotwinned PtCo catalysts. In addition, I discovered a\n“surface molecular pump”\nthat could be used to tailor the catalyst-electrolyte interface and enhance surface microkinetics, leading to unprecedented specific activity in ORR.\nBringing my expertise in electrocatalysis, nanomaterials, and characterization, I joined Prof. Chad A. Mirkin’s group at Northwestern University as a postdoctoral fellow, where I pioneered a new research direction by creating and implementing a\n“megalibrary”\nplatform, which\naccelerates materials discovery\n. and advances the mechanistic understanding of catalytic properties. Massively parallel scanning probe lithography and post-processing was used to generate hundreds of millions of distinct nanomaterials on a centimeter-scale chip. I led projects that employed megalibraries to discover\nnew Ir-free\nand low-Ir catalysts for the acidic oxygen evolution reaction (OER), a critical anode reaction in aqueous electrochemical systems. These catalysts demonstrated excellent performance in both three-electrode setups and proton-exchange membrane electrolyzers. Importantly, the datasets generated are now being used to train AI models to predict the catalytic properties of materials, creating a closed-loop framework between synthesis, evaluation, and design.\nSelected Publications\n1.\nAccelerating the pace of OER catalyst discovery through megalibraries\nJin Huang\n†\n,\nZhe Wang†, Jiashun Liang†, Xiao-Yan Li, Jacob Pietryga, Zihao Ye, Peter T. Smith, Alp Kulaksizoglu, Connor McCormick, Jaerim Kim, Bosi Peng, Zeyan Liu, Ke Xie, Steven B. Torrisi, Joseph H. Montoya, Gang Wu, Edward H. Sargent* and Chad A. Mirkin*,\nJournal of the American Chemical Society,\n2025\n, 147\n, 34\n, 30956–30966\n2. Synthesis of PtCo nanotwinned catalysts via laser ablation\nJin Huang\n†, Yu-Han (Joseph) Tsai†, Shiqi Zheng, Ao Zhang, Sibo Wang, Heting Pu, Bosi Peng, Zeyan Liu, Ting-Jung Hsiao, Changyu Ma, Bingbing Li, Morris, Y. Wang, and Yu Huang*, (In press, Nano Research)\n3.\nSurface molecular pump enables ultrahigh catalyst activity\nJin Huang\n†\n,\nBosi Peng†, Cheng Zhu†, Mingjie Xu, Yang Liu, Zeyan Liu, Jingxuan\nZhou, Sibo Wang, Xiangfeng Duan, Hendrik Heinz*, and Yu Huang*,\nScience Advance,  10, eado3942, 2024\n4. Experimental Sab\natier plot for predictive design of active and stable Pt-alloy oxygen reduction reaction catalysts\nJin Huang\n,\nLuca Sementa, Zeyan Liu, Giovanni Barcaro, Miao Feng, Ershuai Liu, Li Jiao, Mingjie Xu, Denis Leshchev, Sung-joon Lee, Mufan Li, Chengzhang Wan, Enbo Zhu, Yang Liu, Bosi Peng, Xiangfeng Duan, William A. Goddard III*, Alessandro Fortunelli*, Qingying Jia*, and Yu Huang*,\nNature Catalysis,\n5\n,\n513–523\n,\n2022\nNature Catalysis news & views:\nSpectroscopy predicts catalyst functionality\nUCLA newsroom:\nUCLA-led study could be step toward cheaper hydrogen-based energy\n5. 1D PtCo nanowires as catalysts for PEMFCs with low Pt loading\nJin Huang\n†, Bosi Peng†, Zeyan Liu, Ao Zhang, Mingjie Xu, Yang Liu, Qingying Jia, Xiangfeng Duan, and Yu Huang*,\nScience China Materials\n,\n65\n, 704–711, 2022\n6.\nPtCuNi Tetrahedra Catalysts with Tailored Surfaces for Efficient Alcohol Oxidation\nJin Huang\n, Yang Liu, Mingjie Xu, Chengzhang Wan, Haotian Liu, Mufan Li, Zhihong Huang, Xiangfeng D\nuan, Xiao\nqing\nPan and Yu Huang*,\nNano Letters\n,\n19\n, 8, 5431-5436, 2019\n7. Transfer-free growth of graphene on Al\n2\nO\n3\n(0001) using a three-step method\nChitengfei Zhang†,\nJin Huang\n†, Rong Tu*, Song Zhang, Meijun Yang, Qizhong Li, Ji Shi, Haiwen Li, Lianmeng Zhang, Takashi Goto, Hitoshi Ohmori.,\nCarbon\n,\n131\n, 10-17, 2018\n\n---\n\nAbout me | Jin Huang\nSkip to main content\nNorthwestern University\nParent Unit Name\nJin Huang\nSearch this site\nSearch\nMenu\nSearch\nSearch this site\nSearch\nAbout me\nJin Huang, Ph.D.\nJin Huang, Ph.D.\nI am currently an IIN postdoctoral fellow at the Department of Chemistry, Northwestern University supervised by\nProf. Chad A. Mirkin\n. Before joining Northwestern University, I obtained my Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering at UCLA under\nProf. Yu Huang\n’s supervision in 2021 and I earned my Bachler degree in Materials Engineering at Wuhan University of Technology in 2016. My research is focused on the discovery of high-efficient and low-cost electrocatalysts for energy conversion that are critical to tackling daunting energy crisis and climate change challenges.\nLearn more\n\n---\n\nAbout me | Jin Huang\nSkip to main content\nNorthwestern University\nParent Unit Name\nJin Huang\nSearch this site\nSearch\nMenu\nSearch\nSearch this site\nSearch\nAbout me\nJin Huang, Ph.D.\nJin Huang, Ph.D.\nI am currently an IIN postdoctoral fellow at the Department of Chemistry, Northwestern University supervised by\nProf. Chad A. Mirkin\n. Before joining Northwestern University, I obtained my Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering at UCLA under\nProf. Yu Huang\n’s supervision in 2021 and I earned my Bachler degree in Materials Engineering at Wuhan University of Technology in 2016. My research is focused on the discovery of high-efficient and low-cost electrocatalysts for energy conversion that are critical to tackling daunting energy crisis and climate change challenges.\nLearn more"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Zeyu Tang::https://zeyu.one/": {
    "homepage": "https://zeyu.one/",
    "all_emails": [
      "tang@zeyu.one"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/zeyutang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/zeyu-tang-trustworthy-ai",
    "homepage_text": "Zeyu Tang's Homepage\nZeyu\n                        \n                        Tang\n(åæ³½å®)\nPostdoctoral Scholar\nStanford Trustworthy AI Research (STAIR)\nComputer Science Department\n,\nStanford University\nAbout Me\nI am a Postdoctoral Scholar in the\nComputer Science Department\nat\nStanford University\n, where I have the privilege of being advised by Prof.\nSanmi Koyejo\n, in the\nStanford Trustworthy AI Research (STAIR) Lab\n.\nResearch Interests\nI strive to advance\ntrustworthy and responsible AI\n.\nIn particular, I conduct research on\ncausal learning and reasoning\nto facilitate and enhance the capacity of intelligent systems, and also\nalgorithmic fairness\nand\ncomputational justice\nto model and understand the social impact of computational technologies.\nMy ultimate goal is to cultivate intelligence that is both safe and principled with the help of causal perspective and methodology, so that technology can improve our lives with transparent responsibility and clear purpose.\nI seek to foster a\nsymbiotic dance between artificial and natural intelligence\n, where they inspire, collaborate, and enhance each other to\ndrive scientific discovery\nand\nsupport societal progress\n.\nNews\nJanuary 2026\nWe are organizing the\nAlgorithmic Fairness Across Alignment Procedures and Agentic Systems (AFAA)\nWorkshop, which will take place at\nICLR 2026\n,\nApril 26, 2026\n, in\nRio de Janeiro, Brazil\n!\nMay 2025\nOur paper\nâReflection-Window Decoding: Text Generation with Selective Refinementâ\nis accepted to\nICML 2025\n.\nWe propose a\nselective refinement framework facilitated by the sliding reflection-window\nto address the sub-optimality of purely autoregressive way of LLM decoding.\nJanuary 2025\nOur paper\nâPrompting Fairness: Integrating Causality to Debias Large Language Modelsâ\nis accepted to\nICLR 2025\n.\nWe propose a\ncausality-guide LLM debiasing framework\n, utilizing\nselection mechanisms\nto design various debiasing strategies.\nSelected Publications\n* denotes equal contribution\narXiv\nPreprint\nPosition: Beyond Sensitive Attributes, ML Fairness Should Quantify Structural Injustice via Social Determinants\nZeyu Tang\n,Â\nAlex John London\n,Â\nAtoosa Kasirzadeh\n,Â\nSarah Stewart de Ramirez\n,Â\nPeter Spirtes\n,Â\nKun Zhang\n,Â andÂ\nSanmi Koyejo\narXiv preprint arXiv:2508.08337\n, 2025.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nAlgorithmic fairness research has largely framed unfairness as discrimination along sensitive attributes. However, this approach limits visibility into unfairness as structural injustice instantiated through social determinants, which are contextual variables that shape attributes and outcomes without pertaining to specific individuals. This position paper argues that the field should quantify structural injustice via social determinants, beyond sensitive attributes. Drawing on cross-disciplinary insights, we argue that prevailing technical paradigms fail to adequately capture unfairness as structural injustice, because contexts are potentially treated as noise to be normalized rather than signal to be audited. We further demonstrate the practical urgency of this shift through a theoretical model of college admissions, a demographic study using U.S. census data, and a high-stakes domain application regarding breast cancer screening within an integrated U.S. healthcare system. Our results indicate that mitigation strategies centered solely on sensitive attributes can introduce new forms of structural injustice. We contend that auditing structural injustice through social determinants must precede mitigation, and call for new technical developments that move beyond sensitive-attribute-centered notions of fairness as non-discrimination.\nICML\nReflection-Window Decoding: Text Generation with Selective Refinement\nZeyu Tang*\n,Â\nZhenhao Chen*\n,Â\nXiangchen Song\n,Â\nLoka Li\n,Â\nYunlong Deng\n,Â\nYifan Shen\n,Â\nGuangyi Chen\n,Â\nPeter Spirtes\n,Â andÂ\nKun Zhang\nIn\nForty-Second International Conference on Machine Learning\n, 2025.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nThe autoregressive decoding for text generation in large language models (LLMs), while widely used, is inherently suboptimal due to the lack of a built-in mechanism to perform refinement and/or correction of the generated content. In this paper, we consider optimality in terms of the joint probability over the generated response, when jointly considering all tokens at the same time. We theoretically characterize the potential deviation of the autoregressively generated response from its globally optimal counterpart that is of the same length. Our analysis suggests that we need to be cautious when noticeable uncertainty arises during text generation, which may signal the sub-optimality of the generation history. To address the pitfall of autoregressive decoding for text generation, we propose an approach that incorporates a sliding reflection window and a pausing criterion, such that refinement and generation can be carried out interchangeably as the decoding proceeds. Our selective refinement framework strikes a balance between efficiency and optimality, and our extensive experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach.\nICLR\nPrompting Fairness: Integrating Causality to Debias Large Language Models\nJingling Li*\n,Â\nZeyu Tang*\n,Â\nXiaoyu Liu\n,Â\nPeter Spirtes\n,Â\nKun Zhang\n,Â\nLiu Leqi\n,Â andÂ\nYang Liu\nIn\nThirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (preliminary version titled \"Steering LLMs Towards Unbiased Responses: A Causality-Guided Debiasing Framework\")\n, 2025.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nLarge language models (LLMs), despite their remarkable capabilities, are susceptible to generating biased and discriminatory responses. As LLMs increasingly influence high-stakes decision-making (e.g., hiring and healthcare), mitigating these biases becomes critical. In this work, we propose a causality-guided debiasing framework to tackle social biases, aiming to reduce the objectionable dependence between LLMsâ decisions and the social information in the input. Our framework introduces a novel perspective to identify how social information can affect an LLMâs decision through different causal pathways. Leveraging these causal insights, we outline principled prompting strategies that regulate these pathways through selection mechanisms. This framework not only unifies existing prompting-based debiasing techniques, but also opens up new directions for reducing bias by encouraging the model to prioritize fact-based reasoning over reliance on biased social cues. We validate our framework through extensive experiments on real-world datasets across multiple domains, demonstrating its effectiveness in debiasing LLM decisions, even with only black-box access to the model.\nICLR\nSpotlight\nProcedural Fairness Through Decoupling Objectionable Data Generating Components\nZeyu Tang\n,Â\nJialu Wang\n,Â\nYang Liu\n,Â\nPeter Spirtes\n,Â andÂ\nKun Zhang\nIn\nTwelfth International Conference on Learning Representations (preliminary version presented in NeurIPS 2023 AFT workshop)\n, 2024.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nWe reveal and address the frequently overlooked yet important issue of disguised procedural unfairness, namely, the potentially inadvertent alterations on the behavior of neutral (i.e., not problematic) aspects of data generating process, and/or the lack of procedural assurance of the greatest benefit of the least advantaged individuals. Inspired by John Rawlsâs advocacy for pure procedural justice (Rawls, 1971; 2001), we view automated decision-making as a microcosm of social institutions, and consider how the data generating process itself can satisfy the requirements of procedural fairness. We propose a framework that decouples the objectionable data generating components from the neutral ones by utilizing reference points and the associated value instantiation rule. Our findings highlight the necessity of preventing disguised procedural unfairness, drawing attention not only to the objectionable data generating components that we aim to mitigate, but also more importantly, to the neutral components that we intend to keep unaffected.\nACM CSUR\nWhat-is and How-to for Fairness in Machine Learning: A Survey, Reflection, and Perspective\nZeyu Tang\n,Â\nJiji Zhang\n,Â andÂ\nKun Zhang\nACM Computing Surveys\n, 2023.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nAlgorithmic fairness has attracted increasing attention in the machine learning community. Various definitions are proposed in the literature, but the differences and connections among them are not clearly addressed. In this paper, we review and reflect on various fairness notions previously proposed in machine learning literature, and make an attempt to draw connections to arguments in moral and political philosophy, especially theories of justice. We also consider fairness inquiries from a dynamic perspective, and further consider the long-term impact that is induced by current prediction and decision. In light of the differences in the characterized fairness, we present a flowchart that encompasses implicit assumptions and expected outcomes of different types of fairness inquiries on the data generating process, on the predicted outcome, and on the induced impact, respectively. This paper demonstrates the importance of matching the mission (which kind of fairness one would like to enforce) and the means (which spectrum of fairness analysis is of interest, what is the appropriate analyzing scheme) to fulfill the intended purpose.\nICLR\nTier Balancing: Towards Dynamic Fairness over Underlying Causal Factors\nZeyu Tang\n,Â\nYatong Chen\n,Â\nYang Liu\n,Â andÂ\nKun Zhang\nIn\nEleventh International Conference on Learning Representations (preliminary version presented in NeurIPS 2022 AFCP workshop)\n, 2023.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nThe pursuit of long-term fairness involves the interplay between decision-making and the underlying data generating process. In this paper, through causal modeling with a directed acyclic graph (DAG) on the decision-distribution interplay, we investigate the possibility of achieving long-term fairness from a dynamic perspective. We propose Tier Balancing, a technically more challenging but more natural notion to achieve in the context of long-term, dynamic fairness analysis. Different from previous fairness notions that are defined purely on observed variables, our notion goes one step further, capturing behind-the-scenes situation changes on the unobserved latent causal factors that directly carry out the influence from the current decision to the future data distribution. Under the specified dynamics, we prove that in general one cannot achieve the long-term fairness goal only through one-step interventions. Furthermore, in the effort of approaching long-term fairness, we consider the mission of \"getting closer to\" the long-term fairness goal and present possibility and impossibility results accordingly.\nCLeaR\nSpotlight\nAttainability and Optimality: The Equalized Odds Fairness Revisited\nZeyu Tang\n,Â andÂ\nKun Zhang\nIn\nFirst Conference on Causal Learning and Reasoning\n, 2022.\nABSTRACT\narXiv\nPDF\nFairness of machine learning algorithms has been of increasing interest. In order to suppress or eliminate discrimination in prediction, various notions as well as approaches have been proposed to impose fairness. Given a notion of fairness, an essential problem is then whether or not it can always be attained, even if with an unlimited amount of data. This issue is, however, not well addressed yet. In this paper, focusing on the Equalized Odds notion of fairness, we consider the attainability of this criterion and, furthermore, if it is attainable, the optimality of the prediction performance under various settings. In particular, for prediction performed by a deterministic function of input features, we give conditions under which Equalized Odds can hold true; if the stochastic prediction is acceptable, we show that under mild assumptions, fair predictors can always be derived. For classification, we further prove that compared to enforcing fairness by post-processing, one can always benefit from exploiting all available features during training and get potentially better prediction performance while remaining fair. Moreover, while stochastic prediction can attain Equalized Odds with theoretical guarantees, we also discuss its limitation and potential negative social impacts.\n\n---\n\nCV | Zeyu Tang's Homepage\nCV\nEducation\n2025\nPh.D. in Logic, Computation and Methodology\nCarnegie Mellon University, Pennsylvania, United States\nCo-advised by\nKun Zhang\nand\nPeter Spirtes\n.\nPh.D. Dissertation Committee\nKun Zhang\n,\nPeter Spirtes\n,\nSanmi Koyejo\n,\nYang Liu\n, and\nAaron Roth\n.\nHonors and Awards\n2024\nNational Institute of Justice (NIJ) Graduate Research Fellowship\n2023\nK&L Gates Presidential Fellow\nin Ethics and Computational Technologies\n2022\nAdvML Frontiers @ ICML 2022\nBest Paper Award\nNeurIPS 2022 Top Reviewer\nAward\nUAI 2022 Top Reviewer\nAward\nProfessional Services\nOrganizing Committee:\nConference on Causal Learning and Reasoning (CLeaR), 2025.\nProgram Committee:\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR),\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML),\nNeural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS),\nConference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence (UAI),\nAAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI),\nInternational Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS),\nConference on Causal Learning and Reasoning (CLeaR),\nACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency (FAccT),\nIEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR),\nInternational Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV),\nIEEE International Conference on Data Mining (ICDM).\nJournal Reviewer:\nIEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems (TNNLS),\nTransactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR),\nData-Centric Machine Learning Research (DMLR),\nInternational Journal of Data Science and Analytics (JDSA),\nJournal of the American Statistical Association (JASA).\nExperience\n2025\nVisiting Student Researcher\nComputer Science Department, Stanford University\nHosted by\nSanmi Koyejo\n.\n2024\nMachine Learning Intern\nResponsible AI, ByteDance Research\nHosted by\nAndrew Estornell\n.\n2023\nVisiting Student Researcher\nMachine Learning Department, MBZUAI\nHosted by\nKun Zhang\n.\n2022\nApplied Scientist Intern\nMachine Learning Accelerator, Selling Partner Services, Amazon.com Inc.\nHosted by\nChris Jones\n,\nElaine Wong\n, and\nHila Hashemi\n.\n2022\nVisiting Student Researcher\nComputer Science and Engineering Department, UC Santa Cruz\nHosted by\nYang Liu\n."
