  "deep_homepage::Richard Y. Zhang::http://ryz.nz": {
    "homepage": "http://ryz.nz",
    "all_emails": [],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/ryz-codes",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Richard Y. Zhang\nRichard Y. Zhang\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Electrical and Computer Engineering\nCoordinated Science Laboratory\nUniversity of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign\nContact\n-\nCV\n-\nGoogle Scholar\n-\ndblp\n-\ngithub\n-\nLinkedIn\nMy research lies at the intersection of\noptimization\nand\nmachine\nlearning\n. I focus on\nlow-rank optimization\n, both as a\ntheoretical\nlens for understanding how learning algorithms uncover latent signals from complex data, and also as a\ncomputational\nframework for designing large-scale algorithms by operating on these signals.\nCurrent applications span unsupervised learning, adversarially robust neural networks, and electric grid state estimation and optimization.\nI received my PhD from MIT EECS, and completed a postdoc at Berkeley\nIEOR. I received the NSF CAREER Award in 2021. I've served as Area\nChair at\nNeurIPS\n(since 2021),\nICML\n(since 2023),\nICLR\n(since\n2024).\n[Third person bio]\nI am advising PhD students\nHong-Ming\nChiu\n,\nIven Guzel\n, and\nJeter Hwang\n; Masters student\nNalin Tiwary\n; Undergraduate\nKevin Wu\n. Alumni\nfrom my group include\nGavin (Jialun) Zhang (PhD\n'24 → Meta)\n, and\nJune Hou (MS '25 → UIUC PhD)\n.\nI'm always looking for\noutstanding people to join my team -- please send me an email with your\nspecific research interests.\nEmail.\nryz\nwww\n@\nuiuc\nillinois\n.\nedu\nPhone.\n+1 (\n217\n)\n-\nOffice.\n115 Coordinated Science Laboratory\n[map]\n×\nRichard Y. Zhang is an Assistant Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. He received the B.E. (hons) degree with first class honours from the University of Canterbury, New Zealand, and earned his Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering and Computer Science at MIT. Before joining Illinois, he was a Postdoctoral Scholar at UC Berkeley. His research centers on low-rank optimization, both as a theoretical lens for understanding how learning algorithms uncover latent signals from complex data, and also as a computational framework for designing massive-scale algorithms by operating on these latent signals. He received the NSF CAREER Award in 2021, and serves as Area Chair at NeurIPS, ICML, and ICLR.\n×\nUpdates\n(\nSelected\n/\nAll\n)\nMarch 2026.\nNew paper\nNonnegative Low-rank Matrix Recovery Can Have Spurious Local Minima\n, which establishes the lack of benign landscape in the nonnegative-constrained version of the usual RIP low-rank matrix recovery problem, to appear in\nOptimization Letters\n.\nJanuary 2026.\nThe paper\nScalable Second-order Riemannian Optimization for K-means Clustering\n, which proposes a 2nd-order Riemannian optimization technique for solving K-means to statistical optimality,\nto appear at\nICLR 2026\n.\nJune 2025.\nThe paper\nSharp Global Guarantees for Nonconvex Low-Rank Matrix Recovery in the Noisy Overparameterized Regime\n, which establishes minimax optimal recovery from RIP measurements at near second-order points,\nto appear in\nSIAM Journal on Optimization\n.\nApril 2025.\nOur paper\nSDP-CROWN: Efficient Bound Propagation for Neural Network Verification with Tightness of Semidefinite Programming\nis selected as a\nSpotlight paper at ICML 2025\n(one of 313/12107 submissions).\nMarch 2025.\nThe paper\nPower System State Estimation by Phase\nSynchronization and Eigenvectors\n(with Iven Guzel) to appear in\nIEEE Transactions on Control of\nNetwork Systems\n.\nFebruary 2025.\nThe paper\nSimple Alternating Minimization Provably\nSolves Complete Dictionary Learning\n(with Geyu Liang, Gavin Zhang, and Salar Fattahi) to appear in\nSIAM\nJournal on Mathematics of Data Science\n.\nFebruary 2025.\nI gave an invited talk at the\nPower Systems\nEngineering Research Center (PSERC)\nWebinar\non recent work with my PhD\nstudent Iven Guzel on\nPower System State Estimation by Phase\nSynchronization and Eigenvectors\n.\nOctober 2024.\nThe paper\nImproved Global Guarantees for the\nNonconvex Burer--Monteiro Factorization via Rank\nOverparameterization\nto appear in\nMathematical Programming\n.\nAugust 2024.\nThe paper\nComplexity of Chordal Conversion for\nSparse Semidefinite Programs with Small\nTreewidth\nto appear in\nMathematical\nProgramming\n.\nJuly 2024.\nAt the International Conference on Mathematical\nProgramming, my group is giving three talks, in the first morning\nsessions of Wed/Thu/Fri:\n(WA208) Low-rank semidefinite optimization,\nalgorithms, software and\napplications\n;\n(ThA74) The\ninterplay between optimization, statistics and machine learning\nI\n;\n(FA89) Recent advances\nin quadratic and semidefinite\noptimization\nJuly 2024.\nNew preprint on preconditioning interior-point methods\nfor low-rank SDPs:\nWell-conditioned Primal-Dual Interior-point Method\nfor Accurate Low-rank Semidefinite\nProgramming\n.\nJanuary 2024.\nOur paper\nStatistically Optimal K-means Clustering\nvia Nonnegative Low-rank Semidefinite\nProgramming\nis selected for an\noral presentation at ICLR 2024\n(one of 85/7262 submissions).\nNovember 2023.\nMy talk at the University of Michigan\nCommunications and Signal Processing Seminar Series is available on\nYouTube.\nJune 2023.\nNew preprint on chordal conversion, a widely-used\nheuristic that often allows very large-scale sparse semidefinite\nprograms (SDPs) to be solved using off-the-shelf solvers (like SeDuMi,\nSDPT3, and MOSEK):\nParameterized Complexity of Chordal Conversion for\nSparse Semidefinite Programs with Small\nTreewidth\n.\nMay 2023.\nAt the SIAM Optimization Conference, I am chairing the\nsession:\n(MS196) Recent Advances in Nonconvex\nOptimization\n.\nMy group is giving three talks, in the sessions:\n(MS6)\nParameterizations and Nonconvex\nLandscapes\n;\n(MS142) Algebraic and Geometric Methods in\nOptimization\n;\n(MS331) Algorithms and Applications of Large-Scale\nSDPs\n.\nMay 2023.\nOur\nICML 2023\npaper overcomes the \"convex relaxation\nbarrier\" faced by existing method for robustness verification of\nneural networks using a\nnonconvex\nrelaxation:\nTight Certification\nof Adversarially Trained Neural Networks via Nonconvex Low-Rank\nSemidefinite Relaxations\n.\nApril 2023.\nI will serve as Area Chair for NeurIPS 2023.\nApril 2023.\nThe paper\nPreconditioned Gradient Descent for\nOverparameterized Nonconvex Burer--Monteiro Factorization with Global\nOptimality Certification\nto appear\nin\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n.\nJanuary 2023.\nI will serve as an Area Chair for ICML 2023.\nOctober 2022.\nOur\npreprint\nshows that simple alternating minimization is enough to provably solve\nthe Dictionary Learning problem.\nAugust 2022.\nOur\npreprint\nproposes a preconditioned variant of SGD that maintains all of its\nfavorable properties for practical huge-scale optimization while also\nmaking it immune to the effects of ill-conditioning.\nAugust 2022.\nI am co-organizing the\nC3 ai Digitial Transformation\nInstitute Colloquium\nSeries\nfor Fall\n2022 alongside Gauri Joshi of CMU.\nJuly 2022.\nMy\npreprint\nproves,\nin the unconstrained setting, that a constant-factor\noverparameterization is all that is needed for nonconvex low-rank\nmatrix optimization to always suceed, as if it were convex.\nJune 2022.\nIn low-rank matrix optimization, if a locally optimal\nsolution is rank deficient, then it is provably globally optimal. In\nour\nnew preprint\n, we show that a\nsimple preconditioner is all it takes to maintain the rapid\nexponential convergence rate of gradient descent when converging\ntowards a rank deficient local optimum.\nApril 2022.\nI will serve as Area Chair for NeurIPS 2022.\nMarch 2022.\nZico Kolter and I received funding from\nC3.ai Digital\nTransformation Institute\nto\nstudy provably robust AI methods for cybersecurity tasks on the\ncritical infrastructure.\nSeptember 2021.\nPaper accepted at\nNeuIPS 2021\n:\nPreconditioned\nGradient Descent for Over-parameterized Nonconvex Matrix\nFactorization.\nApril 2021.\nI will serve as Area Chair for NeurIPS 2021.\nApril 2021.\nNew preprint makes sharp global guarantees for\nnonconvex low-rank matrix recovery in which the search rank is allowed\nto exceed the true rank:\nSharp Global Guarantees for Nonconvex\nLow-Rank Matrix Recovery in the Overparameterized\nRegime\n.\nJanuary 2021.\nDelighted and honored to receive the NSF CAREER\nAward.\nSeptember 2020.\nTwo papers accepted at\nNeuIPS 2020\n:\n(Spotlight)\nHow Many Samples is a Good Initial Point Worth in\nLow-rank Matrix Recovery?\nand\n(Poster)\nOn the Tightness of Semidefinite Relaxations for\nCertifying Robustness to Adversarial\nExamples\n.\nJune 2020.\nNew preprint shows that the SDP relaxation of ReLU\nnetworks is generally tight for a single hidden layer and generally\nloose for multiple layers:\nOn the Tightness of Semidefinite\nRelaxations for Certifying Robustness to Adversarial\nExamples\n.\nJune 2020.\nNew preprint quantifies the value of a good initial\npoint for nonconvex matrix recovery:\nHow Many Samples is a Good\nInitial Point Worth?\nApril 2020.\nPaper on solving sparse semidefinite programs in\nnear-linear time to appear in\nMathematical Programming\n:\nSparse\nSemidefinite Programs with Guaranteed Near-Linear Time Complexity via\nDualized Clique Tree Conversion\n.\nNovember 2019.\nPaper on optimizing the relative timing of traffic\nsignals to appear in the\nIEEE Transactions on Control of Network\nSystems\n:\nLarge-Scale Traffic Signal Offset\nOptimization\n.\nJune 2019.\nPaper on the Restricted Isometry Property (RIP) for\nnonconvex matrix recovery problem to appear in the\nJournal of Machine\nLearning Research (JMLR)\n:\nSharp Restricted Isometry Bounds for the\nInexistence of Spurious Local Minima in Nonconvex Matrix\nRecovery\n.\nMay 2019.\nI will be joining\nECE\nIllinois\n(UIUC) as an\nAssistant Professor starting August 2019.\nMay 2019.\nPaper on the nonconvex power system state estimation\nproblem to appear in a\nspecial\nissue\nof\nIEEE Transactions on Control of Network Systems\n:\nSpurious Local\nMinima in Power System\nEstimation\n.\nJanuary 2019.\nNew preprint proves that the (2,1/2)-Restricted\nIsometry Property (RIP) is both necessary and sufficient for the\nrank-1 nonconvex matrix recovery problem to contain no spurious local\nminima:\nSharp Restricted Isometry Bounds for the Inexistence of\nSpurious Local Minima in Nonconvex Matrix\nRecovery\n.\nDecember 2018.\nPresented 2 papers at\nNeurIPS 2018\n:\n(Spotlight)\n(Poster)\nHow Much Restricted Isometry is Needed In Nonconvex Matrix Recovery?\n(Poster)\nA Theory on the Absence of Spurious Solutions for Nonconvex and\nNonsmooth Optimization. (Of 4856 total submissions, 1011 were\naccepted, including 30 orals and 168 spotlights.)\nOctober 2018.\nPaper on accelerating ADMM using Krylov subspace\nappeared in\nSIAM Journal on Optimization\n:\nGMRES-Accelerated ADMM\nfor Quadratic Objectives\n.\nPublications\n(\nSelected\n/\nAll\n)\nSharp Global Guarantees for Nonconvex Low-Rank Matrix Recovery in the Noisy Overparameterized Regime\nR.Y. Zhang -\nSIAM Journal on Optimization\n, 35.3 (2025): pp. 2128-2154.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nImproved Global Guarantees for the Nonconvex Burer--Monteiro Factorization via Rank Overparameterization\nR.Y. Zhang -\nMathematical Programming\n, 213 (2025): pp. 1009-1038.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nPreconditioned Gradient Descent for Overparameterized Nonconvex Burer-Monteiro Factorization with Global Optimality Certification\nG. Zhang, S. Fattahi, R.Y. Zhang -\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n, 24.163 (2023): pp. 1-55.\n[permalink]\n[arxiv]\nStatistically Optimal K-means Clustering via Nonnegative Low-rank Semidefinite Programming\nSelected for Oral (one of 85/7262 submissions)\nY. Zhuang, X. Chen, Y. Yang, R.Y. Zhang -\nICLR 2024\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n.\n[arxiv]\nSDP-CROWN: Efficient Bound Propagation for Neural Network Verification with Tightness of Semidefinite Programming\nSelected for Spotlight (one of 313/12107 submissions)\nH.-M. Chiu, H. Chen, H. Zhang, R.Y. Zhang -\nICML 2025\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning\n.\n[arxiv]\nPower System State Estimation by Phase Synchronization and Eigenvectors\nI. Guzel, R.Y. Zhang -\nIEEE Transactions on Control of Network Systems\n, 12.3 (2025): pp.2207-2218.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nPreprints\nWell-conditioned Primal-Dual Interior-point Method for Accurate Low-rank Semidefinite Programming\nH.-M. Chiu, R.Y. Zhang - Preprint, Jul 2024\n[arxiv]\n2026\nNonnegative Low-rank Matrix Recovery Can Have Spurious Local Minima\nR.Y. Zhang -\nOptimization Letters\n, to appear.\n[arxiv]\nScalable Second-order Riemannian Optimization for K-means Clustering\nP. Xu*, C.-Y. Hou*, X. Chen, R.Y. Zhang -\nICLR 2026\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n.\n[arxiv]\n2025\nSharp Global Guarantees for Nonconvex Low-Rank Matrix Recovery in the Noisy Overparameterized Regime\nR.Y. Zhang -\nSIAM Journal on Optimization\n, 35.3 (2025): pp. 2128-2154.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nSimple Alternating Minimization Provably Solves Complete Dictionary Learning\nG. Liang, G. Zhang, S. Fattahi, R.Y. Zhang -\nSIAM Journal on Mathematics of Data Science\n, 7.3 (2025): pp. 855-883.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nPower System State Estimation by Phase Synchronization and Eigenvectors\nI. Guzel, R.Y. Zhang -\nIEEE Transactions on Control of Network Systems\n, 12.3 (2025): pp.2207-2218.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nSDP-CROWN: Efficient Bound Propagation for Neural Network Verification with Tightness of Semidefinite Programming\nSelected for Spotlight (one of 313/12107 submissions)\nH.-M. Chiu, H. Chen, H. Zhang, R.Y. Zhang -\nICML 2025\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning\n.\n[arxiv]\nImproved Global Guarantees for the Nonconvex Burer--Monteiro Factorization via Rank Overparameterization\nR.Y. Zhang -\nMathematical Programming\n, 213 (2025): pp. 1009-1038.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nComplexity of Chordal Conversion for Sparse Semidefinite Programs with Small Treewidth\nR.Y. Zhang -\nMathematical Programming\n, 213 (2025): pp. 201-237.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\n2024\nStatistically Optimal K-means Clustering via Nonnegative Low-rank Semidefinite Programming\nSelected for Oral (one of 85/7262 submissions)\nY. Zhuang, X. Chen, Y. Yang, R.Y. Zhang -\nICLR 2024\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n.\n[arxiv]\nFast and Minimax Optimal Estimation of Low-Rank Matrices via Non-Convex Gradient Descent\nG. Zhang, H.-M. Chiu, R.Y. Zhang -\nAISTATS 2024\nArtificial Intelligence and Statistics\n.\n[arxiv]\n2023\nPreconditioned Gradient Descent for Overparameterized Nonconvex Burer-Monteiro Factorization with Global Optimality Certification\nG. Zhang, S. Fattahi, R.Y. Zhang -\nJournal of Machine Learning Research\n, 24.163 (2023): pp. 1-55.\n[permalink]\n[arxiv]\nTight Certification of Adversarially Trained Neural Networks via Nonconvex Low-Rank Semidefinite Relaxations\nH.-M. Chiu, R.Y. Zhang -\nICML 2023\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning\n.\n[arxiv]\n2022\nAccelerating SGD for Highly Ill-Conditioned Huge-Scale Online Matrix Completion\nG. Zhang, H.-M. Chiu, R.Y. Zhang -\nNeurIPS 2022\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n.\n[arxiv]\n2021\nSparse Semidefinite Programs with Guaranteed Near-Linear Time Complexity via Dualized Clique Tree Conversion\nR.Y. Zhang, J. Lavaei -\nMathematical Programming\n, 188.1 (2021): pp. 351-393.\n[doi]\n[arxiv]\nUniqueness of Power Flow Solutions Using Monotonicity and Network Topology\nS.-W. Park, R.Y. Zhang, J. Lavaei, R. Baldick -\nIEEE Transactions on Control of Network Systems\n, 8.1 (2020): pp. 319-330...[truncated]"
