    "homepage_text": "Zhewei Wei\té­å²å·\nZhewei Wei\n(é­å²å·)\né­å²å·\nProfessor (ææï¼åå¯¼)\nVice Dean of\nGaoling School of Artificial Intelligence\nRenmin University of China\nNo. 59 Zhongguancun Street, Haidian District, Beijing, 100872, P.R. China\nOffice: Room 1715, Lide Building\nEmail:\nPhone: (86) 010-62513716\nChinese Homepage (ä¸­æä¸»é¡µ):\ngsai.ruc.edu.cn/zhewei\nI am currently Professor and Vice Dean of\nGaoling School of Artificial Intelligence\n,\nRenmin University of China\n.  I worked as a Professor (Jul 2019 - Jul 2020), and as an Associate Professor (Sep 2014 - Jun 2019) at School of Information, Renmin University of China. I was a Postdoc researcher at\nMADALGO (Center for Massive Data Algorithmics)\n,\nAarhus University\n, from September 2012 to August 2014, working with\nProf. Lars Arge\n. I was a Postdoc researcher at the\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering\n,\nHKUST\n, from March to August 2012. I obtained my PhD at\nDepartment of Computer Science and Engineering\n,\nHKUST\nin March 2012. My supervisor is\nProf. Ke Yi\n. I received my B.Sc. Degree in the\nSchool of Mathematical Sciences\nat\nPeking University\nin June 2008.\nResearch Interests\nI lead\nThe ALGO Lab\n, where our research focuses on developing simple, elegant, and theoretically sound algorithms to tackle critical challenges in Artificial Intelligence and Big Data processing. Our specific areas of interest include:\nGraph Algorithms and Graph Machine Learning\nDesigning efficient algorithms to process and learn from massive graph structures.\nStreaming Algorithms and Stream Machine Learning\nDeveloping algorithms capable of handling and learning from continuous, real-time data streams.\nAI for Science\nApplying graph machine learning to solve complex problems in biology, pharmaceuticals, and other scientific disciplines.\nAI for Social Science\nUtilizing large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems to model intricate human social networks.\nAI for Databases\nLeveraging machine learning techniques to optimize database systems for enhanced performance and efficiency.\nNews\nNov 24, 2025\nFour papers âTetraGT: Tetrahedral Geometry-Driven Explicit Token Interactions with Graph Transformer for Molecular Representation Learningâ, âRobustness in Text-Attributed Graph Learning: Insights, Trade-offs, and New Defensesâ, âGDGB: A Benchmark for Generative Dynamic Text-Attributed Graph Learningâ, and âRevisiting Matrix Sketching in Linear Bandits: Achieving Sublinear Regret via Dyadic Block Sketchingâ have been accepted by ICLR 2026.\nNov 24, 2025\nOne paper âAeroSketch: Near-Optimal Time Matrix Sketch Framework for Persistent, Sliding Window, and Distributed Streamsâ has been accepted by SIGMOD 2026.\nNov 24, 2025\nOne paper âAbFlow: End-to-end Full-atom Antibody Design by Interaction Enhanced Flow Matchingâ has been accepted by KDD 2026.\nNov 11, 2025\nOne paper âOn Solving Asymmetric Diagonally Dominant Linear Systems in Sublinear Timeâ has been accepted by ITCS 2026.\nNov 06, 2025\nOne paper âRestricted Black-Box Attack on Graphs Beyond Homophilyâ has been accepted by TKDE 2026.\nAwards\nAdvanced Worker of Renmin University of China, 2024\nBest Research Paper Nomination, VLDB 2024\nYouth Outstanding Paper Nomination Award, World Artificial Intelligence Conference 2023\nTest of Time Award, PODS 2022\nYoung Scientist, Pazhou Laboratory (Huangpu)\nTeaching Excellence Nomination Award, Renmin University of China, 2019\n\"Outstanding Scholar\" Young Scholar, Renmin University of China\nServices\nProceedings Chair: SIGMOD/PODS 2020, ICDT 2021\nArea Chair: ICML 2022/2023/2024, NeurIPS 2022/2023/2024, ICLR 2023/2024/2025, TheWebConf 2023\nSenior PC: AAAI 2025\nPC Member: PODS 2025, NeurIPS 2021, ICML 2021, KDD 2021, VLDB 2020, ICDE 2021, SIGMETRICS 2020, ICBK 2019, NDBC 2018, ICLR 2023, ICDE 2023\nConference Reviewer: SODA, ISAAC, VLDB, ICDE, CIKM, PODS, SIGMOD, KDD\nJournal Reviewer: TKDE, TODS, VLDBJ, TOIS, SICOMP, TALG, GEOINFORMATICA, TNNLS, TPAMI\nMember of Journal Editorial Board: IEEE TPAMI Associate Editor, FCS Young Editor\nMembers: ä¸­å½è®¡ç®æºå­¦ä¼æ°æ®åºä¸å§ä¼ä¸å§ï¼ä¸­å½è®¡ç®æºå­¦ä¼å­¦æ¯å·¥ä½å§åä¼å§åï¼\näººå·¥æºè½ä¸æ°å­ç»æµå¹¿ä¸çå®éªå®¤ï¼ç¶æ´²å®éªå®¤ï¼\néå¹´ç§å­¦å®¶\nTalks\nAI TIME,\nMatrix Sketching and Streaming Machine Learning\n(ç©éµç¥å¾ä¸æµæ°æ®æºå¨å­¦ä¹ ), Oct 2024 \n        \n            [\nvideo\n]\n        \n            [\nslides\n]\nAcademy of Mathematics and System Science, CAS,\nGraph Convolutional Networks: Theory and Fundamentals\n, Aug 2024 \n        \n            [\nslides\n]\nCCF Young Computer Scientists & Engineers Forum (YOCSEF),\nAI4DB Algorithm with Theoretical Guarantee\n(æçè®ºä¿è¯ç AI4DB ç®æ³), Aug 2024 \n        \n            [\nslides\n]\nClassical Talk@QuACT,\nSublinear-Time Algorithms for Random-Walk Probability Estimation\n, May 2024 \n        \n            [\nslides\n]\nVALSE 2023,\nGraph Representation Learning\n(å¾è¡¨ç¤ºå­¦ä¹ ), June 2023 \n        \n            [\nslides\n]\nVALSE 2021,\nTheoretical Basis of Graph Neural Networks\n(å¾ç¥ç»ç½ç»çè®ºåºç¡), Oct 2021 \n        \n            [\nslides\n]\nTeaching\nLecturer for Algorithm Design and Analysis (ç®æ³è®¾è®¡ä¸åæ): Fall 2016, Fall 2017, Fall 2018, Fall 2019, Spring 2020, Fall 2020, Spring 2021, Spring 2022, Spring 2023, Spring 2024, Spring 2025\nLecturer for Graph Machine Learning (å¾æºå¨å­¦ä¹ ): Fall 2021, Fall 2022, Fall 2023, Fall 2024, Fall 2025\nLecturer for Seminar for Freshmen (æ°çç è®¨è¯¾): Fall 2021, Fall 2022, Fall 2023\nLecturer for ACM-ICPC Algorithms Design: Spring 2019\nLecturer for ACM-ICPC Algorithms Design: Spring 2018\nLecturer for Massive Data Algorithms (æµ·éæ°æ®ç®æ³): Spring 2016, Spring 2017, Spring 2018\nLecturer for Operational Research (è¿ç­¹å­¦): Spring 2016\nLecturer for Calculus: Fall 2015\nLecturer for Streaming Algorithms and Related Topics: Spring 2014"
