    "github": "https://github.com/EVIEHub",
    "linkedin": "https://linkedin.com/in/fengxiang-he-35b173122",
    "homepage_text": "Fengxiang He's home page\nFengxiang He\nLecturer\nUniversity of Edinburgh\nGoogle Scholar\n|\nEmail\n|\nLinkedIn\n|\nbook a meeting with me\nBio\nI am a Lecturer at\nArtificial Intelligence and its Applications Institute\n,\nSchool of Informatics\n,\nUniversity of Edinburgh\n, leading the Edinburgh Ventures for Intelligence and Economics. Within Edinburgh, I am also a Fellow of\nGenerative AI Laboratory\n, and an Affiliate of the\nInstitute for Adaptive and Neural Computation\n,\nEdinburgh Future Institute\n, and the\nEdinburgh Centre for Financial Innovations\n. I was the founding lead of the Trustworthy AI project at\nJD.com, Inc.\n, a leading e-commerce and logistics firm in China. I received my BSc in statistics from the\nUniversity of Science and Technology of China\n, MPhil and PhD in computer science from the\nUniversity of Sydney\n. I am an Area Chair of conferences ICML, NeurIPS, ICLR, UAI, AISTATS, ECAI, and ACML, and on the Editorial Boards of journals Machine Learning and IEEE Transactions on Technology and Society.\nMy research is in (1) understanding AI, through developing theoretical foundations for deep learning, interpreting its generalisability, stability, privacy, fairness, etc. The scope includes both general neural networks and frontier models, such as large language models and diffusion models. The developed theory covers both neural architectures and optimisation methods; (2) developing new machine learning models and algorithms that leverage the intrinsic symmetries in data, task, environment, amongst others, aiming at more efficient and significant scalability beyond simply scaling model, data, and compute; (3) understanding and developing decentralised and multi-agent AI, aiming at democratic and affordable frontier AI through a decentralised avenue; and (4) I am also interested in employing AI to solve game-theoretical, economic, and financial problems.\nBulletin\nFully funded PhD studentships are available from the Centres for Doctoral Training in\nMachine Learning System\n,\nDesigning Responsible NLP\n, and\nAI for Biomedical Innovation\n.\nVisitors are welcome. Candidates please email me, possibly with your CV, transcripts, papers, or anything you are proud of.\nHow to pronounce\nmy name\n:)\nFeatured Publications\nAny comment is welcome. Please see the full list at my\nGoogle Scholar\nprofile.\nSaimun Habib, Xiao Xiao, Meng Fang,\nFengxiang He\n,\nMission Impossible: Universal LLM Moral Alignment\n.\n2026.\n[paper]\nNEW\nKenton Tang, Yuzhu Chen,\nFengxiang He\n,\nGeneralisation of RLHF under Reward Shift and Clipped KL Regularisation.\n2026.\n[paper]\nNEW\nFinn van der Knaap, Kejiang Qian, Zheng Xu,\nFengxiang He\n,\nPRISM: Parallel Reward Integration with Symmetry for MORL.\n2026.\n[paper]\n[code]\nNEW\nSaimun Habib, Vaishak Belle,\nFengxiang He\n,\nEfficient Counterfactual Reasoning in ProbLog via Single World Intervention Programs.\n2026.\n[paper]\n[code]\nNEW\nKejiang Qian, Amos Storkey,\nFengxiang He\n,\nRationality Measurement and Theory for Reinforcement Learning Agents.\n2026.\n[paper]\n[code]\nNEW\nAijie Shu, Wenbin Wu, Gbenga Ibikunle, and\nFengxiang He\n,\nDeXposure-FM: A Time-series, Graph Foundation Model for Credit Exposures and Stability on Decentralized Financial Networks.\n2026.\n[paper]\n[model]\n[code]\nFengxiang He\n, Lihao Nan, and Tongtian Zhu,\nImagining a Democratic, Affordable Future of Foundation Models: A Decentralised Avenue.\nIn\nHandbook of Blockchain Analytics\n, Springer Nature, 2026.\n[Springer webpage]\nWenbin Wu, Kejiang Qian, Alexis Lui, Christopher Jack, Yue Wu, Peter McBurney,\nFengxiang He\n, Bryan Zhang.\nDeXposure: A Dataset and Benchmarks for Inter-protocol Credit Exposure in Decentralized Financial Networks.\n2025.\n[paper]\n[data]\n[code]\n[visualisation]\nGuanpu Chen, Gehui Xu,\nFengxiang He\n, Dacheng Tao, Thomas Parisini, Karl Henrik Johansson.\nInverse learning of black-box aggregator for robust Nash equilibrium.\nIEEE Transactions on Automatic Control\n(\nTAC\n), 2025.\n[paper]\nXuelian Jiang, Tongtian Zhu, Yingxiang Xu, Can Wang, Yeyu Zhang,\nFengxiang He\n,\nLie Symmetry Net: Preserving Conservation Laws in Modelling Financial Market Dynamics via Differential Equations.\nTransactions on Machine Learning Research\n(\nTMLR\n), 2025.\n[paper]\nTongtian Zhu, Wenhao Li, Can Wang,\nFengxiang He\n,\nDICE: Data Influence Cascade in Decentralized Learning.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2025.\n[paper]\n[code]\nGuanpu Chen, Gehui Xu,\nFengxiang He\n✉, Yiguang Hong, Leszek Rutkowski, and Dacheng Tao,\nGlobal Nash Equilibrium in Non-convex Multi-player Game: Theory and Algorithms.\nIEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence\n(\nTPAMI\n), 2024.\n[paper]\n[code]\nShi Fu,\nFengxiang He\net al.\nConvergence of Bayesian Bilevel Optimization.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2024.\n[paper]\n[bib]\nTongtian Zhu,\nFengxiang He\n✉ et al.\nDecentralized SGD and Average-direction SAM are Asymptotically Equivalent.\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), 2023.\n[paper]\n[code]\n[bib]\nMengnan Du,\nFengxiang He\net al.\nShortcut Learning of Large Language Models in Natural Language Understanding.\nCommunications of the ACM\n, 2023.\n[paper]\n[bib]\nTian Qin*,\nFengxiang He\n* et al. “Benefits of Permutation-Equivariance in Auction Mechanisms.” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2022.\n[paper]\n[bib]\nTongtian Zhu,\nFengxiang He\n✉ et al.\nTopology-aware Generalization of Decentralized SGD.\nInternational Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), 2022.\n[paper]\n[code]\n[bib]\nShaopeng Fu*,\nFengxiang He*\net al.\nKnowledge Removal in Sampling-based Bayesian Inference.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representation (ICLR), 2022. (Part of Shaopeng Fu*,\nFengxiang He*\net al.\nBayesian Inference Forgetting.\n)\n[paper]\n[code]\n[bib]\nShaopeng Fu,\nFengxiang He\net al.\nRobust Unlearnable Examples: Protecting Data Privacy Against Adversarial Learning.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2022.\n[paper]\n[code]\n[bib]\nFengxiang He\n*, Bohan Wang* et al.\nTighter generalization bounds for iterative differentially private learning algorithms.\nConference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence (UAI), 2021.\n[paper]\n[bib]\nFengxiang He\net al.\nRecent advances in deep learning theory.\n2020.\n[paper]\n[bib]\nZhuozhuo Tu,\nFengxiang He\net al.\nUnderstanding Generalization in Recurrent Neural Networks.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representation (ICLR), 2020.\n[paper]\n[bib]\nFengxiang He\n*, Bohan Wang* et al.\nPiecewise linear activations substantially shape the loss surfaces of neural networks.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2020.\n[paper]\n[website]\n[poster]\n[bib]\nFengxiang He\net al.\nControl batch size and learning rate to generalize well: Theoretical and empirical evidence.\nAdvances on Neural Information Processing (NeurIPS), 2019.\n[paper]\n[poster]\n[bib]\n* Co-first authors.\nTeam\nPhD Students\n, me as Primary Supervisor\nKejiang\nBrian\nQian\n, 2024-present, Assistant Supervisor:\nAmos Storkey\nSaimun Habib\n, 2024-present, Assistant Supervisor:\nDan van der Horst\n,\nS. Travis Waller\n,\nTingting Mu\nQian Zuo\n, 2024-present, Assistant Supervisor:\nOisin Mac Aodha\nme as Assistant Supervisor\nJohnny MyungWon Lee\n, 2022-present, Primary Supervisor:\nMiguel de Carvalho\nMScR Students\nYe Wang\n, 2025-present, Assistant Supervisor:\nGbenga Ibikunle\nTianhao Li\n, 2025-present, Assistant Supervisor:\nGbenga Ibikunle\n,\nSihan Zeng\nInterns\nNathan Li, 2026-present\nKenton Tang\n, 2025-present\nShaoxuan Quan, 2025-present\nVeronica Xu, 2025-present\nHamayun Rasool, 2025\nTianhao Li, 2025\nDominik Ochendalski\n, 2025\nSergej Kucenko\n, 2024-2025\nPierre Lardet\n, 2024\nXueyan Liu, 2024\nPhD Visitor\nJuntian\nJudith\nZhu\n, 2024-present, from USTC, Co-Supervisor:\nMiguel de Carva