    "homepage_text": "Peng Liu Research Group | computational organic chemistry\ntop of page\nPeng Liu Research Group\nClose\nExploring Computational Organic Chemistry Innovations\nUnderstanding and predicting  organic, organometallic, and biocatalytic reactions.\nResearch Focus\nThe Peng Liu research group addresses grand challenges in catalytic organic reactions by utilizing multiscale computational simulations to study reaction mechanisms and reactivity. We investigate complex mechanistic scenarios in transition-metal catalysis and biocatalysis to uncover critical factors controlling reactivity and selectivity, such as non-covalent interactions and catalyst flexibility. Ultimately, our work bridges the gap between theory and experiment to guide the design of more efficient catalysts and novel synthetic methodologies.\nRead More\nTransition Metal Catalysis\n​\nInnovative computational approaches to study catalytic reactions.\nRead More\nMechanisms and Reactivity\nDetailed mechanistic investigations and reactivity predictions.\nRead More\nBiocatalysis\n​\nUnderstanding the mechanisms of stereoselective biocatalytic reactions.\nRead More\nGroup News\nLiu Group to Host 5th Houk Research Conference (HRC) at Pitt\nWe are excited to announce that registration is now officially open for the 5th Houk Research Conference (HRC) , which will be hosted here at the University of Pittsburgh from June 24–26, 2026 . Co-organized by Prof. Peng Liu and Prof. Zhongyue (John) Yang (Vanderbilt University), the 2026 HRC will convene under the theme \"Bridging Computation, Experiment, and Data.\"  The event will honor the distinguished career of Prof. Kendall N. Houk  (UCLA) and celebrate the vibrant comm\nJan 8\nGrace Scofield and Haleigh Bos Awarded 2026 GAANN Fellowships\nGrace Scofield  and Haleigh Bos  have been awarded the 2026 Department of Education GAANN Fellowships . The Graduate Assistance in Areas of National Need (GAANN) Fellowship is a prestigious program designed to train the next generation of teachers and scholars in critical areas of national need. This fellowship offers a unique opportunity for students committed to advancing both research and teaching. Fellows engage in cross-disciplinary research, collaborating with two disti\nJan 1\nLiu Group Celebrates the Holidays with Axes and Trivia\nThe Peng Liu Group took a well-deserved break from the computational cluster this week to celebrate the holiday season with some sharp competition. The group headed to Ace Axe Throwing  in Homestead, PA, for an afternoon of team building and target practice. Trading their keyboards for hatchets, group members tested their aim and precision in a high-energy tournament. It was a fantastic opportunity to unwind and enjoy each other's company outside the lab environment. Annual T\nDec 18, 2025\nDepartment Unveils Newly Renovated Theory Graduate Student Suite and Conference Room in Eberly Hall\nThe group enjoyed the opening of the newly renovated Theory Graduate Student Suite  (Room 326) and the adjacent Conference Room (Room 325), both are located across the hallway from our student office on the third floor of Eberly. These upgrades mark a significant investment in the graduate student experience, providing a dedicated environment for socialization, collaboration, and relaxation. The new Theory Graduate Student Suite has been transformed into a modern, multi-funct\nDec 1, 2025\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\nMore Group News\nExplore Our Research Group\nPublications\nRead our impactful publications.\nGroup Members\n​\nMeet the members of our research group.\nAlumni\nFind out about our former members and visitors.\nResources\nAccess valuable resources and materials.\nbottom of page"
  },
