    "homepage_text": "FXV\nSearch\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\nProfessor\nLIGM\nUniversitÃ© Gustave Eiffel\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\nEnseignant-chercheur au Laboratoire d’informatique Gaspard Monge.\nInterests\nOptimal transport; applications and numerics.\nDiffeomorphic flows; machine learning and medical image registration applications.\nCalculus of variations and geometry; applications to shape spaces and fluid flows.\nApplied and computational mathematics.\nConferences\nDec 14, 2025\nMonthly seminar MMSID (Models and Methods for Signal, Image and Data)\nSÃ©minaire mensuel de l’Ã©quipe MMSID, University Gustave Eiffel, Paris area.\nJan 30, 2024\nJournÃ©e SIGMA - MODE 2024\nUne journÃ©e de confÃ©rence organisÃ©e par les deux groupes thÃ©matiques SIGMA (Signal-Image) et MODE (Optimisation) de la SMAI aura lieu Ã  INRIA Paris mardi 30 janvier 2024: huit exposÃ©s de recherche sur des thÃ¨mes communs. Inscription gratuite et obligatoire.\nNov 14, 2023\nOberwolfach seminar: Variational and Information Flows in Machine Learning and Optimal Transport\nA five days conference with introductory lectures in the format of Oberwolfach seminars, 19 â 25 November 2023, Mathematisches Forschungsinstitut Oberwolfach.\nSee all\nProjects\nConference: New Monge Problems and Applications\nA two days conference centered on computational optimal transport and its applications, September 14th-15th, University Gustave Eiffel, â¦\nSÃ©ances du groupe de travail\nPage du groupe de travail du labex BÃ©zout sur les problÃ¨mes de transport optimal: Transport martingale, transport optimal faible, â¦\nPosts\nA word and a question on generative diffusion models for the outsider mathematician.\nIn the recent years, the amazing results of generative models for imaging have triggered a large body of research in computer science â¦\nJun 4, 2025\n16 min read\nBeyond Sinkhorn: Generalized Iterative Scaling\nSinkhorn algorithm (also called IPFP for Iterative Proportional Fitting Procedure) is an alternating optimisation algorithm which has â¦\nApr 11, 2023\n7 min read\nA geometric formulation of Laplace method.\nWhen trying to understand the low temperature asymptotic of entropic regularization in optimal transport, Gibbs measures naturally â¦\nDec 4, 2022\n9 min read\nPartial optimization and Schur complement\nIn a couple of recent papers on applied optimal transport (OT), we used the fact that the so-called semi-dual formulation of OT â¦\nJan 4, 2022\n8 min read\nAbout the geometry of Stein variational gradient descent.\nIn a project with a student, IÂ recently derived a non-local diffusion PDE which turns out, surprisingly for me, to have a name: Stein â¦\nSep 29, 2021\n10 min read\nRecent preprints and publications\nComplete list of publications\nhere\n.\nGromov-Wasserstein at Scale, Beyond Squared Norms\nA fundamental challenge in data science is to match disparate point sets with each other. While optimal transport efficiently minimizes â¦\nGuillaume Houry\n,\nJean Feydy\n,\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\nPDF\nCite\nSliced ReLU attention: Quasi-linear contextual expressivity via sorting\nWe introduce sliced ReLU attention, a new attention mechanism that departs structurally from both softmax and ReLU-based alternatives. â¦\nSiwan BoufadÃ¨ne\n,\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\nPDF\nCite\nToken Sample Complexity of Attention\nAs context windows in large language models continue to expand, it is essential to characterize how attention behaves at extreme â¦\nLÃ©a Bohbot\n,\nCyril Letrouit\n,\nGabriel PeyrÃ©\n,\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\nPDF\nCite\nDecreasing Entropic Regularization Averaged Gradient for Semi-Discrete Optimal Transport\nWe introduce DRAG: Decreasing (entropic) Regularization Averaged Gradient, a stochastic gradient descent algorithm where the â¦\nFerdinand Genans-Boiteux\n,\nAntoine Godichon-Baggioni\n,\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\n,\nOlivier Wintenberger\nPDF\nCite\nStochastic Optimization in Semi-Discrete Optimal Transport: Convergence Analysis and Minimax Rate\nWe investigate the semi-discrete Optimal Transport (OT) problem, where a continuous source measure Î¼ is transported to a discrete â¦\nFerdinand Genans-Boiteux\n,\nAntoine Godichon-Baggioni\n,\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\n,\nOlivier Wintenberger\nPDF\nCite\nFast Large Deformation Matching with the Energy Distance Kernel\nThe cost of large deformation by diffeomorphisms has a quadratic computational footprint which is a practical bottleneck. We reduce it â¦\nSiwan BoufadÃ¨ne\n,\nJean Feydy\n,\nFranÃ§ois-Xavier Vialard\nPDF\nCite\nSee all publications\nTalks\nFeb 6, 2024 9:45 AM\nMFO, Oberwolfach.\nSynthetic nonnegative cross-curvature lifts to the Wasserstein space.\nIn this talk, we insist on the concept of nonnegative cross-curvature and its synthetic definition for a general cost on a product space. Then, by using a formal argument we show why one can expect that such a property should be also true for the Wasserstein space. Then, we give examples of cost satisfying this synthetic nonnegative cross-curvature, in particular a new one with the Bures-Wasserstein case. We extend the result to the case of unbalanced optimal transport and show some potential applications.\nNov 6, 2023 9:45 AM\nBanff, Canada.\nOn the global convergence of the Wasserstein gradient ow of Coulomb discrepancies.\nIn this talk, we study the gradient flow with respect to the Wasserstein metric of the Maximum Mean Discrepancy associated with the Coulomb kernel. In this context, we present several sufficient conditions for global convergence of the gradient flow to the unique global minimum. For instance, on closed Riemannian manifolds, we prove that the so-called Polyak-Lojasiewicz condition holds in some cases, resulting in an exponential convergence. To obtain this result, we use standard estimates from potential theory. An other result is the fact that there is no local minimum apart from the global one. This result is proven using flow interchange techniques.\nOct 11, 2023 11:30 AM\nVariational and Geometric workshop: Alexander Mielke's birthday, Levico.\nThe use of conic formulation related to unbalanced optimal transport: regularity, the Camassa-Holm equation and its relaxation, unbalanced Gromov-Wasserstein.\nIn this talk, we present our use of the conic formulation first proposed by Liero, Mielke and SavarÃ©, in different applications related to unbalanced optimal transport: the first one is regularity of optimal transport, the second is the Camassa-Holm equation which is the fluid dynamic model directly related to unbalanced optimal transport and the metric on metric measures spaces with unnormalized measures.\nSee all talks\nContact\nfrancois-xavier.vialard@univ-eiffel.fr\nUGE, Noisy-Champs\nSkype Me\nExperience\nProfessor\nUniversitÃ© Gustave Eiffel\nSep 2018 â\n              \n                Present\nNoisy-Champs\nMember of the research lab LIGM (Laboratoire d’informatique Gaspard Monge) and teaching signal processing.\nAssistant professor\nUniversity Paris-Dauphine\nSep 2011 â\n              \n                Sep 2018\nParis\nMember of the research lab Ceremade, UMR CNRS 7534, and teaching applied mathematics.\nResearch assistant\nImperial College\nMay 2009 â\n              \n                Sep 2011\nLondon\nMember of the Institute for Mathematical Sciences and the Math department.\nPhD candidate\nENSÂ Cachan (ENSÂ Paris-Saclay)\nSep 2005 â\n              \n                May 2009\nParis area\nMember of the lab CMLA.\nPhD and HDR\nHabilitation Ã  diriger les recherches\nUniversitÃ© Paris-Dauphine\nDec 2017\nOptimal transport, Diffeomorphisms and applications to imaging\nPhD in applied mathematics\nENS-Paris-Saclay\nMay 2009\nHamiltonian formulation of diffeomorphic image matching\nCite\n×\nCopy\nDownload"
