    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaomutan/",
    "homepage_text": "Shaomu Tan\nShaomu\nTan\nResearch on Machine Translation ❙ Multilingual NLP ❙ Deep Learning.\nI am currently a PhD candidate at the\nLanguage Technology Lab\n,\nUniversity of Amsterdam\n. I work with\nChristof Monz\nand my amazing colleagues at LTL. Before my PhD, I obtained an M.S. degree in Artificial Intelligence at\nUtrecht University\n, Netherlands.\nI’m on a quest to make Machine Translation and LLMs more effective, breaking down language barriers and connecting people around the world! My recent research focuses on understanding and improving Multilingual, Multi-Task Learning, Translation Evaluation, and Multilingual Reasoning. I am always open to collaborations, paper discussions, shared tasks and competitions,\nfeel free to reach out\n!\nnews\nMar 06, 2026\nI’m actively looking for Research Scientist / Engineer roles in Multilingual and Multimodal LLMs across the U.S., Japan, and Europe. Please feel free to reach out!\nOct 01, 2025\nI started a new position as\nApplied Scientist Research Intern\nat\nAmazon AGI\nat Foundation Modeling Group, in Berlin, Germany. Feel free to have a coffee chat if you’re in Berlin!\nAug 20, 2025\nOur paper “\nRemedy: Learning Machine Translation Evaluation from Human Preferences with Reward Modeling\n” is accepted to the\nEMNLP25\nmain conference\n.\nAug 07, 2025\nI gave a talk at\nTsuruoka Lab\n, University of Tokyo\n, on\n“The Second Half of Machine Translation”\n, invited by\nProf. Tsuruoka Yoshimasa\nand\nDr. Qiyu Wu\n.\nJul 22, 2025\nI gave a talk at\nNAIST NLP Lab\non\n“The Second Half of Machine Translation”\n, invited by\nProf. Taro Watanabe\n.\nJun 01, 2025\nI started a new position as\nResearch Intern\nat\nSony\nin this summer. I’ll be working on developing self-reflective MT systems. Feel free to havea coffee chat if you’re in Tokyo / Japan!\nApr 18, 2025\nNew paper on\nMachine Translation Evaluation\n: “\nRemedy: Learning Machine Translation Evaluation from Human Preferences with Reward Modeling\n”, achieving new SOTA on WMT22-24.\nMar 11, 2025\nWe obtained\nSilver Medal (Ranked 19/950 teams)\non the\nKaggle 2025 WSDM Cup - Multilingual Chatbot Arena\n, a Reward Modeling Competition to predict the human preference of Multilingual LLMs.\nSep 20, 2024\nPaper “\nNeuron Specialization: Leveraging Intrinsic Task Modularity for Multilingual Machine Translation\n” accepted to the\nEMNLP 2024\nMain Conference.\nMay 16, 2024\nPaper “\nHow Far can 100 Samples Go? Unlocking Zero-Shot Translation with Tiny Multi-Parallel Data\n” accepted to the\nACL Findings 2024\nConference.\nNov 07, 2023\nPaper “\nTowards a Better Understanding of Variations in Zero-Shot Neural Machine Translation Performance\n” accepted to the\nEMNLP 2023\nMain Conference.\nOct 08, 2023\nOur system obtained the\nFirst Place\non the\nWMT23 Translation Competition\non the English-Hebrew and Hebrew-English Constraint Tracks.\nSep 21, 2023\nPaper “\nMake Pre-trained Model Reversible: From Parameter to Memory Efficient Fine-Tuning\n” accepted to the\nNeurIPS 2023\nConference.\nAug 25, 2023\nWe obtained the\nGold Medal\non the\nKaggle 2023 Google’s American Sign Language Fingerspelling Recognition\n,\nranked 11/1,315 teams\n.\n\n---\n\nCV | Shaomu Tan\nCV\nGeneral Information\nName\nShaomu Tan\nEmail\ns.tan@uva.nl\nSummary\nA PhD Candidate at University of Amsterdam, working on Deep Learning for Natural Language Processing.\nEducation\n2022-2024\nAmsterdam, NL\nPhD in Artificial Intelligence\nUniversity of Amsterdam\n, Amsterdam, Netherlands\nMultilingual Machine Translation\nDeep Learning for Natural Language Processing\n2020-2022\nUtrecht, NL\nMaster in Artificial Intelligence\nUtrecht University\n, Utrecht, Netherlands\nDutch GPA: 8.0/10.0\nUS Scale: 4.0/4.0\n2016-2020\nShandong, CN\nBachelor in Information System\nShandong University\n, Shandong, China\nPublications\n2024-11\nNeuron Specialization: Leveraging Intrinsic Task Modularity for Multilingual Machine Translation\nEMNLP 2024\nShaomu Tan\n, Di wu, Christof Monz\n2024-05\nHow Far Can 100 Samples Go? Unlocking Overall Zero-Shot Multilingual Translation via Tiny Multi-Parallel Data\nACL Findings 2024\nDi wu,\nShaomu Tan\n, Yan Meng, David Stap, Christof Monz\n2023-10\nTowards a Better Understanding of Variations in Zero-Shot Neural Machine Translation Performance\nEMNLP 2023\nShaomu Tan\n, Christof Monz\n2023-09\nMake Pre-trained Model Reversible: From Parameter to Memory Efficient Fine-Tuning\nNeurIPS 2023\nBaohao Liao,\nShaomu Tan\n, Christof Monz\n2023-10\nUvA-MT's Participation in the WMT23 General Translation Shared Task\nWMT 2023\nDi Wu*,\nShaomu Tan*\n, David Stap, Ali Araabi, Christof Monz\nCompetitions\n2025-03\nWSDM Cup - Multilingual Chatbot Arena\nSilver Medal - Ranked 19/950 teams\nShaomu Tan\n2023-08\nGoogle's American Sign Language Fingerspelling Recognition\nGold Medal - Ranked 11/1,315 teams\nBaohao Liao,\nShaomu Tan\n2023-10\nWMT 23 General Machine Translation Competition\nFirst Place on English-Hebrew Constraint Track\nDi Wu*,\nShaomu Tan*\n, David Stap, Ali Araabi, Christof Monz"
  },
  "deep_homepage::Shengpu Tang::https://shengpu-tang.me/": {
    "homepage": "https://shengpu-tang.me/",
    "all_emails": [
      "shengpu.tang@gmail.com",
      "me@shengpu.tang"
    ],
    "matched_email": null,
    "github": "https://github.com/shengpu-tang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shengpu-tang",
    "homepage_text": "Shengpu Tang\nShengpu Tang\n唐笙普\nAssistant Professor\nCS @ Emory\nPlease check out the\nLab\nsections above for info about my research group:\nTang's AI Lab\n.\nUpdate Spring 2026\n: Students who are interested in working on a research project with me, please read this first:\nReaching Out\n.\nI am a tenure-track assistant professor of computer science at Emory University. My research focuses on developing and applying\nartificial intelligence\nmethods to enhance\ndecision making in healthcare\n, with a particular emphasis on\nreinforcement learning\n. Inspired by specific real-world use cases, my work identifies technical challenges and proposes novel solutions that are broadly relevant to fundamental AI/ML research. My past contributions span various technical areas including reinforcement learning (\nRLC'25 workshops\n,\nNeurIPS'23\n,\nICML'23 workshops\n,\nNeurIPS'22 oral\n,\nNeurIPS'22 workshop\n,\nMLHC'21\n,\nICML'20\n), time-series and sequence data modeling (\nMLHC'19\n), dataset bias (\nHealthAffairs'21\n), as well as translating AI solutions to advance precision medicine (\nJMIR Med Inform'25\n,\nJAMA Network Open'25\n,\nIDWeek'24\n,\nAJS'21\n,\nJAMIA'20\n,\nJCO-CCI'20\n), with some work specifically addressing the COVID-19 pandemic (\nBMJ'22\n,\nAnnalsATS'20\n). I hope to apply my knowledge and expertise to interdisciplinary problems in healthcare and beyond.\nI served as a main organizer for\nMLHC\n2025 and\nML4H\n2022-2023, Senior AC for\nCHIL\n2025, and AC for\nCHIL\n2024. I also regularly review for top-tier ML venues, including NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, AAAI, AISTATS, KDD, MLHC, CHIL, TMLR, TKDD.\nI am a\n\"Triple Wolverine\"\n- having done my bachelor's, master's, and PhD degrees all at\nUniversity of Michigan\n. During my PhD, I was a member of the Machine Learning for Data-Driven Decisions (\nMLD3\n) research group led by Prof.\nJenna Wiens\n, under the\nMichigan AI Lab\n. I also collaborated with Prof.\nMaggie Makar\nat UMich, Prof.\nSonali Parbhoo\nat Imperial, and Prof.\nFinale Doshi-Velez\nat Harvard.\nInterests\nReinforcement Learning\nMachine Learning\nArtificial Intelligence\nHealth & Medicine\nEducation\nPhD in Computer Science & Engineering, 2024\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor\nMSE in Computer Science & Engineering, 2020\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor\nBSE in Computer Science, Minor in Mathematics,\nsumma cum laude\n, 2018\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor\nRecent News\nOct 2025\n: Congratulations to Yingchuan Sun on the acceptance of his\nML4H proceedings paper!\nJun 2025\n: 4 papers accepted at RLC 2025 workshops! Congratulations to students\nSixing\n,\nYingchuan\n,\nRolf and Matthew\n!\nFeb 2025\n: Presented at AAAI-25 New Faculty Highlights, Philadelphia, PA.\n[Recording]\nOct 2024\n: Gave an invited talk and also presented an oral abstract at IDWeek 2024 in Los Angeles, CA.\nSept 2024\n: My PhD dissertation is available online at\nthis link\n!\nAug 2024\n: Gave an invited talk at RLC 2024 ICBINB workshop, Amherst, MA.\nJune 3, 2024\n: 🎉 I defended my PhD thesis! Special thanks to my thesis committee for their valuable feedback and all my reserach collaborators who have spent time with me throughout my PhD! You can find the recordings at the following links:\nthe Preamble\nand\nthe Defense\n.\nDec 2023\n: 🎉 I presented my latest work on\nCounterfactual Annotations and Semi-Offline Evaluation\nat\nNeurIPS 2023\nin New Orleans, LA. I was also given a travel award at NeurIPS 2023!\nDec 2023\n: 🙌 We successfully organized the\nML4H 2023 Symposium\n, co-located with NeurIPS 2023 in New Orleans, LA. Kudos to the entire organizing team!\nJuly 2023\n: I attended\nICML 2023\nin Honolulu, Hawaii, where I presented\nFactored Actions for OPE\nat the Counterfactuals workshop and the Frontiers4LCD workshop. I also participated in the surfing social!\nJune 2023\n: I attended the\nCHIL 2023 Doctoral Symposium\nin Cambridge, MA.\nDec 2022\n: 🏆 I presented our paper on\nFactored Action Spaces for Offline RL in Healthcare\nat\nNeurIPS 2022\nin New Orleans, LA. It was also selected as \"\nOral\n\" and was presented during the NeurIPS 2022 virtual week.\nDec 2022\n: 🎉 I presented my internship project\nTowards Data-Driven Offline Simulations of Online RL\nat the\n3rd Offline RL workshop\nat NeurIPS 2022.\nNov 2022\n: 🙌 We successfully organized the\nML4H 2022 Symposium\n, co-located with NeurIPS 2022 in New Orleans, LA. Kudos to the entire organizing team!\nOct 2022\n: 🥈 I was extremely honored to have been selected as the\nfinalist\nrepresenting the AI lab, and\nplacing second\nin the\n19th annual CSE Graduate Honors Competition\n.\nJul-Aug 2022\n: Finally attended my\nfirst\nin-person\nconferences\nduring PhD,\nICML 2022\nin Baltimore, MD and\nMLHC 2022\nin Durham, NC.\nJuly 2022\n: I won a free registration to\nICML 2022\nas one of the top reviewers!\nMay-Aug 2022\n: I worked with Microsoft's\nAzure Cognitive Services\nteam as a Research Intern.\nFebruary 2022\n: Our paper on\nM-CURES\n, an open-source machine learning model\npredicting deterioration of hospitalized COVID-19 patients\nwas accepted to one of the world's leading medical journals,\nThe BMJ\n!\nDecember 2021\n: Our paper on\nrespecting autonomy & enabling diversity\nfor medical databanks that drive precision health and clinical AI - was published in\nHealth Affairs\n.\nDecember 2021\n: I co-organized a new workshop -\nResearch2Clinics -- Bridging the Gap: From Machine Learning Research to Clinical Practice\n- at\nNeurIPS 2021\n. Thank you to everyone who contributed and attended!\nAugust 2021\n: Invited to give a talk on\nRL with Set-Valued Policy: A Framework for Clinician-in-the-Loop Decision Making\nat the\nPathCheck Global Health Innovators Talk Series\n! You can find the recording of the seminar\nhere\n.\nJuly-August 2021\n: I presented our paper\nPractical Considerations for Offline RL Model Selection\nat both\nMLHC 2021\nand\nRL4RealLife @ ICML 2021\n! Watch the spotlight talk\nhere\n.\nDecember 2020\n: Won a free registration to\nNeurIPS 2020\nas one of the top reviewers! Excited to see everyone there :)\nJuly 2020\n: I presented our paper on\nRL with Set-Valued Policy\nat\nICML 2021\n. Check out the spotlight video\nhere\n.\nMedia Coverage\nJun 16, 2025\n:\nNew AI tool supports best practices to prevent spread of dangerous C. diff infections\n-- Emory News Center\n.\nJun 13, 2025\n:\nAI Tool Shows Promise for Antimicrobial Stewardship, C Diff Prevention in Hospitals\n-- HCPLive\n.\nJun 12, 2025\n:\nClinically deployed AI guidance for preventing C. difficile spread\n-- UMich CSE\n.\nSept 12, 2024\n:\nThese CSE PhD alums have accepted faculty positions\n-- UMich CSE\n.\nNov 10, 2022\n:\n2022 CSE Graduate Student Honors Competition highlights outstanding research\n-- UMich CSE\n.\nFeb 22, 2022\n:\nAI Triage\n-- Digital Health Wire newsletter\n.\nFeb 22, 2022\n:\nHow to train a clinical AI to predict bad health outcomes\n-- STAT Health Tech\n.\nFeb 17, 2022\n:\nOpen-source patient model tops industry standard\n-- University of Michigan News\n.\nDec 7, 2021\n:\nHow bias can creep into medical databanks that drive precision health and clinical AI\n-- Michigan Health Lab\n.\nJun 22, 2021\n:\nA hospital algorithm designed to predict a deadly condition misses most cases\n-- The Verge\n.\nApr 21, 2021\n:\nIn scramble to respond to Covid-19, hospitals turned to models with high risk of bias\n-- MedCityNews\n.\nMar 30, 2021\n:\nFrom collaboration to validation: How a team of engineers, clinicians, programmers, and administrators evaluated one of the most widely implemented predictive models\n-- Precision Health at UM\n.\nJan 22, 2021\n:\nEpic Deterioration Index May Identify High- and Low-Risk COVID-19 Patients\n-- Clinical Advisor\n.\nOct 12, 2020\n:\nNew framework helps streamline EHR data extraction\n-- Healthcare IT News\n.\nMay 12, 2020\n:\nFaster than COVID: a computer model that predicts the disease’s next move\n-- The Mighican Engineer News Center\n.\nApr 15, 2020\n:\nComputer scientists employ AI to help address COVID-19 challenges\n-- Michigan AI Lab\n.\nPublications (PhD-era)\nalso see\npublications of the lab\nOptimizing loop diuretic treatment for mortality reduction in patients with acute dyspnea using a practical offline reinforcement learning pipeline for healthcare: a retrospective single center simulation study\nJMIR Medical Informatics, Sep 2025\nJung Min Lee\n,\nShengpu Tang\n,\nMichael W. Sjoding\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\nGuiding\nClostridioides difficile\nInfection Prevention Efforts in a Hospital Setting With AI\nJAMA Network Open, Jun 2025\nShengpu Tang\n,\nStephanie Shepard\n,\nRebekah Clark\n,\nErkin Ötleş\n,\nChidimma Udegbunam\n,\nJosh Tran\n,\nMelinda Seiler\n,\nJustin Ortwine\n,\nAkbar K. Waljee\n,\nJerod Nagel\n,\nSarah L. Krein\n,\nJacob E. Kurlander\n,\nPaul J. Grant\n,\nJihoon Baang\n,\nAnastasia Wasylyshyn\n,\nKrishna Rao\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\nTargeting\nClostridioides difficile\nInfection Prevention Efforts with Artificial Intelligence\nID\nWeek Oral Abstract, Oct 2024; Published in\nOpen Forum Infectious Diseases\nShengpu Tang\n,\nRebekah Clark\n,\nStephanie Shepard\n,\nErkin Ötleş\n,\nMaxim Garifullin\n,\nMelinda Seiler\n,\nJustin Ortwine\n,\nPatrick Arnold\n,\nJerod Nagel\n,\nJeremy Jared\n,\nSarah L Krein\n,\nJacob E Kurlander\n,\nPaul J Grant\n,\nJihoon Baang\n,\nAnastasia Wasylyshyn\n,\nKrishna Rao\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\nCANDOR: Counterfactual ANnotated DOubly Robust off-policy evaluation\nunder review\nAishwarya Mandyam\n,\nShengpu Tang\n,\nJiayu Yao\n,\nEmma Brunskill\n,\nJenna Wiens\n,\nBarbara E Engelhardt\narXiv\nCounterfactual-Augmented Importance Sampling for Semi-Offline Policy Evaluation\nNeurIPS 2023\nShengpu Tang\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\ncode\nposter\nvideo\nwebsite\narXiv\nLeveraging Factored Action Spaces for Off-Policy Evaluation\nFrontiers4LCD @ ICML 2023, Counterfactuals @ ICML 2023\nAaman Peter Rebello\n,\nShengpu Tang\n,\nJenna Wiens\n,\nSonali Parbho